      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>NO DEAD CHICKS.</p>
        <p>By using only ARMITAGES DRY CHICK FOOD. It will pay you. Chicks rearsd on this Food alone weigh 20oz. at 5 weeks, commence to lay at 5 mouths. An 8d bag (by post is.) rears 12 Chicks till 3 weeks old. Bags 4d, Bcl., is. 4d., 2s. 6d., etc. Makers :</p>
        <p>ARMITAGE BROS. LTD.,</p>
        <p>--POULTRY FOOD SPECIALISTS, NOTTINGHAM,</p>
        <p>AGENTS:</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON, BRIDGE ST. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, eze.</p>
        <p>MAIIi: McDOUCALL'S PATENT SELF RAISING FLOUR Is UNRIVALLED. The Examiner, Saturday, May 31, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>NOW EGGS ARE PLENTIFUL.</head>
        <p>NOW EGGS ARE PLENTIFUL.</p>
        <p>Spring Cleaning and Whitewashing In the Poultry Yards.</p>
        <p>PROFITABLE BROODY HENS. ,</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP.]</p>
        <p>It is high time that the poultry yard underwent a process spring cleaning. Advantage of the good weather now prevailing should be taken, and all coops, nest boxes, houses and scratching shsis be limewashed. It is far easier to prevent insect pests than it is to get rid of them. The favourite harbour- ing place for these is the perch sockets, nest boxes and any ledges and crevices fin the house. The perches should be taken out, the ends dirped in paraffin and the sockets well soaked with it also. All refuse from the nest and sitting boxes should be burned. When mixing the limewash let the lime be new and whenever possible put it on the boaris whilst hot, be careful of your eyes and never mix ,new lime- wash with the hands. All limewashing should be -done in the early part of the day so as to allow the sleeping quarters to get thoroughly dry before the fowls go to roost lit is not a good thing for the stock to occupy the roost wlailst the houee is damp. Therefore, if done early, leaving all windows and shutters open, it allows plenty of time to get thoroughly dry. This also is the best time to tar the roofs and sides of the fowl houses. If applied whilst hot it runs well in the crevices and soon,tiries. PRESERVING EGGS.</p>
        <p>Eggs are now more plentiful and it is a good plan to preserve a number for winter use. here are many methods of preservation, but, after many experiments, I think waterglass the best medium of all. It entails very little trouble in preparation. All you require is to follow the directions given by the themist and use always earthenware vessels. If you intend preserving eggs in large numbers, then,you may, use clean casks; but metal buckets should never be used. Waterglass can be obtained at any chemist's shop and at most grocery establishments. It is very cheap and a small tin will be sufficient to preserve quite a large number. There is one important point to observe in preserving eggs. Always let the .eggs be as 'fresh as possible when put down, and never more than three days old. With waterglass as the preservative, writes " Economist " in the "Feathered 'World," "cracked or broken eggs have no effect on the keeping of the others, and no anxiety need be felt on this account. The shells are .also less brittle than by using quicklime, etc., and therefore less subject to breakages_ Few or many eggs can be preserved just as they are spared, and taken out as wanted for con- sumption. The great advantage of using waterglass is that the eggs are so easily cleansed when they are taken out of the preservative, and there is no lime to get on one's clothe,.; they•keep longer than by any other known process." Even sorit is always advisable to use only those eggs with the strongest shells dis- carding at all times any that are cracked.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>BROODY IfENS.</head>
        <p>BROODY IfENS.</p>
        <p>It is not a difficult matter to obtain broody hens now. As a matter of fact there are often too many as the weather get warmer. There is, however, a very profitable way of .disposing of .these. As the old hens become broody give •them a batch of eggs to sit. You will have no dificulty in disposing of hen and chicks and at very remunerative prices. It is a good plan to set two or more hens at the same time. This enables you to make up the broods that have not hatched out a good percentage. What is atiair and profitable price to charge? I have ofien been -asked this question. For cross-bred chicks sixpence per head and for pure- bred chicks, ninepence. The price of the hen is determined always by her breed and pedigree. It is not advisable to use valuable hens for this purpose, as it only creates, to a certain extent, a false value. When purchasing a hen and chicks, the buyer's only desire, as a rule, is to have a good 3:rood of chicks; conse- quently the cheaper you can make the hen, the more likely you are to, effect a ready sale. Therefore, if you charge 2s_ Cel. for the hen, and sixpence and nine- pence respectively for a dazen chicks, you are able to sell the broods at Ss. 6d. in the first instance and lie. E;d. in the second instanee. This is a very fair and profitable rata to all concerned. To the back- yarder who is making a start it is a cheap method of doing so; and to the poultry keeper on a larger scale who is making the best use of the broody hens, it is also a profitable method. If the hen and chicks are being dispatched by rail, special methods of packing should be observed. A two-compartment bst should be used, or else the chicks will get crushed and trampled in the journey. If closely-made boxes are used, a few air boles should be made round the sides and at the top. The chicken compartment needespecial construction. Inside this place a sheet of cardboard in a vertical fashion so that the chicks are prevented from occupying the corners of the box and so getting starved. The circular of cardboard should be made small enough to allow only for the easy access of a dozen chicks. In this way they will huddle together and so keep each other warm until they reach their journey's end, when they will be able to get back to the broody hen. On arrival at their destination a good meal of warm bread and milk ahould be given; this is a rare pick-me-up and acts as a good stimulant in reviving any that are seedy as the result of a long railway journey. SHADING THE YOUNGSTERS.</p>
        <p>If your accommodation in no way provides the young chickens with natural shelter from the sun, some shelter should be provided. A good plan is to place a wooden hurdle cn four stakes and cover with wheat straw or a piece of canvas. Old sacks out open are a useful substitute. Under this shelter you should bury a few handfuls of canary seed or dry chick food. This will encourage the chickens to frequent the place and they would find it a happy retreat from the hot rays of the sun. The drinking vessels should also be aitnated in a sheltered corner of the run, and as often as possible these friaould be renewed with clean water. Place a few handfuls of powdered sulphur in the dust bath. This will help to cleanse the bodies and also tend to keep down body lice. All these little hints if carefully noted and carried into effect help to make the work of chicken rearing pleasing and profitable. THE PROPOSED TRIP TO HASLINGTON HALL. Some time ago, readers will remember, I suggested a trip to Haslington Hall Crewe, to visit the experi- mental farm now under the supervision of Mr. F. G. Paynter. Two readers have expressed their- willing- ness to go. I should be glad if others who would care to oin us would express their willingness by just send Eng a postcard to " Redcap " c/o "Examiner" Office, Warrington. It would be necessary for me to make certain arrangements; therefore, please let me know as early as possible. There is a treat in store for readers of this co:num at Haslington</p>
        <p>aOld ; CHICALA • A DRY FEED FOR CHICKENS AGENTS CARTER &amp; SON,</p>
        <p>BRIDGE ST. WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>BRITISH RAILWAY ACCIDENTS.</head>
        <p>BRITISH RAILWAY ACCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>Too Frequent Last Year to be Creditable.</p>
        <p>Returns ha7e:ust been issued of accidents on British railways during 1912. From these it appears that the fatal accidents to passengers in trains resulted in 20 deaths, while there were also 683 injuries. These figures are higher than the records for 1911, in which there were 141 passengers killed and 468 injured.. The total number of passengers killed on the railways as a result of the movement of trains and vehicles was 110, and 2,829 were injured. Many of these accidents, of course, are due to no fault in railway working, but to the carelessness of passengers themselves, such as jumping on and off trains in motion, falling out of carriages and between carriages and platforms, &amp;c. The fatal accidents to employes showed a decrease in 1912 on tke figcres of 1911. There were 313 eoaployes killed last year, compared with 390 in the year before. The number of injured increased, however, fr 05,311 in 1911 to 5,36 f in 1912. The casualties resulting 'from accidents to trains as well as those from other accidents connected with other movements of vehicles both showed an increase on the previous year. The grand total of passeagers, employes, and others killed on the railways was 1,011, or 59 less than in 1911, while the injured amounted to 8,700 persons, or 35C more than in 1911. These totals, of course, include trespassers, suicides, Scc., for which the railw:-...ys are in no way responsible. Out of 867 "other persons" killed and ;injured, no less than 344 were trespassers and 241 suicides. Very often when these accident returns are noticed, no trouble is taken to discriminate between the classes for which the railways may be and should not be held responsible, and the returns naturally appear unduly high. The train actidents, however, last year were above the average, and more frequent than we would like to see for the credit of our railway system.—" Engineering."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WHAT WARRINGTON PEOPLE SEEK.</head>
        <p>WHAT WARRINGTON PEOPLE SEEK.</p>
        <p>What the anxious sufferer from kidney and blaider trouble seeks is not temporary relief—though that would be welcome enough; what is sought for is a last- ing cure. Mrs. Siddall, of Warrington, tells us here how she has found lasting good from Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, after long suffering. "It was after my last confinement that I began to suffer with kidney and bladder troubles," said Mrs. H. Siddall, of 17, Hume Street, Dalton Bank, Warrington, nearly six years aco. "The worst symptoms of the complaint were the sharp, stabbing pains which attacked me right across the small of my back. If I stooped, or had been sitting down, it was difficult for me to straighten trry back. "The action of the bladder was irregular, and the kidney excretions were unnatural. I was altogether out of sorts,and suffered from headaches and dizzineso</p>
        <p>" But I found an excellent remedy for these troubles in Doan's Backache Kidney Pills. They drove away all my aches and pains, and rectified the bladder disorder. Since taking Doan's pills I have felt like a new woman. The ,medicine deserve all praise, and I hope these few lines will induce other sufferers to try it. (Signed) (Mrs.) H. &amp;Drum..." NEARLY SIX YEARS LATER, Mrs. Siddall said :—" My cure has been permanent, I am glad to say. lam always pleased to recommend Doan's pills." Price f2s. 9d. a box, 6 boxes 13s. 9d. •, of all dealers, or from Foster-McClellan, Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford- street, London, W. Don't ask for backache and kidney pills —ask distinctly for Loan's Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mrs. Siddall had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>HALL MARK.</head>
        <p>THE HALL MARK.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the many hard things that are being said about doctors just now, there is not the least doubt that an expression of opinion by a medical man in connection with any remedy carries great weight with the public. And rightly so. for doctors are able, by reason of their peculiar knowledge and experience, to distinguish between those preparations that fulfil the claims that are made for them and those that do ndt. In this matter the proprietors of "Marths," the well-known remedy for kidney ailmezts, must con- sider themselves fortunate, for their preparation is used extensively by medical men, both in private and in hospital practice. As is well known rheumatism, gout, sciatica, lumbago, neuritis, and kindred ailments are caused by accumulation a of uric acid in the joints and muscles. Before any relief can be had this uric acid must be expelled, and it is the function of " Marthu "to do this. " Marthu," is to be had from any chemist at 2s, 9d., or direct from the makers, the Marthu Company Aberystwyth.</p>
        <p>MORE ULSTER "LOYALTY."</p>
        <p>"WILL BE ON THE SIDE OF GERMANY."</p>
        <p>Another threat of rebellion against the Crown has followed Sir Edward Carson's attempt at Belfast last week to dictate to the King. "I shall no longer sing 'God Save the King,' "said Mr. Chambers, K.C., M.P. for South Belfast, at a meeting in that city on Friday night, the reference being to the time when the Home Rule Bill is passed into law. He would, he added, say instead, "England, I will laugh at your calamity, I will mock when your fear cometh." Mr. Chambers asked his audience what side they would take "when the King will have told us that he values cur allegiance no longer," and the answer promptly came, "Germany."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>frtters to tht (Ettiter</head>
        <p>frtters to tht (Ettiter</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. CUE Enrron of the " Examiner " invites correspond- ence on all subjects of public interest. LETTERS should be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information, not abuse. CommusicAnoss received early in the week—except urgent matters—can always be given the best attention.</p>
        <p>VlvisEcTros.—We have received a letter from a correspondent on the above subject,which we cannot use. We welcome full discussion of all subjects in the " Examiner" but our conditions,stated above, are that the object of letters sent to us must be to impart or to seek imformation, not aluse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>VIVISECTIONISTS AND ANTIVIVISECTIONISTS.</head>
        <p>VIVISECTIONISTS AND ANTI- VIVISECTIONISTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,— I was glad to see that Miss Kidd in your last issue corroborated my statement about the change- able opinions of the late Mr. Lawson Tait, and I have no objections to her claiming him as a supporter in his later years, when he was apparently in his dotage. I_xvas interested to learn that although Dr. George</p>
        <p>Wilson held anti-vivisection opinions vet he was not an anti-vivisectionist. What then constitutes an anti- vivisectionist? Does the test consist in giving a sub- scription to some anti-vivisection society ? It I'B encouraging to know that Dr. George Wilson keeps his money for better uses. What with the chameleon-like Mr. Tait, the non- subscribing Dr. Wilson, and last, but by no means least, our worthy Mayor, who does not believe in the abolition of vivisection, the medical support of anti- vivisection grows gradually smaller and beautifully less.</p>
        <p>J. S. MANSON. Winmarleigh Street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>DRINKING FOUNTAINS.</head>
        <p>DRINKING FOUNTAINS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your admirable illustrate article on the above subject and your hint, so courteously conveyed, wdl, I hope, draw attention to a " real want." The average "traveller " on the road will not expect to find Warrington a town 'flowing with milk and honey, but he has a right to expect to find a spot with a flow of drinking water where he can quench his thirst. There are few who use the local main roads more than I do, and if those in authority saw 'how often drivers pull up at the troughs in Winwick and Penketh to refresh both man and beast, your suggestion that troughs, as well as extra fountains, be erected would speedily be adopted. If public gifts are " scrappel " in the manner that the fountains have been, is it surprising that Warring- ton receives so few ? The clock that was for years at Market Gate was also a gift. Is that scrapped, toe: I WONDER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Warringtou. MR. PILLING AND THE GRAPPENHALL SEWERAGE SCHEME.</head>
        <p>Warringtou. MR. PILLING AND THE GRAPPENHALL SEWERAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I trespass a little on your space to refer to your report of the Runcorn Rural District Council meeting in last Saturday's issue, especially in regard to Mr. Pilling's refusal to second the resolution pro- posed by Mr. Kayo? Idoas a ratepayer in Latchford Without, and as one who attended the meeting for the election of the Parish Council, at which it was distinctly stated that whilst it was quite understood that Mr. Pilling did not ‹.gree with the generally expressed opinion of the ratepayers of the Parish on this question of the Sewage Scheme, he nevertheless gave an undertaking to carry out the wishes of the ratepayers by supporting any scheme for a reduction in its cost, and on that uniertaking a further nomina- tion in opposition to him as District Councillor was withdrawn.</p>
        <p>He may quibble and say that neither this nor that resolution was mentioned; but be cannot deny that such a resolution as that proposed by Mr. Kaye came well within the scope of the understanding conveyed to the meeting I have referred to, and why his seconding of the motion would have placed him "in a peculiar position" I utterly fail to see, when he would merely have been doing what he was, above everything else, sent there for.</p>
        <p>I certainly think that -come explanation is due from him, and I hope the members of the Parish Council will see that it is forthcoming at their next meeting. A. 4HORNBY RATHBONE. "Lyndon." Thel wall New-road, Latchford Without.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>CO-OPERATION AND POVERTY.</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATION AND POVERTY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—According to Labor Omnia Vin cit," my last letter is full of "glazing inconsistencies ; but the only " instance " he gives will, I think, make it plain to most of your readers that the truth is rather that" Labor Omnia Vinct " is unable to distinguish between a scheme that ,would rearrange a whole social system at one stroke and a scheme that would deal with one small portion of the industrial organisa- tion within the social system, allowing this to gradually revolutionise society in ways that cannot be foreseen and with results that no Utopianist could possibl7 have contemplated. All reform does, and must, tali() place along the lines of the latter. Unless "Labor Omnia Vincit " can see this difference I am afraid argument is useless. I really thought, when your correspondent stated that the Socialist proposal was " scientific " and my proposal "unscientific," that, at any rate, the various proposals would be considered in connection with economic laws, but on this point he is altogether silent. He does not attempt to show where my own proposal transgresses any economic law, although I asked him several definite questions with a view to elicit, if possible, this information. He may say that this or that proposal is " scientific " or "unscientific," but unless he can state the laws with which they comply or otherwise he is simply dealing in empty phrases. We might go on for months in the style of his last letter without getting any nearer a solution. I cannot do this, and this must be my last. I would only remind him that I took up the challenge he himself threw down. 29th May, 1913.</p>
        <p>JOHN A. J. MORTON.</p>
        <p>THE BANK HOLIDAY MAKER. DEATH OF LORD AVEBURY.</p>
        <p>Lord Avebury—in earlier years known as Sir John Lubbock died on Wednesday morning, in his 80th year, at Kingsgate Castle, near Broadstairs. To public recognition Lord Avebury had titles manifold, but the chief of them may be neatly summed up in three B's—Bank Holidays, Bees, and Books. It ,can never be forgotten that mainly ,by the force of his persistent agitation the Bank Holidays Act of 1881 was passed, which secures to the workers of this Icountry a welcome respite at Christmas, Easter, Whitsuntide, and on the first Monday of August. His books on "Bees, Ants, and Wasps" has not only high scientific value, but is delightful reading for the unlearned ; and his list of the hundred beat books has stood against all the fierce competition to which it has been exposed. A banker by profession, Lord Avebury rendered great service to, and obtained high distinc- tion for, that branch of commerce, and on all financial questions be was a great authority. For the last 30 years of the nineteenth century he sat in the House of Commons, first as a Liberal, and after the Home Rule split as a Liberal Unionist.</p>
        <p>COLONEL WALKER AND CIVIL WAR. "NOTHING WOULD DO MORE GOOD."</p>
        <p>The yearly meeting of the Widnes Division Con- servative Association was held on Monday night in the Conservative Club. Colonel Hall Walker, M.P., in the course of a speech, said the Insurance Act would have to be ruthlessly amended. Great misuse had been made of this Act, and he felt sure that, when dissected, millions of money, it would be found, were wasted on people who did not deserve any benefits at all. He (the Colonel) liked to see the train keep the rails. It was possible to travel fast and they could pander too much to a people. There was nothing that would do England more good than a real civil war. It would be a horrible thing, and God forbid that it should take place, but they had to remember that it was threatened. They had a man—a real man—Sir Edward Carson. Be was not a party leader, bizt a patriot, who said in a loud voice, "My life is in your hands, but you shnl not treat Ulster like dogs." Let the Radical party try on Home Rule, and civil war was a certainty. "We have the men ready to fight," said the Colonel, "and, if ever it does take place, what I have said will come true."</p>
      </div>
