      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>HORSE PARADE.</head>
        <p>HORSE PARADE.</p>
        <p>A Notable Array of Heavy Horses.</p>
        <p>OVER SEVENTY FIRST PRIZES</p>
        <p>The thousands of townspeople who watched War- rington's thirteenth annual horse parade on Saturday must have felt proud of the splendid array of horse- flesh which our local manufacturers and tradesmen had entered for competition. There could be no ques- tioning the value of the work of the Parade Committee, as reflected in the long procession of smartly-groomed, and well-kept animals which did credit alike to their owners and drivers. Beginning on very modest lines, the promoters of the parade set before themselves the woi thy ideal of encouraging drivers to take a humane interest in their charges, and they have succeeded to the extent of their most sanguine hopes. Although the entries on Saturday, which numbered 120, were not quite as high as those of last year, the quality was distinctly superior to anything previously attained. The classes ior heavy draught horses made a particu- larly fine show, which created the greatest impression on the onlookers, but the spick-and-span turnouts of the tradesmen shone equally ontheir distinctive merits.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>IDEAL CONDITIONS.</head>
        <p>IDEAL CONDITIONS.</p>
        <p>Gloricus sunshine favoured the parade acd a large •company of ladies and gentlemen gathered on the Town Hall steps to witness the judging. This work, which was rendered difficult yet plrasurable by the keen competition, was carried out by the following; Messrs. W. D. Bullock, Altrincham ; Vincent Davies, Stockton Heath, R. Fleming, -London ; C. R. Longs, Knutsford ; R. B. Neilson, Liverpool; T. F. Speakman, Kuutsford ; and Jas. Tinsley, Banbury. The horses and vehicles were subjected to a very thorough examination, the object being to ensure that the points of merit were the result of continuous attention on the right lines and not of any strenuous, spasmodic effort on the approach of show day. For instance, it was emphasised that "the horse coat must be clean as the result of regular grooming, and not for the day only." Stress was also laid upon the comfortable fit of the harness and all chains and straps were to be bright and clean. The general excellence of the exhibits was evidenced by the fact that the judges awarded no fewer than seventy-eight first prizes—and this, notwithstanding the fact that a more rigorous standard was exacted. A departure was made in the granting of diplomas. Hitherto these had always accompanied the first prizes, but this year they were limited, at the discretion of the judges, to those premier winners which were considered to be of outstanding superiority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>JUDGE'S OPINION.</head>
        <p>A JUDGE'S OPINION.</p>
        <p>In conversation with an" Examiner" representative, one of the judges declared that the Warrington horse parade compared more than favourably with an similar undertaking at which he had officiated in other towns. He expressed special admiration for the heavy horses and also commented upon the splendid fit of practically all the collars, showing that Warring- ton must have a first-class maker of harness.</p>
        <p>The judging over, the drivers received their cards of merit at the hands of the Mayoress (Mrs. Arnold Nield). The horses and turnouts then formed in procession in Crosfield-street and traversed the main streets of the town, which were well lined with spectators. The distinctly com- mercial and industrial character of the parade seemed to preclude anything in the way of novelty or originality, but most of the horses were decorated in the approved May Day style. The procession was headed by the Borough Band and a touch of variety ails added by the inclusion of the three local fire brigades. One enterprising coal merchants gave a lurry representation of the miner's calling, but this was practically the only feature out of the ordinary. Detachments of the Boy Scouts and Church Lads' Brigade made a collection along the line of route, in aid of the Warrington Infirmary and District Nursing Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WORKERS.</head>
        <p>THE WORKERS.</p>
        <p>The Parade Committee has an excellent set of officials who take a keen interest in its affairs. The complete list is as follows : Presi- dent, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart. ; vice-presidents, the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph), Sir J. Beecham, Col. J. A. Edelsten, Col. J. Reid Walker, Major G. R. Crosfield Messrs H. R. Armitage, W. Bainbridge, John Coxon, H. P. Densham, Wm. Fletcher, F. A. Frost, George Grandage, Henry Greenall, Robert Hutton, E. Isherwoed, T. H. Lyon, Percy Mortimer, Henry Roberts, W. Peter Rylands and J. Fulton- Smith. Committee: Messrs. W. Bainbridge; H. Ferguson, junr., W. Fletcher, E. Ridgway, W. P. Rylands, Geo. Venn, H. Gandy and S. Picton ; hon. treasurer, Mr. J. Fulton-Smith; hon. secretary, Mr. A. H. Clive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>The following is the list of awards : CORPORATION.-1 and diploma, William Watson, Joseph Gibbons, Arthur Holder; 1, :John Morgan, James Newton, Joseph Miller, Thomas Goulding, John Whittaker, C: W. Hankinson, A. E. Ashton, Abraham Astall. William laylor, C. Pendlebary ; 2, Matthew Knowles, Benjamin Gibbons, John Duckworth. GREEN ALL, WHITLEY AND CO., LTD.-1 and diploma, James George, Sam Harmer; 1, Richard Harmer, Jack Harmer; 2, Eph. Quarmby, Charles Holmes. SLATE CO., LTD.-1 and diploma Arthur Walker, Samuel Allen ; 1, George Thomason, Edward Dack, Alfred Ovenstone, James Mooney ; 2, Albert Green. MESSRS. PETER WALKER AND SON, LTD.-1 and diploma, J. Mason, J. Cowburn, J. Malin, W. Johnson, J. Dean, S. Mold, J. Houghton, C. Jennings; 1, J. Scriven, G. Smith, J. Savage, W. Palmer, R. Ford, A. Houghton, T. Whitfield, F. Buckley, J. Frangleton, J. Condliffe, T. Harmer, M. Gilfedder, D. Courts; 2, W. Keys.</p>
        <p>HEAVY MISCELLANEOUS.-1 and diploma. Joseph Clarke, Wilfred Holmes and J. E. Wakefield (Carter and Son, Ltd.), Charles Edwards (Jack Edwards), %sillliam Ryding (owner) T. Spencer, Sam fltrrison, and James Barlow (E. Isherwood), John Jackson (owner), and Robert Barker (Buckley and Co.); 1, James Donaldson and Thomas Davidson (Carter and Son, Ltd.), Thomas Regan (El. Gandy and Co.), John Dutton (John Cooper), Edward Mackley, and Richard Andrews (E. Isherwood) W. H. Pucili (Fairclough and Co.), F. Collier and Thomas Roberts (Mortimer and Co.), T. Gibbons, J. Wilde and H. Whitley (W. and J. Richardson), Herbert Dutton (W. H. Bailey), 5Vm. Holies (Buckley and Co.) ; 2 Albert Buckley (Fairclough and Co.), H. Collier and J Green (Mortimer and Co.), Thos. Massey (I'. M. West), Wm. Barnes, Arthur Parker, (exors. James Newton), Wm. Salt, Wm. Brandon and Thos. Regan (H. Gandy and Co.), J. Roberts (W. and J. Bichardson), Albert Atkinson (Buckley and Co.), Edward Hanks, Walter Bratt (John Chorley) ; 3, Sam. Fiddler (Pendlebury and Co.), Samuel Johnson (Chas. Starkey), and Thos. Blower (Greenall's Distillery). LIGHT HORSE NISCELLANEOUS.-1 and diploma, William Bridge (Strange and Son) ; 1, H. Bridge, jun. (H. Bridge and Sons), H. Leslie (Leslie and Sons), Thomas Pilling (John Pilling), Harry Fox (Bennett), Harold Ackroyd (Walter Sutton), Albert Wheeler, '(Josh. Bradford), James Starkey (Strange and Sons), Edward Stirrup (owner) ; 2, John Nlannion ( l'hornas Roberts), Ernest Jones (William Furnival), Wilfred Haigh (Alf Cummins), William Pilling (John Pilling), Herbert Whalley and Harry Clare (Bennett), Peter Lewis (Lewis Bros.) ; 3, Fred Jones (I'. D. Jones), H. Straw (C. Straw), Herbert Hart and John Jones (Bennett), William Kirkham (James Toole).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>FARMER AS A BUSINESS MAN.</head>
        <p>THE FARMER AS A BUSINESS MAN.</p>
        <p>The farmer is entitled to be ranked as a specialist, but his claim to the designation has its limitations. Mr. B. B. Greig, of the Scottish Board of Agriculture, at a meeting of the Scottish Agricultural Organisation Society, drew an interesting distinction between specialising in production and in trading. He agreed that the farmer was a specialist in the former sense. but not in the latter, and implied that an important point would be gained for the co-operative movement, and also to the advantage cf the farmer himself, if he could be made to realise that he was less competent as a trader than as a producer. On hi-, own farm the occupier was master of the situation, and presumably, with the aid of his local experience, could make his land yield more than anyone else not so well versed in its peculiar characteristics would take out of it. When lie went iuto the market, however, he was not a specialist, for even if he possessed au average knowledge of business affairs, and was naturally shrewd and far-seeing. he was confronted with others who were much more practised in that particular line of business. Mr. Greig argued 1 that if the average agriculturist could be made to realise that, while he was a specialist as a producer lie was necessarily less competent as a trader than those constantly engaged in that sphere of activity, a strong case would be made out for the extension of the co-operative principle, since by working together the farmers could assure themselves of the advantages that were beyond their reach 80 long as they acted individually. The farmer will not feel complimented by being told that he is not a specialist in the marketing of stock and produce, or in the buying of feeding-stuffs and manures, but the assertion is broadly true, and is no reflection upon his intelligence and business acumen, since it is limited opportunity that places him at a disadvantage ,vith his trading opponent, who is not only constantly in touch with the markets, but who, having no other business in hand, is able to concentrate his mind upon that which to the farmer must be second to the ur.inage- meat of his holding.—" Field."</p>
        <p>ELiciou</p>
        <p>The Religion of the Empire.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. S. Townroe on the Religious Aspect of Empire Day.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. S. Townroe spoke on the religious aspect of 1 Empire Day to the members of St. Paul's Men's Bible , Class on Sunday afternoon. He said the mere fact of a man in Earl Grey's position regarding the Empire as an instrument for forwarding the aims of Christianity illustrated the difference between the British conception of Imperial sway and that of former empires. The Roman "imposed peace," but it was the peace of servitude. We imposed peace, and it became the self- government of South Africa. The New Zealander absorbed the Maori race, but gave the old inhabitants an honon-ed place in the Dominion, the position of Native Minister having been tilled by a Maori for over twenty years. Under Rome the Maori would have become a slave. The provincial of Roman Egypt existed only for two duties—to obey his matters and to pay tribute. How different under our government. When the British race took the Soudan in charge in 1898, war, famine, and disease had reduced the population of the Soudan from eight and a half millions to less than two millions, in an area of a million square miles. Under British control the population was now over three millions, served by 1,500 miles of railway, 5,000 miles of telegraph, and a Gordon Memorial College at Khartoum. In view of such accomplishment, could not it be truly said that our conception of exercising lordship was carried out in the religious spirit of "1 am among you as be that serveth " ? _ _</p>
        <p>But the time had come when we must answer the question originally asked St. Paul" Quo vadis ? " We bad put our hands to the plough of Empire, and could not turn back, but when we regarded the greatness and difficulty of our Imperial task, we might well wonder whither we were leading. He asked them to consider the immense problem of India, containing people who speak 147 different vernaculars, and were split up into an infinite number of sects and races. How were we to fulfil our rightful duties in a country so divided in race, language and religion ? Surely members of the Church of England had sufficient imagination and zeal to lift up their eyes from parochialism to the hills of such Imperial needs and responsibilities. To-day there were 268 dioceses belonging to the Anglican communion throughout the Empire and beyond. Two great Anglican missionary societies were letting down their nets in the deep of pagan Janda, and were working side by side with the representatives of the Christian bodies in the mission field in spiritual co-operation. Their work at home was threefold. First, they must do all in their power to assist foreign missions, and in Warrington they could assist next autumn's great missionary exhibition. Secondly, they must try to keep the soul of the mother-country alive and unhurt by the blind forces of materialism. Thirdly, on Empire Sunday, they could pray that modern Imperialism might be given that spiritual impulse which would save it from degenerating into mere vulgarity and jingoism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Holy Trinity Church Curacy.</head>
        <p>Holy Trinity Church Curacy.</p>
        <p>Tbe Rev. Evan Llunworth Jones Roberts.B.A., curate, of Bistre in the diocese nf St. Asaph, has been appointed the curacy of Holy Trinity Church, War- rington, and will take up his auties early in August. Mr. Roberts was ordained deacon in 1910, when he entered upon his present curacy at Bistre. He took priest's orders in the following year. Mr. Roberta succeeds the Rev. J. Lynn Parry at Holy Trinity Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>" Right Thoughts and Words."</head>
        <p>" Right Thoughts and Words."</p>
        <p>There was only a moderate attendance at the meeting of Wycliffe P.S.A. Society on Sunday, presided over by the Rev. T. T. James. Mr. Joseph Owen spoke on " Right thoughts and words." They must be careful not to indulge in vain imaginations and evil thoughts. Let their words be few and well chosen. They should keep a strict guard over their lips and tongue and main- tain a good temper, so that their thoughts and words might be helpful to others. Mr. Yearsley read the lesson. Mr. Himsworth led the prayer. Four new members were welcomed. The Secretary thanked all the members who had contributed money or Roods to the sale of word on behalf of the Elmwood-avenue new school chapel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Tributes to the Late Rev. H. R. Wild,</head>
        <p>Tributes to the Late Rev. H. R. Wild,</p>
        <p>The services at St. Luke's Church, on Sunday, were of a memorial character, and references were made to the sudden death of the Rev. H. R. Wild, for twenty years curate-in-charge of the church. The pulpit was draped in black. Special hymns were sung and the organist played the Dead March from " Saul." Preaching at the morning service, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton said they were all mourners, distressed by the recollection that one who was with them, ministering in that church the previous Sunday, had been in the providence of God suddenly taken away from their midst in a manner that shocked and saddened the hearts of his many friends and acquaintances. The expressions of mourning visible throughout the district on the day of the funeral, and the expressions and respect that were very widespread, would be n very gratifying reflection to his friends and the members of his family, who had all been prostrated by grief of no ordinary character. It fell to his lot to perform the last solemn office of committing his mortal remains to the tomb on Friday, and to witness the expressions of sorrow amidst which it w+s performed. It also fell to his lot to learn the keen appreciation of the members of his family at the demonstrations of respect which followed him from his old parish, and especially from the district of St. Luke's Church, to his last resting-place. It was natural, and it should be so, that one who had served amongst them for the past 20 years,should have won those tokens of affection from his former helpers and associates. He</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>"A GOOD LIVING ? "</head>
        <p>"A GOOD LIVING ? "</p>
        <p>BOYS AND TEACHING PROFESSION.</p>
        <p>Mt§</p>
        <p>S</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Lancashire Education Committee, in Preston, on Monday, Mr. J. J. Cockshott said that 2,700 boys and girls were seeking the assistance of the committee in the attainment of secondary education. He therefore desired to ask the Higher Education Sub-Committee if they did not consider this afforded a field from which they might select those desirous of entering the teach- ing profession, and whether some steps should not be taken to ascertain their suitability for the teaching profession. Archdeacon Fletcher thought it would be a very simple thing for masters of secondary schools from time to time to put before their pupils the advantages of taking up teaching as a means of livlihood, and if it were pointed out to them there was room for a consider- able number to come in, and that there was good living in it, it would be a wise thing. Mr. G. Sharpies (Higher Broughton) said they had not been able to satisfy the boys there was a good living in the teaching profession. (Hear, hear.) With regard to girls, they were more successful ; but he should be failing in his duty as a teachers' representa- tive if he did not point out that there was not a good living in the teaching profession for boys. The maxi- mum in the county area was £l4O, and they had teachers at the present time with familie; who could not make ends meet, because this was nut a living wage for a mairied man thirty-three or thirty-four years of age. He protested against the com- mittee giving authoritative sanction to a statement that the teaching profession was a good living for boys. The expense and cost of training was such that it , practically shut out any boys from the working-classes coming in. The Rev. W. H. Howlett (Bury Grammar School) thought the committee might issue a little pamphlet setting forth what was required, what the advantages were, and what salary a boy might expect to earn. Sir Henry Ifibbert, M.P., said the remarks would receive the careful attention of the Higher Education Su b-committee.</p>
        <p>e-SOCIAL</p>
        <p>deeply appreciated the course pursued by the nieZbiesr; of that congregation, especially of the wardens, "...hay and members of the Church Council in doing What —o—d had done, and for the way they had met that sad a unexpected occurrence. The Rev. J. HG. Hol I ingsvorth,vicar of St. Barc.s,ibtsb9; was the preacher at the evening service. He se"' told news of the death of one who had led their praYeredflas praises for so many years came to them as a tretnen 'r shock. There were always some who never liked tb,elsd parish clergyman, but most in that congregation held theirs in the highest possible respect. A4.tiel death the voice of criticism must be silent, or d erred against Christian charity. Mr. Wild bad ha 0- real liking for the church and people, and had been tent to remain in Warrington for 90 years. The Altbe that he was Lever eager for preferment shawe"oe right spirit. He was one who played the game as be,' we preacher) knew from personal experience. He had su,°.re much consideration for him, and in the Paradise wu'v, he now was it would be added happiness to bi,ch know that the young men and women of the cu" were trying to live as he had taught them to lire' The Workingman's Awakening. nt. Rev. H. Ogden and Modern Social Disconte- The Sunday school anniversary was held at Golbornor: street on Sunday, when the preacher morning and evbed iug was the Rev. H. Ogden. In the morning be proc from Exodus ii. 9, "Take this child awaY " nurse it for me and I will give thee thy wfig-' sysg He said that every penny we spent upon education 0 a sound investment. It would pay good interest W, dreadnoughts had been dropped and all that renl9`,.,°o were in museums. There was a changed outlook t Irtii !En human life. Fifty years ago a youth had to work 4.. 6 a.m. to 8 p.m. with a good long Saturday. His„b.r.etr-sa fast was oaten porridge, his dinner was ditto. Lii imitated his breakfast. Wheaten bread was itulacs9^oio His Sunday clothing was a change of corduroy'. ,soar pleasures, well, watching the leistised few shcw bIIJje it was done. All that had been challenged. Now a w°r the man claimed a place in the sun. What wonder tbectiat pendulum had swung to the other extreme? tbe wonder that picture-palaces were crowded. He °l6ae„ed• son of the man who was cribbed, cabined and con°:ity He (the preacher) had sufficient confidence in the ono of the race to believe that more serious enjoYl°. would be claimed and used by the next geaeretii..eold Our social outlook had altered. Our lathers were,,:oed to be content with the station in which God had P-9 to them and to respect their" betters." It %vas gloricle., to he alive when a man inquired "Why was I bar; e sweep the streets and that snob to wear a coron pettee.Let 48 It was glorious to be living when there is inquiry : Who are my betters ? " Tae rank is 1)1,30 ;if guinea stamp, the man's the gould for a' that.' , of better (continued the preacher) is the man of son, of sacrifice, and holy living. My peer is the bestore , good men. His rank is established by his pot We cannot grow boys as we grow peas. Lads 1 our like rows of pins. What I regard as a disgrace ci biae Blue Coat schools is that the pious founder belie'e° the snits, and white ties, and thicker boots were likeoe Deity—the same yesterday, to-day and for ever. ,bttt has rejoiced when the boys have filed before one Providence made the hair of one boy black and thsLoo another boy a fiery red. If the pious founder have regulated it, all the hair would be one CO Every boy is a personality and repays study. jor The afternoon service was conducted by' Morgan (8.L.8.). A unique feature was the presie of the Boys' Brigade, under the command of CaPreb Davis, the boys presented a smart and well-disoiPiTed appearance. Major Morgan addressed tbe boys advised them "to look out," "to look in," eusd were' look up." During the afternoon special lalfhtir. rendered by the children and anthems by the hed the evening service the Rev. H. Ogden