        <p>Church ? Under instruction of Synod, the obligaticcg usbt responsibilities of Church membership were br°teriati before the worshippers in St. John's PrfsbY geld Courch on Sunlay evening, when tie Rev. VV. with; emphasised some of the points in connection thetfLoteo and which had been placed in leaflet form and distnior throughout the Church. He dwelt upon the 13.,tret3; relationship existing between Christ arid the and of the tender and sacred nature of the tie' declared that as Free Churchmen, while not 1101,,de too much of Christ, they had certainly too little of the Church. He acknowledgfurfef ck grave imperfections, and its oftimes lango ,g 0 but stated that its value could best be 146 oor by imagining whit society would become if everY wore' straining and restraining influence of the Church boat to cease. He held that almost every child throuti the land was virtually a Church member, the 1,1°1,-roll, of names finding a place on the Church's baptiols:iveli and yet in the face of this, churches were comPsrnoos empty. Ile declared that the weakness of the P°30119 lay in the fact that while parents and sponsors fcli het took obligations upon themselves—to say nothlogi,°oV in many instances these are in the meantime istbose they seldom think of formally relinquishiogktfolif, obligations, and placipg them where they rigud ava belong on the shouldgers of the now develoPeqigeot matured youth or maiden. A reasonable and late' 09 presentation of the position, without in s?:YffereSs attempting coercion, would, he felt sure, tell a_lll tale in the life of the "Body of Christ," the Cbrlbey Church. If parents are not prepared to do thi,s, belt ought consistently to try and overcome tiler too superstitious conception of baptism, which is f 3 Common.</p>
