      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before 111 r. R. T. Fairc-lough (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion, Councillor Locker, and Messrs. T. Pemberton _ _ _</p>
        <p>and A. J. Jolley. BROKE INTO AN OFFICE.—John Kelly, 23, Lockett- street. pleaded gnilty to stealing between the 16th and 17th May, £4 2s. 91., the monies of Nlr. Henry Gandy, corn merchant, Wilderspool-road, Warrington.— The Chief Constable said prisoner was seen at about 10•15 p.m. on the 16th inst. in Bridge-street. Ile had evidently been drinking and asked P.C. Turner to direct him t,o chip potato shop. He was then carrying a dinner basket. At 11.10 the officer again saw him but he had not the basket with him. He said his wrist wasseverely cut and that an officer had done it by tripping him up. " That," commented the chief, "is frequently said with the usual amount of truth." Continuing, he said the prisoner was brought to the police station. He had been drinking and the officer preferred a charge of drunkenness against him. It was observed that he was endeavouring to conceal something behind his jacket, which turned out to he a bag containing the money. Later in the night it was discovered thit the premises of Mr. Gandy had been entered.—A window was broken and there were traces of blood. Prisoner's dinner basket was also found near the offices. He was formerly employed by Mr. Gandy and would know where the money was kept.—Kelly was sent to prison for one month with hard labour. MOTHER AND SON Cu ARGED.—Wm. Jenks (13), 20, Albert-street, was charged with stealing a pair of boots value 9s. 6d., the property of Henry Myers. a stallholder in the Market.—The boy's mother, was charged with receiving the stolen goods.—The Chief Constable said that after the boots were missed inquiries were made, and it was found that they had been pledged at two different pawnshops. On the 17th inst. the mother and son were passing through the market, and the boy bad admitted taking the boots.—Detective Clough said the lad had told him that he found the boots under some cabbage leaves.—The Chief: The boots had been marked in order to make them look as if they had been worn —The boy was ordered to receive six strokes with the birch, and the mother was fined 255. (including costs), with an altErn ttive of a month's imprisonment. A SAD CASE.—The adjourned case in which Thomas Knox Gibbs, a well-known footballer, 128, Hume-street was summoned by his wife Martha, for being an habitual drunkard, was heard.—Mr. Browne said the case had been adjourned to sae if the defendant would improve his behaviour. He had, however, b3en no better. On the previous Monday morning he came home at 1 o'clock in a drunken condition.—Nfre. Gibbs said she was tired of it aed would not consent to a mutual separation order because of the defendant's beastly habits. He had previously asked her to apply for a separation, but she would not.—ln answer to Mr. Steel, she said that during the past fortnight her husband had been sober, but had stayed out late.— Mr. Steel submitted that he had no case to answer. The wife had refused to have a mutual separation agree- ment. There was no evidence to the effect that defendant was an habitual drunkard.—The magistrates found that Gibbs was not an habitual drunkard and dismissed the case.—Mr. Browne: She has told him what she is going to do; go into service and leave him with the four children. TRIED TO OPEN I VElN.—Jamee Harris, 19, Beswick- street,was charged on remand with assaulting his mother. —The Chief Constable said that since the case was last heard he had communicated with the Borstal authorities who had prisoner in their charge. During the time he had been on remand prisoner had caused the police a great deal of anxiety and trouble. He had refused to go out of the cells unless the officers carried him. He had attempted, in a mild form, to open a vein in his arm, and he had done other serious things. The Borstal authorities were prepared to give him a chance, but he (the Chief) thought it was not a case where a chance ought to he given. Prisoner ought to be chargad with being an incorrigible rogue and vagabond. The police had founl him a situation, but he had misconducted himself and lust R.—Harris was sent to prison for one month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton and James Evans, Messrs. T. Locker and S. Wright.</p>
        <p>WILLING TO MAKE RIEPARATIoN.—John William Stockton, 11, Gandy-street, was charged with running away and leaving his wife end three children chargeable to the Union.—Mr. F.H. Gid man (relieving officer), said that on the 25th April the wife applied for relief for herself end children. She stated that her husband left her on December 7th, and for four months she had not heard from him. In the meantime she had been relieved to the extent of 355. A fortnight ego she found out that prisoner was living with his father in Sheffield, where he was in work. He bad said he would come over and make arrangements for the repayment of the money. —Prisoner said he had done wrong, hut would make what reparation he could. He would do his best to pay the money.—The Magistrates' Clerk : It is not a c %se of paying the money now.—The case was adjourned for a month to see if prisoner would keep his promise to pay sq• per week to the Guardians and send money to his wife.</p>
        <p>BIGAMIST AND His Wiris.—Thoma• Phillip., late of Middlesbrough, was charged with being £l5 Gd. in Arrears of maintenance towards his wife Lillie, 62, Cartwright-street. Warrington.—Prisoner : I do owe it, I expect, but I have a number of reasons for not paving it. He then handed a statement to the Bench, but the Chief Constable said he would explain the case. He said that the debt was really £35. The prisoner was in a very weak state, having recsntly undergone an operation. It was a question whether he intended to pay anything. He had to go in hospital avin to he operated upon, and it was doubtful whether he would recover sufficiently to be of any further use in life. He had been committed for four months at York assizes for bigamy.—The wife stated that prisoner had paid her only 10s. since the order was made in 1912.—Prisoner : We can never live together again. I have communicated with her from the hospital, through her brother-in-law. —The Chief Constable: Prisoner has said his wife was not true. Wbat has he done to make her true? She cannot subsist on air.—The case was adjourned for a week.</p>
        <p>WANTED A FRESH TRlAL—Robert Rigby, boiler- maker. St. Helens. was charged at the instance of his wife Nora, 11, King-street, 'Warrington, with being .£3 2s. Bcl. in arrears of maintenance.—Prisoner said he had not paid because he had not been working. He would keep up the payments in the future. He had given her only 49. since April 18th. When the warrant was read over to him by Detective 'Metcalfe he said "I want a fresh trial before I pay her any money."—He was ordered to pay the money or go t 3 prison for one month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, IV. Richardson and T. Locker.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO A ITORSE.—William Horrobin, 22, Manley-street, was summoned for cruelty to a hone by v.orking it whilst in as unfit state, and George Hill, contractor, Scotland-road, was summoned for causing the horse to be so worked.—lt was stated that at 3.35 p.m. on May 19th, defendant was in Rowley-lane in charge of an aged bay gelding, attached to a lurry. The animal was in a poor condition, and showed symptoms of a sore on its hack. A sore, the size of the palm of a hand, was found underneath the saddle. Blood was issuing from the wound, and the saddle was soaked with it.—lnspector Wainwright and Inspector Dee rave evidence as to the condition of the animal.—Horrib in said he was a new hand and put the wrong saddle on the horse. He did not notice the sore until the sergeant pulled of! the saddle.—Horrobin was fined ss. and costs and Hill 10s. and costs.</p>
        <p>PROMISE TO REFORM.—Edward Owen, 18, Heaton- street, was summoned by his wife for being an habitual drunkard.—Defendant admitted the offence, but promised to sign the pledge.—The case was adjourned for a month to see if defendant would keep his promise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. A. J. Jolley (in the chair), Mossrs. W. Roberts, J. Upson, W. T. Williams and Dr. Naden.</p>
        <p>"LIKE A FIRE ENGINE." Charles Jones, motor driver, 326, Grimshaws-lane, Middleton Junction, Nlanchester, was summoned for driving a motor-car to the danger of the public in Church-street on the 13th inst.—Air. Field, who had laid the complaint, said that the car accelerated its sped to pass him on his bicycle in order to avoid another vehicle. It went along like a fire engine. It would be going at about 15 miles per hour.—Defendant said be had been driving for the last ten years and had never had a complaint made against him. The vehicle was registered as a motor car and the speed limit was 20 miles per hour.—The Chief Constable : He might only be going six or seven miles in Warrington and that speed wpuld be dangerous to the public.—Defendant said he knew the dangerous roads in Warrington. The last time be was in Court he had assisted a police officer and lost his situation through it, and this was what he got in return.—The Chief Constable spoke of the excessive speeds at which vehicles travelled in the town and said that the danger was evident, more particularly in Manchester-road.—ln fining defendant £1 and costs the Chairman said he seemed to be under some misappre- hension as to his responsibilities. They thanked Mr. Field for bringing about that conviction. STRIKE INCIDENT. John Higham, 21, Melville- street, and William Coyne, 159, Longford-street, were summoned for fighting.—lt was stated that the fight arose when the strike was on at the brewery, Higham was one of the men who continued at work and Coyne was out on strike. Coyne called Higham a " sca.b " and "black leg." A fight ensued and Ingham got the worst of it. There was a crowd of strikers near and there might have been a semi-riot if the fight had con- tinued.—Coyne said Higham rushed at him and he defended himself.—Higham was dismissed and Coyne was ordered to pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RUNCORN PUBLICAN FINED.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PUBLICAN FINED.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Monday, Edward Orme, licensee of the Mariners' Arms, Percival-lane, was fined 20s. and costs for opening his premises during prohibited hours. Samuel and Thomas Bowyer, of Percival-lane; Samuel Johnson, of Clarence-street and William Leyland, boatman, were charged with being on licensed premises during prohibited hours. No evidence was offered, and the Bench 'imposed a fine of 10e. and costs in each case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 31, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 31, 1913.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Conservatives Hold the Seat.</p>
        <p>MR. KAY-SHUTTLEWORTH ON THE FOUNDATIONS OF FUTURE VICTORY.</p>
        <p>After one of the longest and most strenuous cam- paigns on record, the by-election in the Altrincham division, which took place on Wednesday, resulted in the Conservatives retaining the seat. The figures were announced at noon on Thursday as follows : G. C. Hamilton (C) 9,409 L. U. Kay-Shuttleworth (L)</p>
        <p>Conservative majority Historically Altrincbarn is a Conservative seat. On only two occasions has it returned a Liberal, and the late Sir William Crossley, who won it in 1906 and kept it in January, 1910, had powerful local connections. An analysis of the poll, as compared with that in December, 1910, gives the following results : Increase in electorate Increase in total poll 1,671 Increase in Conservative poll - Increase in Liberal poll 264</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>MR. KAY-SHUTTLEWORTH'S SPEECH. The Foundations 0! a Future Victor y.</head>
        <p>MR. KAY-SHUTTLEWORTH'S SPEECH. The Foundations 0! a Future Victor y.</p>
        <p>After the declaration of the result, Mr. Kay Shuttleworth went first to the Altrincham Liberal Club, where he had a great reception. In the cours:, of a speech he said : " One thing we can be quite sure of; we shall never be sorry we have fought this battle, and we have laid the foundations for victory in the future. We may be quite sure that in the end the best cause will win. For the present we have lost on the temporary unpopularity of the Insurance Act, but, however, many by-elections we may lose, every year that passes will show that the Government were right in laying the founda- -60115 a 5 happitr future and more healthy homes for wc;rhingmen. who in times of prosperity should pre- pare for the day of eickness that was sure to come. This cause shall win in the end. I don't believe Nva have lost one vote because of Home Rule—not one on the record of the Government. We have lost simply and solely on the Insurance Act, and by the time this constituency is again called upon to pass its verdict we shall s-.?.e everyone blessing the Insurance Act, as they bless Old Age Pensions to-day. We are confident that the Government are moving in the right direc- pon, and no lost by-elections will make us change one item in our plitical faith."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ELECTION INCIDENTS AT LYMM.</head>
        <p>ELECTION INCIDENTS AT LYMM.</p>
        <p>On Saturday night as the Liberal party were holding a meeting at the Cross the Junior Conserva- tives came up and commenced an opposition meeting, saying that they had secured the rights of the position for that night by having put a board there at 12 a.m. announcing that they would hold a meeting. This interruption rather angered the crowd and trouble might soon have arisen but for the Liberal Secretary asking his party's adherents to hold their gathering in Pepper-street, which they did and had a successful time. However, at the conclusion of their meeting, the crowd returned to the Cross, where the Tory speakers were subjected to a rather warm time. At about 10.15 p.m. the latter party brought their oratory to a close and at once Messrs Gotobed, Gould and Radcliffe took up their stand for the Liberals and had an excellent meeting despite the lateness of the hour. LIVELY ENCOUNTER AT THE CROSS.</p>
        <p>Mon-day found the Liberal rally in the Drill Hall, reported elsewhere in this issue; and a Unionist meeting at the Cross, where a bitter denunciation of the Insurance Act was the main theme. The venues of the meetings were reversed on Tuesday night. At the termination of the Tory gathering the party formed a torchlight procession, supplemented by a bugle band from Warrington, which toured the district. A most regrettable incident occurred when the promotors of the proce,sion tried to break up the Liberal meeting at the Cross, and had it not been for the strong and wise policy of the Liberal leaders in keeping their sup- porters in check, a most ugly scene would have taken place. Wednesday was the testing time of a long campaign ,Ehen the large and excellent band of Liberal workers, though out-aumbered in vehicles in the proportion of bbout nine to one, put forth every effort to get their supForters to the poll. That the endeavours of both "Tarries were responded to was demonstrated by the fact that ever ninety per cent. of the Lymm electors registered their votes. The usual election scenes were enacted during the day, cars, traps, cycles, being decorated with party colours. At about 4.45 p.m. Mr. Hamilton visited the village and was accorded a cordial welcome from his supporters, as was Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth, who appeared soon afterwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>PLEASING CEREMONY.</head>
        <p>A PLEASING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>The Liberal candidate and those with him went up to Newfield where they .were given a tremendous ovation, and a most pleasant ceremony was performed by two little girls on behalf Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth's Booth's Hill Ward supporters. Eva Walker presented Mrs. Kay-Shuttleworth with a charming bouquet of red flowers, and a photo of the candidate taken at the May Queen Festival, whilst Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth was the recipient of a decorated horse shoe from Nellie Bate. The candidate was s overcome with the sincere and unexpected ceremony that he could hardly find words to express himself, and be left Lymm with a feeling that at any rate one part of the Division was staunch and true to Liberalism. The village was the centre of much good humour during the day and it was not till midnight that the locality had resumed its normal aspect. MR. KAY-SHUTTLEWORTH'S SEND-OFF.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Liberal party was called for Thurs. day evening in the Congregational Schoolroom, to commence at 7.30. It was rather unfortunate that the unsuccessful candidate arrived earlier than expected (7.20) as he found the room only partly filled and the cheers which greeted his appearancce, though enthusiastic lacked volume. The building, however, quickly filled, and if Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth had been at the top of the poll the proceedings could not have been more fervent. After a few opening words by the Chairman, Mr. J. D. B. Smith, Mr. Kay- Shuttlevvorth, who was clearly labouring under the influence of great emotion, expressed his thanks to all who had thrown themselves into the conflict on his behalf, and who, like himself, had done everything possible to secure a Liberal victory. After a few words from his younger brother, Mr. Edward Kay-Shuttleworth, the candidate had t 3 leave for another part of the constituency, and received such a send-off as will long live in the memory of, himself and Mrs. Kay-Shuttleworth, who has made herself highly popular in Lymm. Speeches were afterwards delivered by the Chairman, Mr. Mather Mr. Radcliffe, and dila Rev. R. W. B. Whiteway.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LYIVINI LIBERALS.</head>
        <p>LYIVINI LIBERALS.</p>
        <p>MR. W. G. C. GLADSTONE, M.P., ADDRESSES GREAT MEETING.</p>
        <p>TORIES PREACH REBELLION.</p>
        <p>"Part of it was for your grandfather," was the friendly ejaculation of a member of the great audience at the Liberal meeting in the Drill Hall, Lymm, on Monday evening, when Mr. W. G. C. Gladstone, in opening a stirring speech, returned thanks for the magnificent reception which had been accorded him. "Yes. the greater part of it," was Mr. Gladstone's modest comment. True, the reverence which all Liberals feel for the name and lifework of " The Grand Old Man " bespeaks a cordial welcome and respectful hearing for any bearer of the name of Gladstone. But Mr. W. G. C. Gladstone is not one who merely basks in the reflected glory of a great ancestor. Although young,he has already becomea force in British politics, and a worthy upholder of tho.;e traditions of conscientiousness and high courage which his grand- father faithfully observed in every department of life. He is, moreover, a polished and convincing speaker, and possesses the happy faculty of putting political issues in a nutshell, so that they can be readily grasped by all his bearers. , The fact that within a week two crowded and inspiring meetings have been held in the largest ball in Lymm, speaks well for the vitality of the Liberal cause in the district. Monday's demonstration was marked by the utmost enthusiasm and the opposition was quite a negligible element. When Mr. and Mrs. Kay-Shuttleworth arrived, after a round of meetings in different parts of the constituency, the audience rose and greeted them with lusty cheers. _</p>
        <p>The meeting was presided ever by Mr. John Alfred Morris (president of the Altrincham Liberal Associa- tion), and anion a others on the platform were : Mr.</p>
        <p>G L Welford (president of the Lymni Liberal Association), Mr. J. D. B. Smith (chairman), Mr. Reginald Barker (hon. secretary), Major and Mts. England, Revs. F. E. Evans (Appleton Thorn) and Dendy Agate (Altrincham), Mr. and Yrs. J. T. Clarke, Mr. and Mrs. T. Wyatt, Mr. and Mrs. J. Radcliffe, Mr. S. W. Gould, Ur. W. Gould, Mr. S. Davies, Mrs. Dodds (president of the Lymm Women's Liberal Association), Miss Welford (chairman), Mr. R. H. Gotobed, Mr. J. Brearley, Mr. D. Daniels, Mr. J. H. Mather and Mr. H. Cliffe (agent for the division).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>MR. GLADSTONE AND THE ISSUES.</head>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE AND THE ISSUES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone succinctly stated the issues of the fight. A good deal, he said, hangs upon the result of this election—more than some people think. It means a great deal to our opponents, who will look to the result very anxiously for several reasons. They will look to it to see, in the first place, whether the compromise on Tariff Reform, as published at Edinburgh,has sufficiently removed the unpopularity of Tariff Reform to give the Conservative party an opportunity of getting back to office in the sweet by-and-by. To put it in another way, they will look to the result anxiously to see whether it is possible nowadays for a man to be a Tariff Reformer and not a fool, at one and the same time. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>THE FLAG OF CONSCRIPTION. Next they will look to this result to see whether it is safe for the Conservative candidate, in answer to a question, to run up the flag of conscription, provided he calls it compulsory military service. They will look to it to see whether Englishmen really approve of a candidate who pronounces himself in favour of defiance of the law, even to the point of revolution. They will look to see whether it is wise to resist to the end the reformation of the Church in Wales, in accordance with the wishes of the Welsh people, and whether it is possible for the Conservative candidate to secure the support of Liberal Churchmen by rudely tell- ing them, as the C3nsnvative candidate did on May 13th, to put their religion, for once at any rate, before their politics, and also by shamelessly adding, "Stand up and defend your religion, and vote on your' polities at the general election." They will look to the result anxiously to see whether a Conservative candidate, who preaches the immoral doctrine tbat it is right to fight on your religion at a by-election and on your politics at a gene,-al election, can always secure the half-hearted blessing of a bishop. (Hear, hear.) They will look anxiously to see whether the wall-worn dodge of pretending that your only objection to the principle of "one man one vote" is because it is not accompanied by a measure of redistribution, still deceives anyone. Next they will look't) see whether it is still possible—shall I add honourable ?—to endeavour to excite and to profit by prejudice and hostility to the Insurance Act at by-elections, when the leaders of the Conservative party have declared the measure to be a good one in the House of Commons and the Conserva- tive party have claimed, over and over again, as I have beard them, credit for its passage through the House of Commons. Finally, they will look to see whether there is any chance of the Conservatives getting back to office before they die a natural'death—(laughter)—or whether the prospect cf office is limited to th.ir grandsons or great-grandsons, or again, whether the prospect of having to continue to awell in the wilderness and the solitary places is such that it justifies a further altera- tion of their policy and the abandonment of those principles which they had been foonsla enough t; publish, (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>ALTRINCHAM'S PUNISHMENT.</head>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM'S PUNISHMENT.</p>
        <p>Up to 1906 (continued Mr. Gladstone) this constitu- ency was a safe seat for the Tory party. That, thank 'leaven, it can never be again. (Cheers.) So safe was it and so hopeless, that the same Liberal candidate hardly ever fought the seat more than once. Now, that is all changed—and changed so completely that since 1906 the same Conservative has never fought the seat more than once. (Cheers.) In the glorious days of 1906, the scales fell from the eyes of the electors of this zonstituency and they saw a great light—the light of Liberalism. Lfor one,venture to say that they will not be content to return to dwell in the darkness of Con- servatism, once they have seen that light. True that by a most regrettable mistake they have done so for the last two years, but we Liberals are all agreed that two years' representation by a Conservative is sufficient punishment for any constitueney—(laughter)—and we are sure that Altrincham has been sufficiently punished for a momentary back-sliding. We Liberals are quite ready to forgive and forget that little infidelity on the part of Altrincham, provided she now comes back to Liberalism and never makes the same sort of mistake again. (Cheers.) My last word to the electors of this constituency is this : " Come back to Liberalism. Last time you put in the wrong man. Don't do it again." (Loud cheers.)</p>
        <p>AN ULSTER PROTESTANT'S PLEA FOR HOME RULE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert McGhee, M.P., an Ulster Protestant member, then delivered a speech on the Home Rule question, which was characterised by great earnestness, and was particularly forceful by reason of its modera- tion. He paid a graceful tribute to the service which the Librrat candidates further rendered to the " Grand Old Man" in his efforts for the emancipation of Ireland. Continuing, he said it was a remarkable thing that an Irishman should be addressing a British audience on the question of conceding self-government to a small branch of the Empire. " You," he went on, "a community distinguished throughout the civilised world for hundreds of years for standing by all nations struggling for self-government ; you the greatest Home Rulers that the world has ever seen; you, who have built up 28 self-governing nations that compose your empire ; you, who would not dare to allow any human being in your race to attempt to take Home Rule from any one of those States ;—aud yet I am supposed to come and talk to you,to try to convince you of the merits of Home Rule. I would scorn to do anything of the kind. You know all about it, far better than I do." Mr. McGhee proceeded to show how the colonies had been won from disaffection to loyalty by the granting of self-government, and asserted that the United States would have continued a part of this Empire, but for the stupidity of Toryism over a century ago. Just as the Tories were fighting Home Rule for Ireland, so they had opposed it in the case of thellventy-eight States which had already secured it. They opposed Home Rule in South Africa until a l'powerful House of Commons, independent on that question of the House of Lords, was able to carry it in spite of them. Another striking fact was that the Tories, in regard to the States where Home Rule had been conceded, were now just as strong Home Rulers as the Liberals. (Laughter.) When the Irish Home Rule Bill had received the Royal assent, they would probably find hundreds, if not thousands of the Tory party, joining the King's party and going to Ireland to open the new Parliament—(cheers)—and in less than a year from that date they would be declaring that thte Home Rule experiment in Ireland was a great and triiimphant success. (Cheers.) That was the real security against civil war in Ulster. A LAWYERS' REVOLUTION. Those who were really opposed to Home Rule were about ten King'a Counsellors. It was a lawyers' struggle, in which gowns and briefs would be the most formidable weapons employed. , (Laughter.) Could theyitell him .of any part of the world where a handful of lawyers had made a Civil War? (Laughter.) They would make civil turmoil, because it brought them briefs, but it was all they intended to do. (Laughter.) Before twelve months had passed, the men of Ulster would be the very best men in the Irish Parliament, helping them to work the Act. (Cheers.) The Ulster people were not coining to England to make bitter personal attacks. It was only the interested gentlemen, who had been fattening and battening upon the affairs of Ireland, who were making all the dust and trouble. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>REVIVING IRELAND'S INDUSTRIES.</head>
        <p>REVIVING IRELAND'S INDUSTRIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. L. Welford, who moved the vote of confidence, said it was only doing an act of justice to Ireland to give back to her the means of ruling herself, that she might in the future re-establish some of the industries which once were hers, but which had been ruthlessly stamped out by the Government of this country. The manner in which we had governed Ireland, interfering with her progress and power of developing herself, was one of the very worst chapters in the pages of history. Whether they voted Liberal or not, they could not stop Home Rule. It was as certain as that the sun would rise to-morrow. (Cheers.) Referring to the Insurance Act, Mr. WeHord said it was only the beginning of one of the greatest social steps ever taken by any civilized country. (Cheers)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>PRIME MINISTER'S MESSAGE.</head>
        <p>THE PRIME MINISTER'S MESSAGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth increased the enthusiasm of the meeting by reading the following message from the Prime Minister:—</p>
        <p>I send you all good wishes for success. The issues before the electors of Altrincharn have been very clearly stated by you. The policy of the Opposition is to meet the persistent demand of two nations by mere negations, while on its constructive side there is presented a truncated edition of Tariff Reform, exhibiting all the vices of Protection, and which could not fail to work incalculable damage to the enpandinc, commerce of the country, and particularly to the cotton industry of Lancashire. Food taxes are neither repudiated nor abandoned, but postponed to a more convenient time. The great system of National Health Insurance has only been in operation a few months, and is proving of immense value to the working people. As was expected, experience has suggested some amend- ments to the Insurance Act, and a Bill embodying these amendments will shortly be laid before Parliament. I should welcome your return to the House of Commons, not only as a supporter of the Liberal policy, but as the son of an old colleague in Mr. Gladstone's Government.</p>
        <p>The Cindidate devoted a large part of his speech to Mr. Hamilton's preaching of rebellion and civil war in Ireland, and pointed out the incongruity of a gentle- man courting the votes of a great constituency and then showing his contempt for Parliament by words of that character. Alluding to the Government's record, Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth said no one could say that the country had suffered as the result of eight years of Liberal government. Our trade at home and over-seas was never more prosperous than to-day; the percentage of unemployment was never less; great measures of social reform had been placed on the statute book, and we had made provision, in the face of growing diffi- culties, to maintain the supremacy of the seas. (Cheers-)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>BOTTOM DOG.</head>
        <p>THE BOTTOM DOG.</p>
        <p>Liberalism's War Against Slums and Poverty.</p>
        <p>THE ULSTER "CARRION CROWS."</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Carruthers, M.A., of New Brighton, was the principal speaker at the weekly open-air meeting in connection with the Progressive Labour League, held at Bridge Foot on Monday evening. Mr. J. 'NORTON, who presided, spoke of the useful work the League had been able to accomplish in facilitating the free expression of free opinions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>APPLIED CHRISTIANITY.</head>
        <p>APPLIED CHRISTIANITY.</p>
        <p>Mr. CARRUTHERS said that Liberals were very proud of their political faith and of their glorious record. The spirit of Liberalism, which was jus',, another name for applied Christianity, was permeating everyone. Better times were in store to make happy the worker whose existence was drab and monotonous. Speaking of the Old Age Pensions Act, Mr. Carruthers said the Tories had for many years dangled it in front of the electorate as a bait to catch a vote; but they did not, take a single step to make the pension scheme an accom- plished fact. No sooner did the Liberals come into power than they pass.d that great measure of reform. In Mr. Lloyd George they had a practical visionary. (Applause.) He lifted up his eyes and, looking along the dusty roads, said, "I must do something for those for whom there are so many obstacles to stumble over by the way." He brought into operation the Insurance Act, which they should all look at broadly. It was a beginning and that was what they wanted. A very large proportion of the mothers of England discharged the sacred yet perilous duty of motherhood under conditions which were full of danger both to mother and child. The Insurance Act did a great deal to better those conditions and to make the mother and child happy. The attitude of the Tory party towards those two great measures of reform had been a tragic example of what history had gone to prove—that the Tories did not carp one jot for people. TdE KEYNOTE OF LIBERALISNI.</p>
        <p>The keynote of Liberalism was increased liberty for the bottom dog; increased freedom for the aged, worker, greater comfort for the mother and succour for the consumptive. They would find the keynote of liberty in all Liberal reforms. Home Rule meant the granting of greater freedom to Ireland. We had been cradled in liberty and the granting of Home Rule would be doing something towards binding the Irish more closely to the British Commonwealth. " King " Carson thought it a terrible insult for Mr. Birrell to have called the Ulster members "carrion crows." In his (the speaker's) opinion it was an insult to the carrion crows, because they did their work without any thought that they were ,any- thing else but carrion crows; they did not think they were eagles. (Laughter.) A contented Ireland would be a greater source of strength tons than a.discontented Ireland. The long winter of discontent would shortly be turned into a beautiful summer of happiness and peace. Dealing with the land question, the speaker said that many ills would be solved by taxing land values for rating purpw-i, Ile deubteci if there Nvn anyenr, who was a thoughtful man who had not be disturbed by that question which was occupying the minds of many of toe greatest thinkers of the age. They looked around them and saw the great inequalities in wealth. They saw also that during the ltst 40 or 60 years there had been enormous advances in labour-saving machinery. There were poverty, crime and slums in all our great centres. At one end of the social scale was misery and chaos, and at the other end boundless wealth. From all parts of the world there was rising with increasing volume the cry from the worker for relief from some- thing which he could not ucderstaud. The aggregate of wealth still grew beyond the dfeatns of avarice. It had been said—and he was inclined to believe it—that at a period of great national cri-lis the slums would boil over. The dynamic of the Liberal movement was to try to get down and help the man on the poverty margin. That was a question lying at the very founda- tion of national life, and if not tackled seriously it would greatly affect the social and moral life of the country. He believed, however, that the scales were beginning to drop from our eyes. In the Insurance Act it was recognised that the health of the nation was the care of the nation. It was also the duty of the nation to see that the worker got a due share of the wealth which he produced. (Hear, hear.) That was the great problem of the future and they believed that the Liberal policy would help to solve it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>NOT SOCIALISM.</head>
        <p>NOT SOCIALISM.</p>
        <p>It was not the socialistic " policy " of the division of property, for he believed,whether rightly or wrongly, that the division of property meant the destruction of property. Ruskin well put it when he said that it did not necessarily mean that the possession of riches would oppress the poor. "Riches," he said," were a form of strength." The manhood of a nation was that nation's greatest asset. Nations were renewed from the bottom and ' not from the top. They would no doubt ask how the Liberals were going to get right down to help the worker on the margin ? They wanted to tax the value of land held up by landowners who nad secured that land—he would not mince words—by sheer robbery. When the feudal system passed away and the industrial system took its place, the landowners thought it equit- able that they should retain the land and transfer the burdens of it onto the people. This was their idea of a fair division, and ever since that time the burdens had been gradually transferred to the backs of the workers. Mr. Lloyd George said that the land monopoly lutist be burst up, for under that lay the foundation of a great deal of social reform. (Applause.) The Liberals propoEed to tax the land values and use the money to defray the education rate, from which the people cried out, to relieve the poor, main roads, asylums and police rates, which they claimed ought to be discharged by the national exchequer. Tbe rates at the present time were £80,000,000, and under an equitable system of taxation they would be reduced by half. Large farms that were not producing what they should produce would be gradually broken up into small holdings. The result would be that a labourer who secured one of those small-holdings would make 3s. per week for each acre of land. The inevitable result would be to retain on the land men who would otherwise gravitate to the city to flood the labour market and so reduce wages. Nothing should be done without the land, and, by increasing the amount of land at the dis- posal of the community, they were increasing the possibities of life and depleting the labour market in the town. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>HOTEL HYDROPATHIC, PEEBLES.</head>
        <p>HOTEL HYDROPATHIC, PEEBLES.</p>
        <p>,That medizeval town of ancient antiquity, Peebles, Tclth its historical associations, owes a very great deal of its popularity to the erection of the Hotel Hydropathic. The structure, which is Georgiaa in style, combines all the stateliness, solidity, and com- pactness of design which usually characterises this style of structure. The internal design and arrange- ment is most pleasing to the eye, and in conformity with all modern ideas of comfort and luxury. A wide and handsomely appointed corridor opens at the west end into a beautiful conservatoryand at the east end into a huge dining hall, in this way securing a vista of nearly 400 feet. Billiard and smoking rooms both present a very attractive feature in having their walls covered with an enormous oil canvas of the Battle of Banncckburn. The ballroom is of unusually large size, and opens off the grand corridor opposite the lounge. The floor, which is 100 ft. long by 40ft. wide, is on springs for dancing. There is a spacious lounge, the view from which is beautiful in the extreme,and it makes a delightful common meeting- place. Sheltered balconies and verandahs abound, and are very grateful to the invalid visitor. The hotel stands in its own grounds of upwards of 33 acres, which are well wooded and laid out to great advan- tage. There are several tennis courts, both ash and grass; putting green, also a bowling green and archery court. Golfers are also well provided for in the way of a full-sized 18-hole course. There is also plenty of fishing in the locality. Special attention should be called to the medical treatment, which is in charge of an eminent physician. An illustrated brochure relating to this department, as well as other details, can be obtained from Mr. W. A. Thiem, the manager of the Hydro.</p>
      </div>
