      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</head>
        <p>BIRTHS, MARRIAGES, DEATHS.</p>
        <p>Notices of births, deaths, and marriages, in memoriam notices, and acknowledgments of sympathy are inserted in the " Examiner " at the following rates : —Not exceeding 20 words is. 0.1. each; every additicnal seven words, tid. NOTE :—Announcements for this column must be authenticated by the name and address of the sender. In no case can they be received by telephone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Deaths.</head>
        <p>Deaths.</p>
        <p>FAIRBROTHER.—At 2, Chester-road, Elizabeth Fairbrother, aged 7 years. MILLAR.—On the 27th inst. at Southport, Helen (Nellie) dearly beloved wife, of Archibald Millar, " Normabhurst," Fairfield-road. Latchford, aged 35 years. Interment St. Thomas's Church, Stockton Heath, Saturday at 3 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>JUVENILE WORKERS,</head>
        <p>JUVENILE WORKERS,</p>
        <p>A Scheme for Selecting Suitable Employment.</p>
        <p>BUREAU TO BE ESTABLISHED.</p>
        <p>An important scheme for the regulation of juvenile employment has been drawn up by the Warrington Education Committee. It is proposed to establish a bureau, with the object of giving to boys and girls under the age of 17 years assistance with respect to the choice of suitable work. A SPECIAL COMMITTEE. It is proposed to appoint a special sub-committee to be known as the Juvenile Employment Sub-com- mittee," and to be constituted as follows, namely : II members of the Education Committee (including the Chairman and Deputy-Chairman) ; 3 teachers (of whom one at least shall be a woman) to he nominated by the local Branch of the National Union of Teachers; 2 representatives of employers. 2 representatives of the Trades and Labour Council; and 2 other persons appointed by the Education Corn-I mittee. The Divisional Officer of Labour Exchanges for the district and the Manager of the local Labour Exchange I will be invited to attend the meetings of the Sub- committee, but will not be members thereof. It will be the duty of the Sub-committee, subject to any directions of the Education Committee : (a) to arrange for suitable information and advice with regard to the choice of employment to be given to boys and girls directly, or through their parents, both before and after they leave school ; (b) to co-operate with the local Labour Exchange officers in registering applications of boys and girls for employment, and in selecting applicants for suitable vacancies ; and (c) to advise the Board of Trade in regard to the manage- ment of the Labour Exchange in relation to juvenile applicants for employment. TO ENCOURAGE EVENING CLASSES. In carrying out their duties the Sub-committee shall : (a) take every opportunity of encouraging boys and girls to continue their education after leaving the day schools ; (b) study the conditions of employments as these affect the prospects of boys and girls, and the need and facilities for continued education, for which purpose the officers of the Labour Exchange will furnish the Sub-committee with all the information in their power with regard to the prospects and conditions of employment; and (c) make use, as far as possible, of existing organisations or appoint Sub- committees, and take such further steps as may seem desirable for the purpose -)f promoting the welfare of children up to the age of 17 years. In carrying out its duties, the Sub-committee shall have the services of the Director of Education and his •chief assistant, who shall have the assistance of an officer for this purpose. BUREAU TO BE ESTABLISHED. The work of the Sub-committee in connection with the registration of applications for employment shall be condncted at a central office to be established jointly by the Authority and the Board of Trade, under the name of the " Warrington Juvenile Employment Bureau," and to be situated in the Education -Office, Sankey-street. An officer or other representat;ve of the Sub-com- mittee shall be regularly available at the Bureau, at such hours as may be determined by the Sub- committee, in order to interview applicants for employment</p>
        <p>THE TEACHERS' PART.</p>
        <p>-- The head teacher of every day school shall furnish the Sub-committee with information as regards employment concerning every pupil leaving the school, and with a report as to the character, conduct and capabilities of the pupil. The Sub-committee shall also be furnished with a copy of any repott made by the School Medical Officer on such pupil, and of any school record or leaving card given to the pupil on leaving. If any pupil has not,within six weeks before leaving, obtained suitable employment, and the assistance of the Committee is required on his behalf, the head teacher shall forward to the Sub.committee an applica- tion on such form as may from time to time be pre- scribed by the Sub-committee. NOTIFYING VACANCIES. Boys and girls desiring employment who have left school and who are below the age of 17 years, shall apply in person at the Bureau, and will be registered there. Arrangements will be made for them to be interviewed, either at the time of registration or as soon as possible thereafter, in order that they may be fully advised before vacancies for employment ate brought to their notice, and that the desirability of continuing their general education may be impressed upon them. All applications for employment which reach the Bureau, either from the schools or from individual applicants, shall at once be made available for the use both of the Sub-committee and of the Labour Er-change. The officer of the Labour Exchange will in all cases inform the representative of the Sub-committee as to the manner in which each vacancy is ultimately filled.</p>
        <p>PREMIER MOTOR CYCLICS.—SOIe Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge et, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>THE MESSAGE FROM ALTRINCHAM. The message from Altrincham will not be wasted on the Liberal Party. There is plenty of educational work to be done, as the result of this latest election shows, and that educa- tional work will assuredly be done before the next General Election comes round. Con- sidering the many interests that have been " touched " by the Liberal Party, it is really surprising that the losses at the by-elections in more than two years can be numbered on the fingers of two hands, and that the Opposi- tion are thrown into such ecstacies when they retain their own seats that they hoist the flags over their buildings and betake them- selves to laying odds by means of the public hoardings. The fact of the matter is that in the Altrincham Division the Conservatives are convinced that Tariff Reform is dead and that they can return to the fold to which they belonged without threatening the financial tability and industrial welfare of their country.</p>
        <p>That accounts for much and it is as satis- factory to the Liberals, who have killed Protection by their determined stand for ten years, as it is to the gentlemen in charge of the flags and the odds. But misrepresentation was rampant, especially relating to the Welsh Church Bill and the , Insurance Act, which Conservatives are still willing to run the danger of exploiting for the sake of immediate gain. Mr. ASQUITH', in his message to the Liberal candidate, announced that the amendments which experience has shown to be desirable will be embodied in a Bill during the present Session. Mr. ' ASQUITH'S Message also contained a reminder which the British elector everywhere should take to heart. The Opposition, he said, presents "a truncated edition of Tariff Reform, exhibiting all the vices of Protection, and which could not fail to work incalcul- able damage to the expanding com- merce of the country. Food-taxes are neither repudiated nor abandoned, but postponed to a more convenient time."</p>
        <p>_ At the worst, it can not be that the electors will change a Liberal Government for what might follow at the hands of men who dare not assert their intentions, but prefer to wait</p>
        <p>fora more conveqient time, and to pray in the meantime for a spell of bad trade that might somewhat obscure the workingman's vision. It is a desperate claance, but it is the only one, and is known by fhe pleasant name of the "new method."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 31, 1913. 1 THE PASSING WEEK.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 31, 1913. 1 THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>STONES IN GLASS HOUSES.</p>
        <p>Our contemporary last Saturday, in a poem by Alderman Bennett, makes the stately lines of a flowing first verse declare that: We'll drink ta-night in pease . . . " The Empire of our dreams."</p>
        <p>We claim to know something of Alderman Bennett,and, therefore, decline to believe that he would ever write anything so foolish. In olden times when trial by ordeal was in vogue prisoners might have been set to chew peas and other dry morsels, and their ability or inability to perform this task in such exciting and saliva-drying conditions— life sometimes depending on the issue— left to establish their innocence or prove their guilt. But to drink to an Empire, if only an Empire.of dreams, in pease would be ridiculous. The Empire of a nightmare might possibly suit but we do not press that point. Pea soup would be more readily , disposed of, because a semi-liquid concoction is better suited for drinking purposes than a hard, unprepared and uninviting vegetable. But against that arrangement comes the fact that the drinking has to be done "to-night," and pea soup is better taken with the day before one. But as we began, so we conclude. We know the writer never said any- thing of the kind. It is one of those little errors that will occur in newspaper offices, however careful those responsible try to be. A few weeks ago we made a slight slip and our friendly contemporary went out of its way to call attention to it. We should not have referred to these "pease," which so incongruously do duty for "peace," had it not appeared necessary to remind our critics that those who live in glass houses should not throw stones. It is a dangerous practice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>CRITICAL JUNCTURE.</head>
        <p>A CRITICAL JUNCTURE.</p>
        <p>The discussion which took place at the Education Committee meeting on Monday night relating to the Grammar School would seem to show that a critical point has arrived in the history of secondary education in the borough. Not much real information was allowed to be made public but the general points are clear when taken con- jointly with other well known facts. There is in contemplation an extension of the facilities for affording secondary instruction to our boys and girls. At the present time we have a well equipped Secondary School but it is woefully small. It accommodates 200 pupils, boys and girls, or, including bursars, 250. This year 160 applicants of both sexes have applied for admission; only 70 or 80 can be accepted. The rest must stand down and perhaps have their futures blighted by a chance non-admission to benefits for which their parents pay, in common with the parents of the more successful applicants. In such circumstances the Education Committee are considering a plan under which it is hoped they will be able to erect a fitting school for the boys of War- rington, leaving the present buildings 'com- pletely to the girls. It is a capital arrange- ment, and is well calculated to achieve the ends which Mr. Henry Roberts says they have in view—the establishment of a comprehensive system of secondary education for the borough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ENTER THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>ENTER THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The importance of the existence and the ambitions of the Grammar school interpose prominently at this point. It would seem that the Government Inspector, hearing that the governors of the Grammar School were contemplating the removal of their premises in School Brow, which admittedly is no longer suited to such an establish- ment, to a provisionally selected site in Latchford, was sufficiently practical minded to suggest that the local education , authority should be consulted with a view to a conference at which the question as a whole could be considered For reasons which the Governors of the school may con- sider sufficient but which the Education Committee and the public do not think are sufficient, the Grammar School authorities appear to treat somewhat lightly the claim of the committee to be consulted. True, they have entered into negotiation with a view to arranging a conference but they state—if Mr. Sawkins Burgess is their spokesman—that they have no suggestions to make as a preliminary to the conference and the 'inference is that they have no suggestions to make public at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>ALTERNATIVES.</head>
        <p>THE ALTERNATIVES.</p>
        <p>Well, the situation is this: The Grammar School has certain traditions which the governors are doubtless anxious to maintain. Those traditions have been pretty well dis- figured in the past if only by reason of a free institution having been placed at the disposal of a privileged few. On the side opposite these traditions the Education Com- mittee have the general good of the education of the Borough to see to, and they will be bound, in fulfilment of their trust, to see that privileges are not perpetuated to the detri- ment of the general good, at all events at the expense of the public. We sometimes hear the complaint from well-to-do quar- ters that the middle and upper classes pay for the education of the children of the poorer classes. Let us come to concrete facts. The children in the elementary schools in the borough c'ost, on the whole, less than n a head. At the Grammar School, attended by</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>LOCAL LADY'S SUFFRAGE EXPERIENCES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL LADY'S SUFFRAGE EXPERIENCES.</p>
        <p>Miss Waring Sees Lively Times in East Cambridge.</p>
        <p>A correspondent sends us.the following communique concerning the recent election in East Cambridgeshire: Miss Waring, 8.A., one of the most brilliant writers and organisers in or around Warrington, went down to represent the National Union of Woman Suffrage Societies at this election. The following is a r6'aumc; of the experiences she went through during the fort- night before polling day. It is of course, militancy which has let loose hooliganism—some people say "given rise" to it; but the hooligan element has been there all the time. The courage of Miss Waring and her fellows in facing such rowdyism is worthy of all admiration; and one does not wonder with such spirit in the leaders that, despite militancy the NatioLal Union numbers alone went up by 12,000 last year. The campaign began on May sth, with an open-air meeting. Most of the inhabitants turned out to hear the speakers, Mrs. Vulliarny and Mrs. Rootbam. Later there was an indoor meeting which ended in uproar. On the oth, rain interfered, but two indoor meetings were held. Next day a meeting at Isleham was attended by a howling mob and broken up. An election egg, lumps of earth and sticks were thrown into the car as it moved away. On the Bth,Mrs. Ramsey and Miss Waring addressed a meeting at Linton, where the small hall was so over- flowing that they had to hold a seconctineeting outside. Next day, by contrast, there was a lifeless meeting at Fulbourne, where people were timid to a degree. After it, the speakers (Mrs. Rackham, Mrs. Heitland, Mrs. Vulliamy and Aliss Waring) drove to Newmarket, where the meeting was anything but lifeless ! Dis- turbances were expected, the police were in force, and the Town Hall filled to overflowing. Mr. Vulliamy, with a contingent of Cambridge men, mostly under- graduates, of whom two were boxers, sat in the front seats to be ready in case of accidents. The meeting</p>
        <p>middle-class boys. there are 120 scholars. The town subsidizes the school from the rates</p>
        <p>to the extent of, and up co, 1:400 a year if there is a deficiency of that amount in any one year. The deficiency has not yet been so much as that, but last year the rates contributed over £2OO towards the education of those 120 boys. Nor is this all. The Government grants—paid of course, by the public purse— amounted to £377, in consideration of the school having been "recognised" as a secondary school. Nearly £6OO in aid of the education of 120 boys looks tolerably like £5 or £6 a head against the elementary school total of £3 a head. From endowments the school received £930, trust money left for the public good ; and the remaining income was made up of £929 paid by parents in fees. There can be no doubt,on these figures, that the Grammar School governors have no -case for holding aloof and abstaining from co-operating with the Committee for the best interests of the town. The days for exclu- siveness are gone by, at all events the daysl when the public may be expected to pay to support it. If the Grammar School governors wish to have their Grammar School to them- selves they may have it but, as Mr. Henry Roberts said, plainly and emphatically, the Education Committee have charge of the secondary education of the town and they will proceed With their work with the general interest in view. If the Grammar School comes into that scheme it will be welcomed. If it does not,it must be content to go on as a semi-private concern while the Education Committee proceed with their broad-minded, all-embracing scheme without consideration I—or grants in aid—for the Grammar School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>JUVENILE LABOUR BUREAU.</head>
        <p>JUVENILE LABOUR BUREAU.</p>
        <p>In a neighbouring column we have a special article on the proposed labour bureau for children leaving school, which will be placed before the Town Council for approval next Tuesday night. A similar proposal has been placed before the Council and rejected twice on previous occasions but we venture to think that the wisdom of adopting the completed scheme will appeal irresistibly on this occasion to the majority of the Council and that an effective means of placing boys and girls in touch with suitable employment will soon be at the disposal of those concerned. At a cost to the town which is negligible— E5O a year, to be exact—parents will have the opportunity of consulting those who are best able to give the advice as to the capabilities of their children for the various walks in life that lie open to them. We believe there is an objection,in some quarters, to the cost but such an objection can surely carry no weight. It is urged, too, that ther will be an infringement of that gloriously undefined thing, parental responsibility. We have yet to find a mother or father who would call assistance to find employment for a budding man or woman interference with parental prerogatives. The alternative is that the Board of Trade will set up an advisory committee worked from London. How London is expected to know our local industrial needs or how anyone in a pro- gressive community can sanely suggest that we should put our own affairs into alien hands it is difficult to comprehend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FAREWELL.</head>
        <p>A FAREWELL.</p>
        <p>If you and I were sweethearts In some white-blossom'd Spring, And through these low green barley -fields Soft winds were whispering; Should we for any curfew, Bird-song, or flaming west, Quit our sweet tryst among the corn,</p>
        <p>Your head upon my breast, And in our hands Love's golden rune,</p>
        <p>Its mystery half confessed? The ruddy sun would languish, The wood-birds cease their trill, The night-mouse go her ghostly quest, We should be heedless still— If o'er these low green barley-fields Soft winds were whispering, And you and I were sweethearts In that white-blossom'd Spring.</p>
        <p>* * * * The wind blows where the barley Once rustled in the breeze, The white frost-flowers are in the fields, The snow-blooms on the trees; The wood-bird's note is silent, And in a bloodless sky Past the cold moon's averted face Dark clouds drift drearily; No sunset flame for curfew Tells out the fair day fled, Nor need we these frost-flowers to show Our summer rose is dead. Your head is bent beside me With all its wonted grace, And framed in dusk I feel unseen</p>
        <p>The white flower of your face; And silence falls between us two, So garrulous of late, And you are trembling, even as I— * * *</p>
        <p>Why, here we've reached your gate. Hark ! is that striking ten? It is . . .</p>
        <p>How ghostly still and white The meadows lie beneath the moon . . . How strange to dream of barley-fields . . And we are only friends you know. Dear friend—Sweetheart—Good-night! ROSA E: GLADDING.</p>
        <p>was held despite the accompaniment of a concertina, songs, squibs that resounded like pistol shots, and witlicisms of a wide variety. Difficulty arose on leaving the hall,•however. The crowd was in high spirits, and had to be restrained by police and under- graduates. The car would not go ; water had been poured into the petrol tank, and literature, tea and cups carried off from it. The speakers remained for some time at a hotel, then at another, finally leaving by a back way in another car. On May Nth, a meeting at Soham, at which disturb- ances were expected, passed off as quietly as a religicus gathering! Thus all sorts of weather was encountered, metaphorically speaking; but fair or foul, the meetings were held every night, sometimes to the number of two or three, throughout the fortnight. Miss Waring issued a leaflet in which she pointed out that each candidate was holding out to the electors prospects of better wages if he was returned to Parliament. "In the face of these election addresses, it is impossible for anti-suffragists to main- tain that votes do not affect wages." The National fTnion may well be proud of such organisers as Miss Waring, and of the work being at present accom- plished despite the troublesome element to which recent militancy has given rise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>" BOISTEROUS " TACTICS CONDEMNED.</head>
        <p>" BOISTEROUS " TACTICS CONDEMNED.</p>
        <p>In regard to this, your readers may hear with interest that the German " Bund " for Woman Suffrage says that the revolutionary doings of that small section of the Woman Suffrage movement in England have been discussed and condemned throughout Germany. Their German movement has proved the great difference between quiet and patient work and the boisterous tactics of militancy ; yet as every fresh act of violence on the part of the militants is used against them they are proclaiming everywhere that "any application of force stands in direct contradic- tion to the views and aims of our movement, and it can neither be justified by conviction nor by self- sacrifice." So also say the hundreds of thousands of women in the law-abiding suffrage societies of England, to whom the militants are in a proportion perhaps of one per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>'4OHN BRIGHT.</head>
        <p>'4OHN BRIGHT.</p>
        <p>Story of School Days at Penketh told in Biography.</p>
        <p>GKEAT ENGLISHMAN'S CAREER.</p>
        <p>The Life of John Bright has been a long time in making its appearance, but Mr. Trevelyan has pro- duced a work, published this week by Messrs. ,Constable &amp; Co. that covers the ground completely,and, like several other stately biographies that have been published in late years —Tennyson's, Gladstone's and Cobden's—leaves no room for unauthorised writers to dabble with the same subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>AT PENKETEI FRIENDS' SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>AT PENKETEI FRIENDS' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>As a child John Bright was, according to his mother, volatile, pliable and timid because delicate. At eight he attended a school in Rocbdale,but between the ages of ten and fifteen he spent a series of years at Friends' Schools in Lancashire and Cheshire. "The first of these was at Penketh,near Warrington, which he began to attend in 1820, boarding with some of his fellows, boys and girls, in the hospitable farm of same Quakers named Davies. ' Behind the house,' he writes, 'was a good garden and orchard, and a vinery where grays were abundant and beyond the garien were cornfields through which we walked daily to the school. We had Scripture reading in the family and I remember howl found a place for same of the New Testament narrative. The vineyard mentioned in the 13th chapter of Luke, I pictured as just like our vineyard ; and I fancied I could see the discon- tented brother of the Prodigal Son returning from the field down the short lane which led from the house to the neighbouring cornfield. These imaginings of my boyhood have remained with me ever since. Our schoolmaster was not well qualified for his office. His temper was not good, and the school was much less pleasant than our home with the kind and generous farmer.' Comparing the schools of our day to those of the boyhood of Dickens, Cobden and _ _ _</p>
        <p>Bright, we may boast that the masters and the food ate certainly better. If the breed of scholars had proportionately improved we should be doing well." At the last school Bright attended, at Newton-in- Bowland, he ceased to be " the timid and docile boy," and became the heart of oak we know, the bracing winds of the Pennine moorlands having wrought a lasting improvement in his bodily condition which had a corresponding effect upon his spiritual equipment. COULD BE NO OTHER THAN LIBERAL.</p>
        <p>The times and conditions in which the Brights lived made the doctrine of " Liberty, equality and fraternity" part of their inherent and inured religion, and we learn from John himself: In fact I could not be otherwise than Liberal. My father was Liberal, though we had no elections in our town and there was rarely a contest for the county." His feelings in those early days may be gauged in some measure by the fact that as an old men, he recalled the emotion he felt at a Reform Bill meeting at Rochdale attended by himself and thousands more, "ripe for rebellion," when Dr. Kay, an orator of the day, rolled out the magnificent and prophetic lines of Shelley : Rise like lions after slumber In unvanquishable number! S-ake your chains to earth, like dew Which in sleep had fAll'n on you Ye are many—they are few. His resentment at the c9ndition of things in the bad old days of Protection is well summarised in an entry in his diary in P-40 on the day of the Queen's mrrriage : "This day the Queen is married. In Rochdale there is no appearance of rejoicing and how or why should there be ? The people are dts3ontented, out of employ, short of food. Let the Government repeal the Corn Law, cease to plunder the mass for the aggran- disement of the aristocracy and give them education in spite of the Church,and then they may reasonably be asked to feel and to show some pleasure at a royal</p>
        <p>marriage." It sounds almost like a message from Irishmen smarting under the sense of long ages of injustice and oppresion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>BRIGHT'S LIFE WORK.</head>
        <p>BRIGHT'S LIFE WORK.</p>
        <p>"Save for his friend, Cobden, he would afford the unique instance in our history of a member of Parlia- ment in no connection with any official party, exercis- ing an immense influence on the thoughts and hearts of his fellow-countrymen. That personal influence covered the whole range of political action and touched on all the main topics of the day; but the chief incidents of his story are the Corn Law agitation, the Crimean war, the American Civil war, and the winning of the franchise for the workingmen. In the first of these controversies Bright served under Cobden as his chief lieutenant; during the Crimea he fought at his side as his equal; but the American and the Franchise questions were Bright's own. At length, after °Palmerston's death in ISOS, Gladstone in three eventful years recon.tituted the Liberal oarty, no longer as a Whig party, but as a party of pr,sgress and democracy, sworn to carry 13right's principles into effect and in the first instance to enfranchise the working-classes. Then there followed in rapid succession the Franchise Act of 1567, which Disraeli indeed introduced, but which Bright and Gladstone compelled him to make effective; the disestablishment of the Irish Church ; the Irish Land Act; the Ballot Act; and a host of other reforms in that great period of Liberal fruition which Bright had prepared by thirty years of guerilla war- fare, carried on from the public platform and the benches below the gangway in defiance alike of Whigs and Tories."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>CONFLICT OF IDEALS.</head>
        <p>CONFLICT OF IDEALS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bright belonged to a family of the Society of Friends, but he was impelled by the conditions of his times to take part in politics. "It was a conflict," says Mr. Trevelyan, " between two religious ideals— on the one hand the quietist spirit of the older Friends who sought seclusion from the evil world, and on the other the new type of Quakerism that thought it a religious duty to mix actively with the world and to win it over to the principles of neace, humanity and justice." We know the path Mr. Bright chose for himself. When in 1808 he was asked to join the Ca binet by Gladstone, who "thought he had a claim upon me which I could not reject," Bright asked for time, spent a sleepless miserable time et the thought of entering office, consented against his own judgment, but made the famous statement that he wished to dwell among his own people—which he did till the day he died in his old home at Rochdale. FREE TRADE BEGINNINGS.</p>
        <p>When Bright lost his wife, "Mr. Cobden," he writes, "called upon me as his friend and addressed me, as 37 m might suppose, with words of condolence. After a time he looked up 'and said, There are thousands of homes in England at this moment where wives, mothers, and children are dying of hunger. Now,' he said, when the first paroxysm of your grief is passed, I would advise, you to come with me, and we will never rest till the Corn Law is repealed.' I accepted his invitation. I felt in my conscience that there was work which some- body must do, arid from that time we never ceased to labour hard on behalf of the resolution which we had made."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>HOME RULE BREACH.</head>
        <p>THE HOME RULE BREACH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bright decided to vote against Mr. (Iladstone's Home Rule Bill, but wrote to the other dissentients that they might be content with abstaining. They followed his lead rather than his advice. Writing to Mr. Chamberlain on June Ist, 1880, John Bright said :</p>
        <p>" I was surprised when Mr. Caine told me last night of your deci,;ion, and that my props3d vote had much influenced it; for my note was intended to make it more easy for you and your friends to abstain from voting in the coming division. If I had thought I should do harm I should have said something more or less. Even now, if it is not too late, I could join you in abstaining, if we could save the House and the country from a dissolution which may, for the Liberal party, turn out a cltastrophe, the magnitude of which cannot be measured."</p>
        <p>It was, however, now too late to retrieve the situation and on June 7th the first Home Rule Bill was defeated by a majority of 30, the Ayes being 311 and the Noes :111.</p>
        <p>When John Bright was dying in ISSS his son wrote to Mr. Gladstone :</p>
        <p>"My father is sinking and the end cannot be far off. He sent for me last night when he became aware of his condition . . . and he wished me to write to you and tell you that he could not forget your unvary- ing kindness to him and the many services you have rendered the country'. He was very weak and did not seem able to say any more and I saw the tears running down his cheeks."</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone replied that he was " Deeply touched and moved that I have been even for a moment in your father's thoughts at this solemn hour. I can assure you that he has been littls absent of late from mine, that my feelings towards him are entirely unaltered by any of the occurrences of the (Continued in next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
