        <p>Sanscrit Scholarship. Mr. Donald Wadding, BA, Scholar of Brasenose College, Oxford, has been awarded the Boden University) Sanscrit Scholarship. Mr. D maid Gladding is the E. on cf Rev,. Thomas Wadding, of Macclesfield, formerly of Padgate.</p>
        <p>Sanitary Inspector's Success. We are pleased to 'note that the name of Mr. Benjamin Knowles, assistant sanitary inspector of Warrington, appears in the list of successful candidates in the examination held under the auspices of the Liverpool University on the ,20th and 22nd of May, in the subject of meat and food inspection. Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. At the meeting of the Education Committee on Monday, the Chairman (Councillor 11. Roberts) extended a hearty welcome to Mr. C. Jackson Holmes on his return tr) the committee as a co-npted member. Mr. R. T. Fairclough also expressed satisfaction that Mr. Holmes had been appointed a member of the Sites and Buildings Committee and New Schools Visiting</p>
        <p>Committee. Baptists' Sale of Work. A sale of wErk is to be held on Thursday next in the new schoolroom attached to Golborne-sti set Baptist Church. Mr. W. lloberts has kindly consented to open the sale, and an old friend of Golborne-street, (Mr. Norman 13a1inford) will preside. The ladies have been working for several months and a very useful assortment of articles will be on sale. In the basement there will be a fair fancy and otherwise, and here the young men, for the nonce, will be showmen. The effort is to wipe out the debt that has been incurred in the new building.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. J Morton to Emigrate. Mr. J. A. J. Morton, who has resided in Warrington for periods of eight and sixteen years with a break between, during which he was engaged in the Forest of Dean, has decided to leave England with his three sons for Victoria, Vancouver Island. Mr. Morton, who is well known to " Examiner " readers as a corre- spondent who writes well and with authority on most social subjects, sails next week. He was secretaty of the Progressive Labour League. His many friends, while wi.3hing him every success in the future, will regret his depaxture front their midst. As, however, Mr. Morton has kindly undertaken to let us hear from him from time to time, they will not altogether lose sight of him.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Walkers' New Bowling Green. The bowling green provided for the employes of Mess's. Peter Walker and Son, Ltd., was opened on Saturday. Mr. It Hutton, the Lead brewer, performed the ceremony, in the pi esence of Mr. J. Fulton Smith, Major A. F. G. Potter, Messrs. E. Parry, J. J. Dunn, W. Mercer; Ivan Fulton Smith, J. Baxter, and a large number of employes. Before opening the green, Mr. Hutton spoke in very complimentary terms of the directors, to whom, he said, the best thanks were due for the howling green. They took a great interest in all games of sport in which the men in their employ were interested. Mr. Hutton also gave some very good advice as to the usage of the green, and asked the members to play the game in a sportsmanlike manner. At the close of his speech, Mr. Hutton threw the first wood, followed by one from Mr. Parry and Mr. Dunn, after whicta the members made a raid on the bowl- house, and the green soon presented an animated appearance.</p>
        <p>Crostields' Recreation Club. To celebrate the winning of the Works Association Football Competition for the second year in succession, about 100 members and friends sat down to dinner at the King's Cafe last Friday night. After dinner a concert was organised by the cpmmittee, presided over by Mr. T. Wilkinson, who proposed the health of "The King." A most enjoyable programme of songs, sentimental and humorous, was gone through, the artistes being Messrs. Dickenson, Fell, Podmore, Sedgeley, Ashcroft and Harrison. Messrs. Brobbin and Taylor demonstrated their skill as instrumentalists. During the evening Captain Layton, the works manager, presented the medals to the players, after- wards giving the audience some very amusing experi- ences encountered whilst travelling in China and Siberia. The party showed their appreciation by singing "He's a jolly good fellow " with real Lancashire gusto. Mr. Osborne proposed the health ef the firm, and eulogised them for their interest towards their employes in all their recreative pursuits. Messrs. R. Lee and IVolfenden were efficient accom- panists.</p>
        <p>DON'T LOOK OLD—Keep the ravages of time at bay by darkening the grey streaks with LOCKYER'S SULPHU R HAIR RE STORE 11,which deepensto a naturalcolouiin afew days.Lockyer's maintains a youthful appearance:l.:age bot s ,eYerywhere. Post free 1/6. Bedford Laboratory, London.S.H</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 31, 1913,</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 31, 1913,</p>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The Idol of Paris."</p>
        <p>This play from the pen of Charles Darrell, deals / with the aristocratic and Bohemian life in the gay city in an effective and interesting manner. The unorthodox nature of the plot readily stimulates interest and the appeal of the heroine for the sympathies of the audience is novel, though rather far-fetched. In the first place we are in the dark as to the capricious Flare Flare's past. It would certainly conduce to a better understanding of her character if we knew that her first foolish action was committed to get the money to pay off her lover's debt to the Nihilist Society. But still the play, as such, is fairly successful, and if one may criticise the plot, the same criticism cannot be mane in regard to the artistes who play their difficult parts exceedingly well. Miss Annie Bell puts plenty of dash and vigour into the part of Flare Flare, a passionate variety star who is "Idol of Paris." She at once captivates the audience with her realistic acting and fascinating stage presence. Mr. Charles Kean as Phillipe D'Esterreplays the part of the unscrupulous villain with success, being a really clever actor, who is a past master in the art of facial expression. Mr. Frederick Carr knows how to appeal to the audience and, as Victor Cashelle, he has ample scope for his unquestion- able talent. Mr. Gordon Robey is well placed as the Prince Zoresty, whilst Mr. Alfred G. Raymond keeps the audience in good humour in the role of Tatave, the presumptive schoolboy. Miss Edith Evans is a capital Puritanical Madame Sancterre, who apparently approves of nothing except gifts for cannibals. Miss Kitty Dillon, as the demure Quaker girl Ann, also aids in the humorous interludes. The other characters are in the capable hands of Miss P. Anderson, Mr. T. H. Parker, Mr. Alf Harvey and Mr. F. Whilby. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>The attraction next week will be a drama entitled A blind girl's love," a story of the Alps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>One of the most popular turns at the Hippodrome this week is that supplied by Haydee's miniature vaudeville theatre. It is one of the finest marionette performances we have ever seen, and the setting of the show is most elaborate. The dolls mimic the action of Little Tich in dancing speciality, and Chung Ling Soo, the Chinese magician. The latter is exceedingly well carried out, and two of the dolls dance the Turkey Trot in a very realistic manner. The Keeley Bros. are clever ball punchers, and have a good turn of genuine wholesome comedy which keeps the audience rocking with laughter. " A Bit o' Bother " is the title of a hiizhly diverting sketch presented by Wright, Aitken and Co. The bother is caused by two sons who marry without their prents' consent. There is plenty of laughter when the two daughters-in-law meet their husbands' father. Rosa Crouch and George Welch give an eccentric dancing turn, which is quite out of the ordinary. Welsh is also a clever knockabout comedian, and greatly pleases the audience with his eccentricities. The Rawsons are pleasing entertainers who present a refined vocal harmonising act. Their songs are chosen with taste and rendered in a capital manner. Ruby Maybutt is a dainty comedienne and also a smart buck and sand dancer. Peter and Potts are a couple of burlesque comedians who score a decided success in The comedian and the call boy." NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Next week Fred Karno, whcss name is synonymous with fun and frolic, will present a new version of the " Mumming Birds." This attraction will be a capital one and other turns to appear will be :—Albert Le Fre,comedian ; Tom Gott, Yorkshire dialect comedian; Marjorie Dawson, the crinoline girl ; Al. Lawson and Frances Namon, comedy cycling and ball punching ; Sados, novelty juggler,assisted by a little comedy (ian) Little Bella and Jules Terry, juvenile performers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>THEATRE ROYAL.</head>
        <p>THEATRE ROYAL.</p>
        <p>"The Way of the World."</p>
        <p>The newly-organized and augmented Repertory Co. have achieved a decided success this week in the great human drama, " The way of the world." Mr. Patrick Branigan plays the leading part of Cecil Harding with every confidence. Mr. L. Yorke gives a clever character study as Matthew Malyon, the circus pro- prietor; Mr. C.A. Chandler made an unscrupulous Capt. Fred Slade ; Miss Phyllis Morton is a charming Kate Montague and earns the hearty applause of the audience when she rides the horse to victory in place of the jockey who has been drugged. Miss Hazel Head is Slade's able foil as Lady Adele Harding and earns her quota of " hisses." The eranedv is supplied by Mr. Arthur Len Frere and Miss Cissie Cleveland, the latter being well received in her "To the Bodega " number. Miss Nellie Masterman is a capital Eric Malyon ; Mr. George Perry is a clever comedian and Mr. Sydney Ellis maintains his popular- ity as Sir George Harding. The scenic effects are well can ied out, particularly the circus and racecourse scenes. NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>Patrons will have the opportunity of seeing "A man La a thousand " presented by the :` 'Royal " company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>PICTUREDROME.</head>
        <p>THE PICTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>Next week the great attraction at all the perform- ances will be "The Irocmaster " (adapted from the celebrated drama by George Ohneo.in which, for the first time, the great actress,sladame Jane Hading.plays before the camera. All the other pictures will be changed is usual on Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>ROYAL VISIT.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT.</p>
        <p>LORD DERBY APPROVES LIST OF</p>
        <p>PRESENTATIONS.</p>
        <p>We understand that Lord Derby has approved the list of ladies and gentlemen submitted by the Recep- tion committee for presentation to His Majesty the King on the occasion of his visit to Warrington on July 7th. The names have already been published in the "Examiner." The general Purposes committc-e has decided to make an order closing the whole of the route of the Royal ptocession to traffic at 1 p.m., and to close and barri- cade such streets as the Chief Constable may think necessary for the safe pissage of his Majesty. Bank Park will 1 e closed to all persons other than those holding tickets of admissic,u.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>REVIEW OF LINERS.</head>
        <p>REVIEW OF LINERS.</p>
        <p>Imposing Pageant at Liverpool.</p>
        <p>The Liverpool Steamship Owners' Association on Thurs -lay issued p trticulars of the arrangements made for providing a great display of Liverpool line's, and other vessel,.;, which will be inspected in the Mersey by the Kiug and Queen on the occasion of their visit to the port in July. The Association formed a com- mittee at the request of Lord Derby, and the committee sent out invitations to all shipowners in Liverpool to send vessels to take part in the review. A large response has been received, although there ai e many firms who from unavoidable causes are unable to enter vessels for the purpose. Many of the biggest passenger boats plying on all the important routes ‘vill, however, be included. A:together there are twenty-six big ocean-going steamers included, and in addition there will be a large number of miscellaneous smaller craft and steam and sailing yachts. The steamers alone will form an impressive line five miles in length. Each boat will be anchored 1,200 feet from the others, with the exception of the Mauretania, which will require a distance of 1,500 feet to swing in. Their Majesties will embark on the Dock Board's yacht Galatea, and will steam round the line of vessels, proceeding first to the southward and passing round the extreme end of the line of vessels, and then, turning northward, their Majesties will inspect the Mauretania. All the ships will be decorated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>PREMIER CYCLES. Excelsior Cycles, Lea Francis Cycles, £4 10/-to £l6 16/-.—Sole Agent, J.S. GARLAND 51 to 55, Bridge et., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>NEW CHAPEL AND SUNDAY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>NEW CHAPEL AND SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Forward Move By the Grappenhall Independent</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Methodists.</head>
        <p>Methodists.</p>
        <p>FOUNDATION</p>
        <p>Saturday was an epoch-making day in the history of rappenhall Independent Methodists, for it witnessed the laying of the foundation stones of the new chapel and Sunday school. The present church was built in 1880 on the Chester-road, Grappenhall, and some nine years ago it was evident that the cause in this neighbourhood was not very flourishing. In order to resuscitate it, Councillor R. Henshall and Mr. A. Jolley were sent from the Friars Green Church. Along with the few members of the church they worked 15trenu, usly, and their efforts met with such abundant success, that three years ago the church deemed it necessary to raise funds for the erection of a larger building. The members offered fr:2so within three years time, and also promoted various small efforts, the result being that at the present time they have raised £4OO. Toe contributions on Slturday amounted to .£221. The new church, which will cost fr:2,300 is being built by Mr. H. Fairclough, to the plans of Mr. S. P. Silcock. Seating accommodation will be provided for 250 both in chapel and school, with the additions of vestries, classrooms, &amp;c. DESCRIPTION OF THE BUILDING.</p>
        <p>The building will stand upon a corner site facing Knutsford-road, with Barton-avenue down one olde, and will include a chuich, with transepts and Com- munion, a large assembly hall, four class-rooms, minister's and choir vestries, and suitable lavatory accommodation. The building will be bced with Yorkshire stone pal-points and red s3nd stone dressings, and the roof covered with Velenhelli slates. The church roof will be open-timbered and the inside joiners' work throughout, including pews, will be finished in pitchpine for staining and varnishing. The building will be heated by hot water on the low</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>TONE LAYING.</head>
        <p>TONE LAYING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith's remar.r.s,his Worship said that the assembly he presided over was not held in quite RS healthy an atmosphere as that one. (Hear, hear.) Continuing, the Mayor said he was glad to see that religious strife, rivalry and p-rsecution had passed away long ago. Legislation hod done a great neal towards improving the state of things, but education, religious practices end the broadening of minds at the present time had done even more than legislation to remove that spirit of intolerance and persecution which existed in the past—which the stronger sects had exercised over the weaker ones. That was one form of strife, but there was another kind which existed even to this day and that was the enmity which might exist, but which was getting rarer, even in congrega- tions. He could hardly imagine that anything could be more against the red piety of religious worship. There ought to be true unity arid love existing amongst congregations, all sup- Heating to one great Father for benefits and blessings. He did not thick the spirt of enmity existed in that congregation. He would he glad if he thought other congregations were as happy and united as the one at Grappenhall, hut he was afr-,id he was expecting too much in this world of imperfections. PROSPERITY THE PARENT OF APATHY.</p>
        <p>Continuing, he said that proverity was certainly the parent of apathy. It was the same with the individual as with the history of countries. Tne individual who was struggling for advancement fought as a man fight- ing against, the wall, and he was generally successtul, but when he gained his point he became indolent, care- less, and neglectful, ever trading on his past reputation. Countries which were struggling for their nationality were also very careful as to their policy—not only their home policy but their relations with other countries. When they once gained their points and got territory after territory they were apt to become careless. He would venture to say the same</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Photograhph, by Burrell. MRS. W. J. WALKER DECLARING A STONE TO BE WELL AND TRULY</head>
        <p>Photograhph, by Burrell. MRS. W. J. WALKER DECLARING A STONE TO BE WELL AND TRULY</p>
        <p>pressure principle, with pipes and radiators, and ample inlet and outlet ventilation will be provided. The trustees have not been sparing in the land, and the,surrcunding grounds will be properly laid out and planted with trees. It is anticipated that the build- ing will be completed and ready for occcupation some time towards the end of the year.</p>
        <p>LAID.</p>
        <p>thing in regard to religious bodies. In olden times they had to fight against opposition and adverse circuni- stances. Tney were even more strenuous in their religious work than they were at the present time. But in that enterprise there was ample proof that the Independent Methodists of that district were still persistent in their efforts for further advancement. In spite of the lull liberty which existed to-day, they were not lethargic or apathetic. There was nothing more damaging to any cause than a feeling of apathy and there was nothing so slothful as the spirit of indifference. (Applause.) PRESENTATION BY THE 'MAYORESS. - •</p>
        <p>_ In presenting Mrs. Smith with a silver trowel, the Iklayoresi (Mrs. A. Nield) said her duty was a light but congenial one. Whilst she had not had the privilege of meeting Mrs. Smith lefore that day, she felt sure that during her term of office as Mayoress of Colne she performed her arduoui duties well, and her selection for the honour of laying a foundation stone of that building wis well merited. (Hear, hear.) Mrs. Smith then laid the stone, in the hopes that it would be a foundation stone for the Church of Christ, and she hoped that when the church was filli6hed it would do its duty as the members wished it to do.</p>
