      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>CEREMONY.</head>
        <p>THE CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>There was a large company at the stone-laying ceremony on Saturday, and the proceedings were crowned with success. Under the stones laid by the Mayor (Councillor Dr, Joseph) and Mrs. J. Smith (ex-mayoress of Colne), glass bottles hermetically sealed were placed containing • copies of the " Examiner," the " Guardian," the names of trustees and officials of the church and school, copy of the Independent Methodist plan of ministerial appoint- ments for Warrington circuit, the Connexioual Magazine and cards of the Mayor and Councillor R. Mr. R. B. Woods (Connexionsl angelist) presided, and he was supoorted by the Mayor and Mayoress of of Warrington (Councillor Dr. Joseph and Mrs. Arnold Nield), Mr. and Mrs. John Smith (ex-ruriyor and ex-mayoress of Coins), Alderman James and Mrs. Evans, Councillor James Shaw, Mr. W. Price (president of Connexion), Mr. Robert Shakeshaft, Mr. James Astlev, Chester ; Mrs. W. J. Walker, Grappenhall ; Mr. W. H. Bradford, Granpenhall ; Mrs. 'l'. Critchley, Mrs. W. Inc., Leigh; lr. Arthur Jolley, Mrs. .1. H. Holt, Lymm ; Councillor Beesley, J.P., Mrs. T. Hamblet, Ridgate ; Mr. F. L. Garside, Mr Edwards, sirs. Hickman, Mr. George Leah, Mrs. R. Nelson, Latchford Without; Mr. Thomas Alhinson, Mrs. R. Henshall, Latchford ; Mr. James Hudson, Mr. Thomas Critchley, Latchford Without ; Mrs. George I.3lenie Councillor Heashall, Latchford; Mr. E. Howard, kiss Annie Shs.kestraft and members of the church. VILLAGES NOT NEGLECTED.</p>
        <p>At the outset, the Chairman said they were full of thanksgiving for the men and women who had worked so hard for the cause. They yore glad to be associated with a church which stood very largely for democratic principles. Speaking in regard to tee villages, he said tbey had not neglected them. In the villages of Cheshire they had 12 Established Churches, while in the towns of Cheshire they had only two. That showed that they had paid a large amount of attention to the village life, which they believed to be esential for the common good. (Applause.) They were also greatly encouraged by the grand character built up by the splendid gospel and teachings given in those village societies. Tee man who had been sent forth from the village churches had been n power for good. They were glad to see the desire to extend the facilities for , the work in the Sunday schools, especially for that aide of the work which dealt with the grading of their scholars. They were also glad that it had been imposed upon them to build more convenient premises for public worship. Some people thought they were going too far, but he would like to remind them that they were men and women of venturesome spirit and they had faith in humanity just as they had faith in God. He was sure the Mayer would feel at home with them, because thee was a large amount of fellow feeling. His Wor2.iiip presided over what was called a democratic assembly, and he had experiences in that assembly which manifested themselves in a lust for tale. I(Laushter.) Methodists also found in their respective church lives that they had a difficulty in bringing into surijection that unruly member—the tongue. (Applause.) PRESENTATION OF TROWEL. Mr. J. Smith (ex-mayor of Comae), in presenting the liayor with a silver trowel suitably inscribed,on tiehalf of the trustees of Grappenhall Church, said he knew of the Mayor's manifold duties which took up every moment of his time. He (the speaker) was present that day to endeavour to wipe off a debt which he owed to Councillor Henshall. Independent Methodists were hard to satisfy, for they always wanted more, but they always made the best of what little they could get. The people in Grappenhall would appreciate fully the efforts which were done for them. (Applause.) THE MAYOR AND RELIGIOUS TOLERANCE. In declaring the stone to be well and truly laid to the glory of God and the furtherance of that Church, the Mayor said be was greatly honoured in having to perform that function. He had been associated for many years with numerons members of that eenomina- tion and he had probably been thrown into personal contact more with Independent Methodists in that dis- trict than with members of any other denomination. He could see many old familiar faces present that day, but his recolle-Aions were tinged with sadness when he was reminded of those who had passed away. Referring to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>OTHER CEREMONIES.</head>
        <p>OTHER CEREMONIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Shitkeshaft presented Mr. James Astley with a silver trowel,and Mr. Astley, in laying the stone, said it was in memory of a sainted motner and sister who did n noble work for that church in the early days. Stones were also laid by Mrs. W. J. Walker, Mr. W. H. Bradford, Mrs. Walter Ince, Mrs. J. H. Holt, Mrs. T. Hambiett, Mrs. llickman, Miss Hartley, Mrs. R. Nelson, Mr. Ethsards, Mrs. F. H. Jolley, Mrs. t. Henshall, Mr. Thomas Critchley, Councillor H. Henshall and Mr. E. Hayward, who were the recipients of silver trowels at the hands of the Chairman, Mrs. 'l'. Critchley, Mr. A. Jolley, Councillor Beesley, J.P., Mr. F. L. Garside, Mr, George Leah, Mr. A. Mounfield, Mrs. Grreenhalgh, Mr. Thomas Albinson, Mr. James Hudson, Mr. George Blease and Miss Annie Shakeshaft.</p>
        <p>THE ONLY FREE CHURCH IN THE DISTRICT. Mr. Bradford said that although they were retaining the name of Grappenhall for the churcn, they were not actually in the parish of Grappenhall. Stilt it was the only Free Church in the neighhourhoud, and he wished them every success. Councillor Beesley (chairman of Warrington Circuit) well remembered the amount of trouble the Warrington circuit took in order to make an effort to redeem the cause. lu the efforts that day, teey had abundant proof of the labour, energy and interest ulaich must have been manifested in connection with the working of that church. He attributed it very largely to the self- sacrifice and loving service Councillor Heushall and Mr. Jolley had rendered, that they had been so successful. The Chairman said the Mayor had just reminded him that they were making history that day in Latchford Without. They were an awfully heathenish lot for they had no church, chapel or mission hall, and, thank God, no public house. (Hear, hear.) Now they were to have at least a Free Church in the neighbourhood, and be was quite sure they were going to improve the character of the district. (Applause.) Ceuncillor Henshall said it was a great day in the history of the church. At an interval in the proceedings, the Mayoress received a beautiful bouquet at the hands of Miss Dorothy Holt. Alderman James Evans proposed a hearty vote of thanks to all who had helped in that day's ceremony. He said the Mayor was keenly interested in everything that tended to improve the social, moral and religious well-being of the people. Councillor Shaw seconded, and said he prayed that the higher purposes of the building might be fulfilled. The proposition was heartily carried. An excellent tea was partaken of in the large marquee and afterwards smeller stones were laid by teachers. scholars and friends, Councillor R. Henshall presiding. Stones were laid by one of the youngest members of the congregation and also by the eldest. Others taking part in the ceremonies were: F. Scarisbrick, H. Henshall, W. Shakeshaft, A. Foden, A. Shakeshaft, A. Garside. S. Barton, Edward Barton, E. Howard, R. Haves, N. Foden, W. Jones, D. Stanley, A. Woodcock, T. -Leah, F. Woodcock, Robert Wilson, William Barton, Mr. Hamblett, H. Harding, W. A. Woodcock, T. Nilson, J. Sanderson, J. Hudson, jun., F. H. Jolley, T. Hamblett, B. Warourton, W. Fairhurst, Mrs. F. L, Garside, Mrs. R. Bennett, Mrs. Robinson, Mr. .1. Smith. Misses Eva Terman, Dorothy Holt, Annie Leah, Hilda Jolley. Florence Barton, Hilda Birch, Mabel Critchley, May Gough, Mary Blease, Blanch Lewis, Elsie Holt, Nora Prince, Dorothy Hartley. Mesdames William Barton, Stanley, Harding, Birch, Woodcock, Warburton, Hamblett, L. Dale, Miss Maggie Meese, Mrs. Howard, Miss Baron. Mrs. J. Hudson, Mrs. Woodward, Mrs. W. Barton and Miss Maggie Fairhurst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>LA-ROLA FOR THE COMPLEXION.</head>
        <p>LA-ROLA FOR THE COMPLEXION.</p>
        <p>The Holiday Season is now with us and those who wish to preserve their skins from the rays of the sun or contact with sea-water, &amp;c., will have to use a reliable emollient. For this purpcse, and also for building up the skin tissues and beautifying the com- plexion there is nothing equal to" La-rola," the fame of which is world-wide. A little rubbed on the hands will also be found distinctly beneficial, as a few applications will leave them beautifully ~oft and white Messrs. Beetbam offer to forward a sample box con- taining La-lola, a tube of La rola tooth paste, aromatic, antiseptic, with delightful cleansing properties, a tablet of delicately perfumed toilet soap and a packet of La-rola: toilet Powder and rose bloom, to any of our readers sending them 3d. to cover cost of packing and postage. Write Messrs. M. Beetham and Son, Cheltenham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
