      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Round the World in Two Hours : A Notable</head>
        <p>Round the World in Two Hours : A Notable</p>
        <p>Picture Achievement. On the 9th of June a picture which is something out of the ordinary will be submitted for public patronage at the Picturedrome and will be shown for a fortnight—it is confidently expected—to crowded and delighted audiences. It is no less a feat than a journey round the world in two hours The journey starts at the very door of the Picturedrome, goes by way of Sankey-street to the Central Station, is con- tinued round the Globe and ends up, via Crewe and Bank Quay Station, at the starting point, the Picturedrome door. The pictures will be explained by a specially engaged lecturer Mr. Rickard Saker. Visitors to the Picturedrome will be able to witness scenes in and realise the habits of the people of the United States, Canada, New Zealand, Australasia, China, Japan, India, Africa, Turkey, the Balkan States, Italy, Spain, Switzerland, Austria, Russia, Norway, Sweden, Germany, France, &amp;c. "If this class of entertainment, says the Southport Guardian,' is continued, and there is no reason why it should not be so, the Picture Theatres of the present and future will not have worked in vain, and par- ticularly in Southport, as the directors of the Picture- drome have been the Ii rat to start what all the greatest educationists of the world are agreed upon, namely, instruction by aid of the cinema." Cemetery Hotel Bowling Club. Mr. and Mrs. Allison, the popular host and hostess of the Cemetery hotel, were on Thursday evening the recipients of a presentation from the members of the Cemetery Hotel Bowling Club, on the occasion of their retirement. The gift took the form of a handsome chiming timepiece. Mr. J. Willett, who presided over a large gathering in the billiard room, made the presentation and spoke of the assist- ance which the club had invariably received from Mr. and Mrs. Allison.—Mr. T. Bolton, one of the oldest members, also eulogised Mr. Allison's interest in the club.—Mr. Allison, in responding,said that during the 21 years he had been associated with the Cemetery Hotel the club had been successful in competition matches, and he attributed that success to a great extent to the kindly feeling which had always existed, between the members of the club,officials and himself. He wished the club every success in the future, and hoped both team, might win the County Champion- ships, as they did in 1897,and which performance had never been equalled in the annals of those competi- tions. (Applause.) Refreshments were then partaken of, and songs were given by several members of the club. -At another social gathering, Mr. T. Burke, on behalf of a section of the customers, presented Mts. Allison with a handsome silver coffee pot. Mrs. Allison, who afterwards gave an enjoyable supper, briefly responded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>PIONEER LIFE ASSURANCE CO. LTD.</head>
        <p>THE PIONEER LIFE ASSURANCE CO. LTD.</p>
        <p>The twenty-first annual meeting of this company took place on Wednesday, 2Sth May, the Chairman (Mr. G. 13.Smith-Brodrick) presiding. He said the report was the best in the history of the company. In the last six years the premium income had increased £26,000, or 45 per cent. ; the life funds had increased £46,000, or more than doifile, and in addition, ample reserves had been provided for all depreciation. Last year the credit balance was £11,000; this year it was £17,000. The company had since its commencPment paid £280,000 in &amp;Wm.. A dividend at the late of 2!.; per cent. wasdec!are3. The retiring directors, Alderman W. H Brown, J. 13., and Mr. J. Redman Ormerod, were re-elected. The report submitted to the meeting showed that after providing for all claims and expenses there remained a credit balance of £17,483, of which £13,033 was added to the life assurance funds (£10,494 to the ordinary branch, and £2,589 to the industrial branch), £2,400 to the investment reserve fund (which fully providei for all depreciation), awl £2,000 carried to the sharOolders' dividend account. Out of this latter sum the directors, after conferring with the consulting actuary, recommended the payment to the shareholders of &amp; dividend of per cent., free of income-tax, on the paid-up capital. The progress of the company in recent years was evident from the following figures : Life Premium Life Assurance Income— Funds— Total claims combined. combined, paid to date. 1901 1907 - £38,930 ... £lOB,OOO 1913 The part which it had been necessary for their repre- sentatives to take in assisting the working of the National Insurance Act had to some extent interfered with the procuration of business, but numerous agencies of the company were now making sagsfactory progress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>NEW GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>NEW GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED TRANSFER TO SITE IN LATCHFORD. EDUCATION COMMITTEE CLAIMS.</p>
        <p>PLANS FOR A GENERAL SECONDARY EDUCATION SCHEME.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>SUGGESTED GIRLS' SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>SUGGESTED GIRLS' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A development of far-reaching importance in the work of that venerable educational foundation—the Boteler Grammar School—was foreshadowed at the meeting of the Warrington Education Committee on. Monday evening. For a considerable time the govern- ing body has been faced with the necessity of rebuild- ing the school in a more suitable position than the present site in School Brow, the Board of Education, having regard to the changing requirements of the times, thrown the weight of its influence on the side of this momentous departure. A definite decision has now been arrived at, the governors having provisionally selected a site at Latchford, which, it is stated, will fully answer the purposes of the erection of an up-to-date and commodious building and tle provision of large playing fields, as well as allow of future extensions, should occa ion arise. The lccality of the site was not disclosed at the meeting of the Education Committee, but we have the bes authority for stating that it consists of the piece of land lying between the grounds of "The Hollies" and Latchford station. It is bounded by Wash-lane on one side and the old Grappeuhall-road on the other.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>EXISTING SCHOOL '</head>
        <p>THE EXISTING SCHOOL '</p>
        <p>Its History and Resources.</p>
        <p>The projected change is not merely a question of the rebuilaing cf the Grammar School, but has an important bearing upon the whole subject of the supply of secondary education within the borough. The Boteler Grammar School is, of course, a recognised secondary school, and, as such, receives a certain amount of support from the public purse. To what extent the ancient endowments, which are held in trust for the benefit lof the people of Warrington, are to be regarded as public property is perhaps a debatable point. Apart from that source of income, however, the state- ment of amounts for the year Dl2 shows that the school received £377 in grants from the Board of Education, and £220 from the Corporation of War- rington and the Lancashire County Council. The other chief items of revenue were .037 from the endowments and :029 from school fees. At the meet- ing of the Education Committee on Monday evening, the Chairman (Councillor 11. Roberts) claimed that that authority should have been consulted before the site had been provisionally agreed upon. However, a conference has been airanged between the School Management Committee and the governing body of the school, when it is hoped that there will be a full and frank discussion of the relation of the school towards the general educational scheme of the borough. ANOTHER SECONDARY SCHOOL REQUIRED. We understand that the Education Committee have for some time had in mind the fact that, as soon as circumstances would permit, the provision of an addi- tional secondary school for boys would have to be undertaken. The school in Palmyra Square would then be set apart s, lsly for girls. The project has, however, been postponed in view of the Boteler Grammar Schcol itself having become a secondary school, but now that the removal to a new site has become a pressing question, it presents a favourable opportunity for con- sidering the whole question of the position of the new school in reference to the Education Authority's scheme to meet the needs of the town. The governors of the school are : Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Revs. Canon Willis and E. C. E. Carleton, Professor R. S. Conway, Messrs. Charles S. Bargess, W. Peter Bylauds, J. Charlton Parr, R. Forshaw, Roger C. Parr, Hugh Stewart, and the following representatives of the Town Council : Councillor H. Roberts (chairman of the Education Committee), Aldermen W. Bolton, W. J. Furshaw, J. Smethurst and A. Bennett. HOW THE SCHOOL WAS FOUNDED. In the early days of the school the site on School- Brow was ideally situated amid rural surroundings, but the march of industry has changed all that. In view of the contemplated developments, it will be interesting to recall how the school came into being. It had its origin during a remarkable era in our literary and religious history, of which the foundation of grammar schools was a striking characteristic. A. large proportion of those schools owed their founda- tion to charters from King Edward VI. and Queen Elizabeth, granting to them the endowments of chaunti ies which had been suppressed. The Warrington Giammar School, however, owed neither lands nor charter to royal favour, but was indebted for its establishment to the pious bounty of Sir Thomas Boteler. of Bewsey, one of the heroes ot Flodden, whose knightly family, as lords of Warrington from a period very shot tly after the conquest, were so closely identified with the for-tunes of old Warrington. By his will, dated 16th August, 1520, he directed that lands should be pur- chased to found a free Grammar School in Warrington, and, moreover, he set about the good work in his life- • time by acquiring certain lands and tenements in War- rington and Tyldesley. He died on the 27th April, 1522, and his pious intentions were carried into effect by an indenture dated, IGth April, 1526. A recital of the lofty motives of the founder tells us that Sir Thomas Boteler" virtuously calling to his good remembrance that in ye said county and shire of Lancashire be very few schools of grammar whereby men's sons might learn grammar to ye extent that they thereby might ye better learn to know Almighty God and to serve Him according to yr (their) duties by virtue whereof they might ye better avoid and eschew all vices arid use good manners, thinking also inwardly in his hearte that throu ye grace and good- ness of Almighty God many poor children ani young men applying themselves to learn grammar which is ye originail ground and fountayn out of ye which cloth proceed and spring ye very mean and plain way to come to ye clear understanding of good liveing, might approach to such knowledge of ye light of grace that perchance they might happen to be ye very clear lanthorn of good example in vertuous living to all ye country" fully intended to have founded a free grammar school in Warrington, if it had pleased Almighty God to have suffered him to " have lived in this transitorry life it few years longer than be did." In accordance with Sir Thomas Boteler's bequest, the parties to the deed " ordained, established, and made a free grammar school, to he kept and holden for ever in Warring- ton" ; and they appointed the Rev. Richard Taylor to be the first schoolmaster of the school, for his life. Further, a house in Bag-lane and a little croft adjoining were set apart to the use of the said schoolmaster for the time being. therein to keep the said free-school, and that the same should be called the school-house of Warrington." SOME QUAINT PROVISIONS. Some of the " statutes and ordinances " of the school make quaint reading. It was provided : That the said schoolmaster for the time being (wits) was to be a priest) should teach any scholar coming to the said school after Whittington's Grammar or such grammar as should he most used to be taught there- after in free grammar schools, the same to be taught freely, without taking any reward, stipend or school hire, or any other thing by promise, grant or covenant made ; but it was Provided that it should he lawful for the schoolmaster for the time being to take of any scholar of the said school, learning grammar, four pennies a year, viz., in the quarter after Christmas a cock-penny, and in the three other quarters one potation penny, and that for the same time he should make a drinking for all the said scholars in any of the said three quarters. Directions were given as to the ferial-days and holidays to be allowed in the school, as to the school hours, and as to the master and scholars going to the Parish Church—" two and two together in proces- sions "—every Sunday, Wednesday and Friday to sing and join in the performance of divine service accord- ing to the usage of that period. It was also ordained that an anniversary should be kept on April 27th every year "for ye souls of ye said Sir Thomas and his ancestors and his heirs, and for ye soul of Dame Margaret Boteler, after her decease," explicit directions being given as to the offering of Requiem Masses. Further, the bellman of (Continued on Page C.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>NEW GRAMMAR SCHOOL</head>
        <p>NEW GRAMMAR SCHOOL</p>
        <p>(Continued from Page 5.) Warringt on had to go through the town and,according to custom, des ire every man, woman and child to pray for the soul s of Sir Thomas and Dame Margaret. LITIGAIION ABOUT SCHOOL LANDS. Within a century after the foundation of the school, a large proportion of the school lands became alienated from their i:ghtful uses, but in 1602 Sir Peter Warbur- ton, then sergeant-at-law, took it upon himself to vindicate the charity and, through his perseverance and public spirit, the estates were restored to the founda- tion. In 1810, there was a suit in the High Court of Chancery, as a result of which the office of school- master was declared to be incompatible with that of rector. The two situations had before been held by the same person. Rules approved for the better government of the school, and confirmed by the Court in 1820 laid it down that the schoolmaster should diligently, apply himself to the duties of his office by teaching the scholars grammatically the learned languages. A writing master was to be appointed " to teach writing and arithmetic at such hours as would not interfere with the grammatical learning of the scholars," and his salary was to be not less than 5.40 and not more than A.:60. WREN THE SCHOOL WAS FREE TO ALL. A report of the Charity Commissioners, as the .result of an inquiry in 1828,stated : "The school is 'ree to all boys from whatever place they may come, Ind they are admitted on presenting themselves to .he master, provided they are above the age -of seven years, as prescribed by the rules confirmed by the Court of Chancery, and provided also they are able to read English with tolerable ease. No charge is made for instruction, except that accord- ing t•-) the foundation deed each scholar pays id. a civarter. The instruction in the school under the headmaster is entirely classical, and at stated periods the scholars learn writing and accounts under the</p>
        <p>writing master." in 1859, in consequence of a continued falling off in the number of scholars, the trustees were authorised to make application to the Court of Chancery for an amendment of the then existing scheme in the direction of giving more prominence to the modern and com- mercial element in the curriculum. It was at that time that the payment of tuition fees was first authotised, the trustees, however, being given power to admit boys into the lower department at reduced lees "in cases of great poverty." The arrangement of 1859 was, in turn, super- seded in 1880 by a much more elaborate scheme, whereby the basis of appointments to the governing body was revised, provisions made with regard to the qualifications and payments of the staff, and a new scale of entrance and tuition fees payable by the scholars received sanction. A certain number of free scholarships and exhibitions were, however, to be maintained. From this stage onwards the development cf the school is familiar history. It has continued with increasing success to adapt itself to the requirements of a commercial and industrial town, while always giving prominence to the classical side of the curri- culum, and is at the present time filling an honoured and invaluable place in the educational life of the community.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE'S ATTITUDE. COUNCILLOR ROBERTS MAKES A SIGNIFICANT HINT.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE'S ATTITUDE. COUNCILLOR ROBERTS MAKES A SIGNIFICANT HINT.</p>
        <p>The future of the Boteler Grammar School was the principal topic under discussion at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee on Monday evening. A proposal to re-build the school on a more convenient site is occupying the attention of the governors and it is suggested that there should be a conference with the School Management Com- mittee and representatives of the Board of Education presumably with a view to considering the position of the school in relation to the scheme of setondary education for the town. Councillor H. Roberts presided. It was reported that at a meeting of the School Management Committee a letter was submitted from the governors of the Grammar School with reference to the proposed acquisition of a new site for the school, and it was resolved that the Governors of the Grammar School be informed that this committee is of opinion that the quest ion of the provision of a new site for the Warrington Grammar School is a matter having an important bearing upon the whole subject of the supply ' of secondary education within the Borough (for which the local education authority is responsible), and that they will, therefore, be willing to meet the governors of the Grammar School with representatives of the Board of Education for the purpose of discussing the matter with them, should the Board of Education deem such a conference desirable. At a later meeting of the School Management Com- mittee, a further letter was read from the governors of the Grammar School, asking, on the suggestion of the. Board of hlducation, that representatives of this Com- mittee should meet representatives of the governors at a preliminary conference on Monday, the 9th June,l9l3. It was decided to suggest that the full body of governors should meet the whole of this Committee at the time mentioned, and that the meeting should, if possible, take place at the Town Hall. It was also decided to ask the governors to furnish the committee beforehand with a statement of the present position of affairs, and also of any proposals they might wish to make for consideration at the conference, and that this statement, if received, should be considered by this committee at a meeting to be held on the afternoon of Tuesday, the 3rd June, 1913. FAR-REACHING CONSEQUENCES. Canon STEVENSON, in. moving the adoption of the minutes, said the School Management Committee felt that the proposed removal of the school was a very important matter which might have far-reaching con- sequences upon the secondary education of the borough and lead to various new plans. Therefore, they looked forward to the conference with the earnest hope that something might be done which would be for the great benefit of the secondary education of the town. They did not at present know anything about the plans which the governors of the Grammar School had in their minds, but he was sure he would say, on behalf of the School Management Committee, that they would like, in every- way, to further anything which might be for the educa- tional interests of Warrington. A SITE SELECTED. Mr. BURGESS said the resolution of the School Management Committee hardly represented the exact</p>
        <p>state of the case, as it stood at the present time. The governors had not themselves asked for the conference with the Education Committee. They were iuformed by his Majesty's Inspector of Secondary Schools that there were certain developments in contemplation by the local education authority, and it was well-known that the governors were also contemplating some ehange—a change which had been forced upon them, in a way, by the Board of Education and the circumstances of the times. They were on the look-out for—in fact they had provisionally found a site upon which to build a new scnool. He did not know that it was any secret that that site was within the Borough and that it was sufficiently large,not only for the erection of a grammar school but to provide playing fields for the boys and to allow of any developments which might be required for many years to come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ATTITUDE OF THE GOVERNORS.</head>
        <p>THE ATTITUDE OF THE GOVERNORS.</p>
        <p>It was not the Grammar School governors who were seeking the conference and they had not exactly asked tor it. Therefore, he did not think it was at all likely that, at the present stage, any proposals could come from them to the local education authority. Of Course, the concern of the governors was the welfare and efficiency of the institution. At the same time they would welcome any suggestions tram the local authority with regard to the improvement of secondary education in the town, but he thought that, at this stage, any proposals should not come from the governors but rather from the Board of Education' who had suggested the conference, or from the local authority. At the present moment the governors, as far as he knew, had no proposals to bring before the conference. LINKING UP THE WHOLE SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said they all wanted to approach the question with open minds and in the broadest spirit. He simply pleaded for an absolutely non-committal attitude on both sides, a careful comparison of aims and ideas, and then au endeavour to link up the whole system of the town on lines that would be acceptable to everyone conc,...rnee. They would all wish to achieve that object without doing violence to old traditions or to the institution of which they were so proud.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>"MILK AND WATER" ATrITUDE.</head>
        <p>A "MILK AND WATER" ATrITUDE.</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON asked : Why these disclaiming— speeches? The conference was a matter for the future and he did not think they should have speeches suggesting what they should or should not do. He took it that, after the meeting with the governors, the School Manage- ment Committee would present a report and perhaps give advice as to the course of action to be taken. What they had heard from Mr. Burgess seemed to be a kind of discounting of anything they might do, a limiting of their powers or even of the advice they should give,and asking them to adopt such an absolutely milk and water imitate that they could only suggest to the governors the opinions of the committee. They were apparently to be placed.in such a helpless position that they would mercly be able to report that they had had a non-committal talk with certain gentlemen and that they had nothing to lay before the Education Committee. If they were going to throw open the whole question, let them have a full dress debate now. If not, let them take part in the conference and after- wards debate their suggestions,. HOW THE CONFERENCE WAS PROPOSED.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said the ordinary members of the committee did nut know whet they were talking about, with reference to this question, as they had not had the opportunity of seeing the letter from the governors. It looked as if the Board of Education had interfered and said that the Education Committee should have some say in the matter. If that were so, Mr. Burgess's remarks were uncalled for. He (the speaker) could not understand the attitude voiced by Mr. Burgess. Either the committee had something to say or they had not. If they had, they ought to give the best opinion they could for the benefit of the town. Of course, the governors might feel a bit sore on the point, especially the older ones. Canon STEVENSON explained that on April 23rd the Clerk to the Governors of the Grammar School sent a letter to the committee in which he stated that it was desirable that representatives of the Education Com- mittee, the governors and the Board of Education should meet in conference to discuss the question of the new site for the Grammar School. If the Education Committee agreed (he added) he proposed to write to the Board and suggest that course. On receiving the letter the Board of Management passed the first resolution now before the meeting. That was their position and that was all he (Canon Stevenson) knew.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>ANXIOUS FOR A CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>ANXIOUS FOR A CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. BURGESS said he appeared to have been mis- understood. His only point was that be did not think that, at this stage, the governors of the Grammar School hai any proposals to bring before the confer- ence. It was simply a preliminary meeting to discuss what should be done in view of the fact that the inspector had informed them that there were certain developments in contemplation by the locil authority. The governors, falling in with the suggestion of the Board of Education, %ere willing and anxious to have a conference in order to see what could be done for the betterment of secondary education in the town, and the question of the new site naturally came under that head. He had no idea of suggesting that the governors desired a" milk and water " conference. THE CHAIRMAN'S IMPORTANT PRONOUNCEMENT.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said the question was one of very great importance. The education authority of War- rington, which, after all, was the authority responsible for both elementary and condary instruction in the borough, was not only entitled, but, as a matter of policy, it was essential that it should be consulted from the initiation of that scheme, and until some agreement—if an agree- ment were possible—was effected. It aas quite obvious that the removal of the school to a new site ought to be considered in its relation to the adaptability of that school and site to the general purposes of secondary instruction in Warrington, if that school was to receive that recognition and financial support which it appeared to him would he essential, if it was to be made a success. In other words, it came to this: Either the Grammar School, under its new conditions, must be an important part of the general scheme of secondary instruction in Warrington, or it must relegate itself, through the action of the governors, to its old position as a semi-private school. The latter course would, to his mind, be extremely unfortun- ate for everybody concerned. As far as the Education Committee was concerned, they only desired first to serve the cause of education, and secondly to adapt existing institutions, as far as possible, with that object in view, under proper and suitable conditions. It would have been better if the Education Committee had been consulted, even before the site suggested bad been provisionally approved. (Elear, hear.) Mr. Burgess had not left an impression that the conference was likely to be extremely hopeful in its possible results. However, he (Councillor Roberts) hoped the result of the conferences might be to evolve such a scheme as would be worthy of the school itself, and especially in regard to that part which it ought to take and might take, under proper and suitable condi- tions, in the general scheme of education in the town. Failing that, there was no alternative before the Education Committee but that it should, in due course, make proper and adequate provision on its own lines, and under its own control, for secondary instruction. Mr. JOLLEY said that, in passing the resolutions referred to, the School Management Committee had nothing to go upon but the opinion of the governors, as voiced by their law clerk. They knew nothing about the opinions of individual governors although it would appear that there was a divergence of view among them. The minutes were then approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>5</p>
        <p>LY MAL</p>
        <p>TBE Poon RATE.—At the Sale Petty Sessions beld on Monday morning, the magistrates approved of a poor rate for the township of Lymm for the next half- year of 9d. in the £ on agricultural land, and ls. Gd. in the £ on buildings and other hereditament.—At a monthly meeting of the Lymm Urban District Council on Thursday night a general district rate of 2s. Bd. in the £ was laid.</p>
        <p>TERRITORIALS' RETURN.—On Sunday the Cheshire Territorial Brigade concluded a fortnight's successful camp at Abergavenny, the local men arriving at Lymm Station soon after 1 o'clock in the evening. With tanned faces and looking in first-class condition, they stated that they had had an excellent time ; the weather, after the first two or three days being all that could be desired, and they are naturally elated at their Battalion, the sth CheThires, having won the cup for shooting, and the second prize for drill. SUFFRAGE MEETING.—Though Lymm possesses several ladies of avowed suffrage tendencies it was not until Monday night that public meeting was ventured upon. The local ladies were supported by an Altrin- charn representative of the National Union of Woman Suffrage Societies (law-abiding and non-party) and the Rev. Dendy Agate. L: nfortunately for" the cruse," the Liberal meeting at the Drill Hall and the Conserva- tive gathering at the Cross were too much of a counter- attraction with the result that only a short and sparslev- attended meeting was held at the entrance to the Dingle. CONFIRMATION AT THE P.A.RISH CHURCH.—On Tuesday afternoon a confirmation was held in the Lyrnrn PAish Church. A large number of candidates, who had been prepared by the Rector, the Rev. C. B. Thurston (adults and girls) ; Rev. H. Gray (boys), and the Rev. E. P. Wethered (Oughtrington candidates), were presented to the Bishop of Chester for confirma- tion. The Revs. F. C. Bozman, H. A. D. Surridge, and J. Edgerton-Warburton took part in the proceedings. The church was filled with parents and friends who witnessed the solemn rite. At the conclusion of the service, the confirmees confirmed adjourned to the Rectory where they were kindly entertained to tea. afterwards reassembling in the church for a special service.</p>
        <p>THE MAT QUEEN FESTlvkl.—On Friday night last week a meeting of the May Queen Committee was held in Hurst's Pavilion, when the principal business was an examination of the financial statement of this year's Festival. As forecasted in our last issue, the com- mittee are hopeful of just making ends meet, though the subscription list is much below that of previous years. After a lengthy discussion, it was unanimously decided that a tea be given to the children of the dis- trict in July, although this would mean an adverse balance to commence the next Festival with, unless, as WS s hoped would be the case, the local supporters and friands would rally to the assistance of the committee and by giving small subscriptions thus defray the extra expenditure. ACCIDENT TO A CYCLIST.—During the past few weeks there have been quite a number of serious acci- dents in Lymm—two motor smashes at the Eagle-hrow cross roads ; one on Ditchfield's Bridge, where, by the way, the proposed and much-needed alterations are assuming a more practical form ; and lastly, a disaster on Tuesday afternoon at the corner of Cherry-lane and and Booth's-lane. It appears that an employe of Messrs. Burgon's, Ltd., Percy Moss, was coming down Cherry- lane, on a bicycle, which though a free-wheel machine, was fitted with neither brake nor bell, and as he turned into the main road he was run into by a car going towards Warrington, driven by Mr. George Lines, chauffeur to Mr. Frank Mortimer, of Golden Green, Llanasa, Flintshire. The boy was thrown from his cycle, which was completely smashed up, sustaining injuries to the back of his bead. Mr. Lines at once took the unfortunate cyclist to Dr. de Jong's where he was found to have sustained a fractured skill. BURGLARS' VISIT.—A band of burglars, undeterred by the politicians' solemn assurance that the eyes of the world are centred on Lymm, have selected the present exciting time to visit the locality in pursuit of their nefarious calling, several daring attempts having been made to enter the residences of local gentry, though fortunately with but trifling advantage to the depredators. During the small hours of Sunday morn- ing Mr. 11. Hesketh was awakened by the barking of his dog, and on looking through the window saw a man climbing over the back-door. Mr. Hesketh shouted to the would-be intruder who at Once took to his heels. From this point the man—or men —proceeded in the direction of Lyrnm village and entrance was gained to the house of Mr. Lathom by sawing through the brass fastenings of a window. It was not until Sunday afternoon that the family realised that they had been entertaining visitors unawares, their suspicions being then aroused by their inability to discover the whereabouts of a newly-boiled ham and other comestibles. This led to an examination of the premises and disclosed the fact that the house had been forcibly entered, though nothing of any great value had been stolen. After partaking of a substantial repast, at the expense of Mr. Lathom, the thieves pro- ceeded to " Bleak House," tbe residence of Mr. G. L. Welford. Here they likewise effected an entrance by means of a window skilfully unfastened from the outside It would he about 4 o'clock when Miss Welford heard a slight tapping sound and on looking through the window she observed a suspicious-looking character standing on the roadside,evidently keeping a sharp look- out. She called Mr. Welford who at once went downstairs and entered the dining-room just in time to witness the departure of the intruder through the window; under the circumstances pursuit was, of course, out of the question. Here again the would-be thieves failed to take anything of value. On Tuesday evening, at about 9.30, Mrs. Welford, who had been attending a Liberal meeting at the Cross, where her husband had been speaking, reached her home, luckily in company with the gardener,when one of these miscreants bounded from a window. The gardener at once gave chase, but though they twice made the circuit of the grLuads, the man eventually escaped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>COMPLAINTS OF BLACK SNIOKE FROM HEATLEY AND SANDY-LANE. The monthly meeting of the Lymm Urban District Council was held in the Council Caamber on Thursday evening, Mr. S. Davies presiding. There were also present: The Rev. G. B. Thurston, Messrs. 0. G. Smith, G. L. Welford, S. W. Gould, W. G. Groves, T. Warburton, R. H. Gotobed, F. Lockyer, T. F. W. Dixon, J. H. Holt, with %V. Mullard (clerk), D. Mort (surveyor) and W. L. Donaldson (Council's manager). MASSEY BROOK BRIDGE. A letter was read from the Tnelwall Parish Council with reference to the Massey Brook Bridge and also the report of the surveyor on the bridge. It was resolved that no action be taken by the Council. SMOKE AND SMELL. At a meeting of the Sanitary Committee the Clerk was instructed to write Messrs. Clarke and Moore to the effect that complaints had been received with regard to smoke at Heatley Salt Works, and asking that the same be abated. At the Council meeting a letter was read from Mr. Alfred H. Fray, Mill-lane, Heatley, complaining of the smoke, also of the smell. He would also like to draw attention to ths question of pumping brine in the district and suggested that the matter of power to compel a sinking or compensation fund against any question of subsidence should he given consideration. Mr. Gotobed said that the people of Heatley felt very keenly about the matter. There was some ground for those complaints which came up very frequently. J ust after they had written the firm there seemed to be an improvement for a time. Mr. Gould said they had talked about the matter for many years. They kept getting it over and over again, that seemed to boa standing dish. If they could not do anything in the matter they ought to drop it, but there was no disputing the fact that it had been a nuisance for years. Mr. Mort had said he would go and make inspection, but they were no nearer a solution of the problem now than they were years ago. Rev. G. B. Thurston : It all depends on the observa- tions made by the surveyor. The Clerk : We have given them three months. Mr. Warburton : It is not so much the big chimney as the portable engine in the field. When people were having their meals and when they were washing all the clothing and utensils were black with soot. Complaints were made regularly, but there was no abatement. It was only a few yards from one's back door and they got the worst of it. It was high time the Council did something in the matter. In regard to the stench he would say in conclusion that whichever .way the wind blew they got it, and it did not half " hum." The Chairman: What power have we in regard to that stench ? Mr.Warburton said it only came at times. He would, however, like to know how Mr. Mort or the doctor</p>
        <p>was going to report on the matter unless he lived there? He. was prepared to stand by the Council and give evidence and they could get quite a dozen people from Heatley. Mr. Welford moved that they ask the firm for an explanation and also get to know whether the nuisance could be stated.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gutobed seconded, and the proposition was carried.</p>
        <p>A DANGEROUS BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>A letter was read ii:o-rn-l\ii.-W;Yai:t-e-rsall, St. Ann's- square, Manchester, in which he stated that he had called attention to the dangerous bridge over the canal at Lymrn between the village and Warrington, and his complaint was brought to the notice of the Cheshire County Council. The bridge was exceedingly dangerous to cyclists and pedestrians and there bad been several "near shaves." He hoped the matter would be seriously comidered and some action taken to remove the danger. Mr. Smith : We are cursed with a lot of bad bridges in Lymm. Mr. Gould : We have the matter in hand.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said they -attempting to do something in the matter. He had spoken to the County Surveyor about it.</p>
        <p>DAMAGE BY SEWERAGE SCHEME,</p>
        <p>On the recommendation of the General Purposes Committee it was decided that Mr. S. W. Gould be asked to act as valuer on behalf of the Council in con- nection with any damage caused to surface crops by the new sewerage scheme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PPOINTM ENT S.</head>
        <p>A PPOINTM ENT S.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. G. Groves was re-elected governor to repre- sent the Council on the Board of Management for three years of the Lymm Grammar School and Mr. .1. H. Holt was deputed to attend the conference of local authorities to be held at Llandrindid Wells in July.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>EMPIRE DAY.—The children attending the Counc School celebrated Empire Day in loyal fashion yester- day week. The rooms had been tastefully decorated by the children and teachers. The principal feature was the presentation of medals annually given to the best boy and the best girl by Mr. Fu7ness. There were present the Rev. J. R. Jones and Miss Jones and Mr. and Mrs. Furness. The medals were presented to Annie Bond and David Ronehill. Patriotic songs were sung during the proceedings by the scholars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>CONCERT IN THE WESLEY HALL, PADGATE.—A concert was given on Thursday:of last week before a good audieace. in Wesley Hall, Padgate, by Miss Roscollor's pupils. The chair was taken by Mr. F. Wright. A cantata entitled "The Three Bears" was the chief item on the programme, and caused great amusement. The principal characters were ably taken by the following : Father Bear, Annie Lowe; Mother BeJ,r, Jane Riley; Little Bear, Sydney Dale :IGoldlocks, Lavinia Rigby ; Purple Emperor, Eileen Davies; Red Admiral, Hilda Bell. Several good action songs were performed, among which were "Sailor Jack," "Give me a Piggy-back,' " got my eye on you" and "Good-bye Mr. Teddy Bear." Another item which deserves special mention was a _ _ _ . _</p>
        <p>minuet, gracefully danced by four girls. Jane Riley sang a solo entitled "Just like bein at home," and Annie Lowe recited "The pride of Battery B " Hilda Rathbone gave two recitations" Mother's little maid," and "Good-night and Good-morning." Harold Percival played a pianoforte solo "The fairies' dream," and May Taylor and Hilda Bell a duet "The snowdrop waltz." All the children were admirably trained, for which great credit is due to Miss Roscollor, who also accompanied them throughout the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>GRAMMAR SCHOOL SCHOLARSHIPS.—Eric Hal3lllM and Douglas Hallows have been successful in passing the Cheshire County Council scholarship examinations, and have been awarded free scholarships for Lymm Grammar School.</p>
        <p>WESTAYAN CHURCH.—The Sunday school anniversary services, which were held in this church on Sunday, were more than usually successful. Eloquent sermons were preached by the Rev. Howaru N. Burton, of St. Helens. A flower service and cantata entitled "Spring- time and flowers " was given by the scholars in the afternoon. Mr. George Weaver presided. The children took their parts with great credit under the conductor- ship of the choirmaster, Mr. F. Penketh. Solos and duets were sung by the Misses E. Houghton, E. Whit- field, D. Speakman, S. Gamble and Master W. Ward. Recitations were given by J. Shaw, J. Judd, E. Travis, L. Gerrard. S. Gamble, E. Rustage, E. Williams, L. Sandford, H. Adamson, Miss Mann Kirkland, M. Rams- bottom. J. Hamlett, E. Worrall, C. Worrall and Daisy Summers. The choir rendered special music at the morning and evening services, and the singing of the children at these services wis also a much appre?Aated feature. Miss Bertha Stringer, one of the scholars, sang a solo at the evening service. Mr. .1. L. White was the organist at all the services. On Monday even- ing the Rev. Howard N. Burton gave a miscellaneous recital to a large audience. Mr. John Taylor presided. The collections amounted to £lB 13s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee was held at the Hall Cafe on Tuesday, Mr. G. Pilling presiding. There were also Present :—Messr“. J. Clarke, H. J. Westbrook, J. Whitley, F. Rose, A. Bottornlev, C. Dukes. J. Hallows, C. Hallows, J. McDermid, W. Mclntyre, F. Pearson, G. F. Ashton (clerk), and A. S. Wright (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>POLICE DUTY.</head>
        <p>POLICE DUTY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Reginald Pottis (Clerk of the County Council) wrote acknowledging the receipt of the committee's letter with ref r3nce to the attendance of officer on duty at the Stockton Heath Police Station. He stated that the contents would be brought under the notice of the Chief Constable and, if necessary, of the Standing Joint Committee.</p>
        <p>A FLOODED ROAD. STRONG COMPLAINT FROM RESIDENTS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the surveyor had received a complaint from a number of residents of Alexandra. road, as to a nuisance which was alleged to exist in the road. The surface water, in places a foot deep, com- pletely covered it from side to side and for half the length of it, leaving barely room to pass even on the footpatb. The signatories stated that they were quite aware that a resolution had been passed by the Pariah Council for the road to be made, but it would be a lengthy procedure, and they asked the committee to make use of their powers to effect an immediate temporary improvement by causing the water to be drained off and the hollow filled up with cinders. When the water dried up, the road gradually became a stink- ing quagmire, as it was the receptacle of all sorts of rubbish.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor wrote that during the last fortnight the road had been flooded more than ever. He suggested that, whilst the Council wns giving the usual legal notices under the Private Street Works Act, a temporary corner should be made in the main t welve- inch sewer which ran down the centre of the road, and a grid put in by the Council. It was reported that the Surveyor's suggestion had been acted upon. Mr. Mclntyre moved, and Mr. J. Hallows, seconded that this course be approved. Mr. Dukes: How song is the road to be left in this temporary condition? Mr. Pilling : The question is now before the Local Government Board, the notices for the making of the street having been issued. There has been a great improvement since the alterations were carried out. Mr. Dukes : Would it not have been possible to do Derby-road at the ssme time ? Mr. Hallows explained that it would be impossible to drain Derby-road in the same way. The action already taken was approved.. THAT BELL !</p>
        <p>The Clerk of the County Council forwarded for approval agreement with the County Council on behalf of the Runcorn Rural District Council, with reference to the fixing of the fire alarm bell on Stockton Heath Police Station.</p>
        <p>Mr. McDermid said the Fire Appliances Committee bad met and considered the agreement. They suggested that the annual payment of ss. to the County Council should be reduced to the nominal sum of Is. which was originally agreed to for the fixing of the bell in the old position. The agreement, as drafted, left the defining of the position in which the bell should be hung, excln- aively in the hands of the County architect and Chief Constable. The committee, however, suggested that two representatives of the Parochial Committee should be consulted.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: As a matter of fact, you will not sign the agreement until you are satisfied as to the position. Mr. Westbrook: We have practically fixed the position of the bell. It has been agreed upon verbally. Mr. McDermid said the committee further proposed the addition of a clause to the effect that, in case of termination of the agreement, the District Council should be at liberty to re-place the fire-call hut on its present site in Victoria-square and resume their right to the site. Subject to the suggested alterations, the agreement was approved.</p>
        <p>TENDERS FOR STREET WORKS. Tenders for private street works having been received Pottomley proposed that, in their tabulation for' Consideration of the committee, numbers should be Substituted for the names of the persons tendering. .I‘lr Dukes urged the importance of taking precautions with, regard to the rate of wages paid. They should see that labour wag not remunerated at such a tow rate as to encourage the scamping of work. On that ground it was necessary that they should know the type of firm with whom they proposed to deal, before letting the contract. It was decided to adopt Mr. Bottomley's proposal. The tenders will be considered at a special meeting.</p>
        <p>PENKETH• LOCAL ATHLETE'S SUCCESS.—At the Liverpool University Guilds Athletic Club's 20th annual athletic sports, held on Saturday, on the Athletic Ground, Calderstone, Mr. J. Baker, son of Mr. J. W. Baker, of Penketh, carried off first prize in the Quarter Mile (Handicap) in 51 3-ssecs. : 2nd prize in the 100 yards race for challenge cup ; 2nd prize in the 220 yards handicap ; 2nd prize in the quarter mile race for challenge cup. He was also selected with Mr. H. Barlow to take part in the inter-Varsity 100 yards race, which speaks well for his athletic prowess. TANNERY BAND'S SUCCESS.—Penketh Tannery Band journeyed to Coppull on Saturday to take part in the annual brass band contest. Seven bands entered, viz.: Penketh Tannery, Loneridges, Nelson Old, Glazebury. Leyland, Haslingden Temperance, Skelmersdale Old and' Heywood Old. The test pieces were "Bohemian Girl's (Balfs). Mr. W. Adamson, bandmaster of Wingates Temperance band, was the adjudicator. The result was as follows :-1, Skelmersdale ; 2, Penketh Tannery ; 3. Leyland ; 4, Nelson Old ; 5, Haslingden Temperance, In the march competition (piece own choice) Penketh Tannery were first and Nelson Old second.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>RISLEY.</head>
        <p>RISLEY.</p>
        <p>ANNIVERSARY.—The 251st anniversary services in connection with the old Risley Church were held on Sunday. The preacher was the Rev Samuel Morrison, ot Manchester, and his services were much appreciated by large congregations. Mr. A. 11. Clarkson presided at the organ, and at the afternoon and evening services Mrs. Clarkson played two violin solos. Miss Clare sang "Come unto Me, ye weary," and "Abide with me." The offertory for church funds realised the gratifying sum of £l4 93. Gd. INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHURCH, FEARNHEAD.— The Sunday school anniversary sermons were preached on Sunday. In the morning, Mr. Wm. Evans, of War- rington, gave a very interesting address to the children and elder scholars.—ln the afternoon and evening, Mr. James Trickett, of Ince, preached two eloquent sermons, which were highly appreciated. The choir and children acquitted themselves well in the musical part of the service. The anthem "I know that my Redeemer liveth " was given with fine taste, the solo being taken by Miss A. Bate, and a duet by Mr. John Barton and Miss Bate. Mr. E. Bate gave a bass solo. The anthem " The earth is the Lord's" was also creditably sung. Mr. T. Cook and Miss A. Bate took the solo parts in a pleasing manner. The children's pieces " Sing, for the Saviour" and "The world is very beautiful " suited the youthful voices and were very pleasant to listen to. Praise is due to Mr. J. Barton for the training of the children.—The collections amounted to the very satisfactory sum of £l2 2s. 4,1 d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>VISIT OF THE BISHOP OF LIVERPOOL.—The Lord Bishop of Liverpool preached in Orford Church on Sunday evening, on behalf of the Vicarage Fund. The service was conducted by the Vicar, (Rev. G. Vale Owen. The Bishop took for his text I. Kings, vi., " And the house, when it was in building, was built of stone, and made ready before it was brought thither, so that there was neither hammer, nor axe, nor any tool of iron heard in the House while it was in building." His Lordship said that as they heard those words, two pictures came before the eye and the mind. Upon a hill, a magnificent building was slowly rising. It was built of the best stone and rarest wood. The remarkable point was that no sound was heard, for everything had been prepared before being brought. This was the building of the House of God by Solomon, on Mount Zion. in the other picture, we looked upon a quarry which was all a scene of confusion. At Liverpool to-day we might see the quarry and the Cathedral, but while atl Solomon's Temple there was perfect silence, on St. James' Mount there is a babel of sound. In these two pictures we had a type of earth and heaven—of earth, where the souls of men were being prepared, and of that great temple heaven,the corner-stone of which was our Lord Jesus Christ. As with the stone, our souls had to pass through three processes,separation,prepara- tion and translation. It had been his lot to work for the Lord Jesus Christ during the last 43 years, during which time he had had the pleasure of seeing many a soul separated from sin,and given to God. The worries and cares of our daily life were really blows of the " great Mason's chisel," fitting out our soul for the part it was to play in the world to come. Finally, the time came when the stone was ready. It was taken when it was ready for the place, and the place was ready for it. Thus there was no such thing as premature death. When the person dies, the soul was ready for the place above, and the place is also ready. In con- clusion, the Bishop said they were giving their alms to the vicarage fund. A generous landowner had given them a flue site. Last year they raised £IOC. That was met by another £lOO, and it will next he met by £2OO by the Ecclesiastical Commissioners. They had done well in setting an example to the diocesein showing that they had thought more of their vicar than of themselves in building the vicarage first and then work- ing for an organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>TACT.</head>
        <p>TACT.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. Spurgeon on "The Salt of Every Good Word." .</p>
        <p>"Tact, the talent of talents, " was the subject of a highly interesting lecture by the Rev. Thomas Spurgeon, son of that ever-revered man, the late Rev. Charles H. Spurgeon, of the Metropolitan Tabernacle, in the Hill Chile Baptist Church, on Tuesday evening. There was a large attendance, over which Councillor H. Roberts presided. The Rev. G. Curtis was also on the rostrum.</p>
        <p>In his opening remarks, the Chairman said he did not know that he felt altogether at home in a Baptist pulpit, although he might say that he felt quite at home in a Baptist Church. Be did not think there was much need to introduce Mr. Spurgeon who was well known to all of them. They cordially welcomed the speaker for his name, for the work he was doing, and the same he had come there to support. They would hear from his own statements, sufficient of the noble work of the institution which had so many claims particularly on all Christian communities and above all upon Baptist communities. (Applause.) The Rev. T. Spurgeon. who dealt with his subject in a pleasing manner, always with an eye for the humorous, prefaced his lecture by references to the Pastors' College and the Stockwell Orphanage. It was more than 21 years since his father passed away on the shores of the Mediterranean, but his works were still impressing the people of this country. Critics bad said that the Pastors' College would pass away because Spurgeon was dead, but the good work had gone on there. Prophets had said that the great Metropolitan Tabernacle would soon become a music hall. He would say it always was a music hall—one of the best sort in which the high praises of God were sweetly and earnestly sung. Contrary to what the prophets said, the Stockwell Orphanage was doing more than surviv- ing—it was thriving. There were 500 children in that orphanage. The greatest need had the loudest voice; it was for the poorest of the poor, and there were none so poor as those who once had no anxiety as to bow to make both ends meet. They bad no difficulty in finding situations for their boys and girls when they bad finished their course at the orphanage of which he was the president.</p>
        <p>DRESSED UNIFORMLY, BUT NOT IN UNIFORM.</p>
        <p>The children were not clad in uniform, lest it should be a badge of pauperism. They dressed them uniformly, but not in uniform. Both institutions were run on distinctly gospel lines, and he looked upon them as Spurgeon's legacy. Speaking on "Tact," Mr. Spurgeon said that perhaps his greatest difficulty was to provide a definition of his subject. They all had an intuitive conception of tact, but it was not easy to boil the definition down into one sharp, crisp phrase. He was always reminded of the little child who was reported to have given the definitirm of salt as" that which makes potatoes taste nasty if you have not got any with them."</p>
        <p>Tacr THE SALT OF LIFE. Similarly, tact was that quantity that made words or actions always insipid and harmful when there was none of it with them. It was the salt which seasoned every good word. He compared it to the guardian aogel who told them what to do or say and how to do</p>
        <p>Aes, t o cteigt of it. There were three classes of people would like to refer in connection with tact. oicoP'; all there were the naturally tactful; people fenidg' jolt; could not help it—it was the way of them. vee in the well of the heart there was the clear coo st to of consideration for others. That was 51w.,63;6t, we.,ttt bottom of tact. He questioned very much possible for a selfish man to be a tactful roali,A,, ode', by accident. The clean cool water of cous,y-r, tb` , came up in the bucket of the speech. There tooP second class of tactful people, those he honour,nt i(l3,ej of ell—people who were not naturally tactful v;b001 became so by dint of prayer and praise. TheY fjch rd themselves to speak with caution and set a oa,v tbt,t their lives. Then there was the third clast3t ve'tficl pot class of people, who had not got tact, who, t to cultivate it and who would not know vc'e.</p>
        <p>with it if they had it. AN EXAMPLE OF SPURGEON'S TAGI Illustrating his father's tactfulness the 5Pe5.,,5,te,,,1V1 the letter which his father sent to Alias Tb0rn199,;,. sang a song against Spurgeon in the music 103,`;', per , wrote him a sarcastic note, but he Tel,- h;oo.,cos madam, your courteo,s note came duly t° b Lel reply I have only to say that the matter to refer is so entirely a question of good taste t'lp sure I can, with the utmost confidence, s: absolutely in your own kind hands." The resie-bdo,,if that the objectionable song was at once vb,:dertivu'l and Spurgeon's reputation for tact was n°,11,6„,.00;il enhanced. (Applause.) Tact was the inn's' I in) - quality for Christian workers. It was essenti: Christian service. He had found by dint 't .0) trouble and by keeping his eyes open that chrigt„ire possible to came across now and then, minister who would be all the better for a uneetTbi tact. (Applause.) The speaker briehtenei b'e'sg by relating numerous hu,norous stories, illustrB-1 various points in the address. ' tit tia° A collection was taken on behalf of the ingti Mr. Spurgeon represented.</p>
      </div>
