      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>"I REMEMBER</head>
        <p>"I REMEMBER</p>
        <p>REMINISCENCES AND STORIES OF OLD WARRINGTON. GLEANED FROM MANY QUARTERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mounfield invites contributions and hints for his " I Remember" column, which is one of the most popular of the many features of the " Examiner."</p>
        <p>Warrington's "Pubs." The very delightful satire of your corre- spondent of last week leads me to say a word on the subject of Warrington's "pubs," or, as they would be more accurately described the Warrington coaching houses. It is easy to make merry about the numerous places where thirst could be quenched, but it ought to be put down as a matter of history that these places hardly existed for the Warrington people at all. To show the point I am trying to make, let us put the numbers of licensed houses against the number of inhabitants. A hundred years ago there were perhaps 11,000 to 12,000 people in the town. Comparing this with the present population the town was about one-sixth or one-seventh its present size. Yet we learn that Bridge-street had nine- teen public-houses, and in addition to these around Market Gate there were half-a-dozen more, and around the Corn Market, near by, at least ten more. Horsemarket-street had a generous number. Now, could anyone suppose that all these licensed premises,many of them large and well-equipped,.existed for the thirst-quenching of Warringtonians ? Surely there could not have been enough of surplus wealth in Warrington in those days to keep stout such an army of publicans. The explanation is, of course, that War- rington was a station upon a very much- frequented highway. Its streets echoed to the sound of the horn as the stage coaches rolled in. At the doorways of the hostelries passengers alighted for dinner or supper. , Those who arrived late rested for the night; those at mid-day took dinner and climbed back into the coach; those passing when neither bed nor dinner were due took War- rington ale as an antidote to monotony and all the other ills that flesh is heir to. No, we cannot leave out the " pubs " in any true picture of old Warrington. Inn keeping was a business, and a pretty big one, , in the old towns and the inns, however we ' look back on them, were things of comfort and a joy for ever to the much-jolted and weary , limbs of the wayfarers of long ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHAPELS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAPELS.</p>
        <p>The Baptists. I can supplement the interesting notes of your last issue by a reference to the Baptist Chapels. Your correspondent is right in saying that the Baptists worshipped in the warehouse in Back Dallam-lane. (This build- ing was originally a malt kiln and the upper part was the part used for meetings.) But they were the strongly Calvinistic Baptists. William Gadsby, of Manchester, was their leader, and the cause was part of a movement which spread pretty rapidly in the early years of the nineteenth century. The church began, I believe, in 1833. In 1846 they were joined by Mr. Samuel Smith, who proved a capable leader, and they were able to erect Ebenezer Chapel. Salem Chapel. Salem Chapel has not always been a Baptist meeting place. It was built by a number who seceded from Stepney Chapel in 1811 and was a Congregationalist (Inde- pendent was then the word) Chapel. There was, perhaps, hardly room for two churches of one denomination so close to each other and Salem was closed. It was occupied again in 1853 by a number of worshippers who seceded from St. John's Church, Winwick- street. A few years later it became a Baptist Church. I have been told that it was taken over in 1876. The history of the Golborne- street Baptists appears to be this: There was a desire to establish an evangelical Baptist Church in Warrington and some members of Hill Cliffe and Latchford Baptist Churches united to hire the Masonic Rooms in Sankey-street and held services there. In 1869, after about three years in the hired rooms, they built an iron chapel in Rylands- I street. In 1876 they were able to buy Salem I Chapel and the church has continued there. Among the members of Salem sixty years ago were William Silcock,wire manufacturer; J. B. Edelsten, file maker; Glazebrook Rylands,whose home was Heath House (now Messrs. Tunstall's offices) ; and also his next- door neighbour, Dr. Hardy, whose home has been absorbed by the" Examiner "Office. I think that these were members, not of he Baptist, but of the Independent Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>SMALL HOLDINGS IN ANCIENT DAYS.</head>
        <p>SMALL HOLDINGS IN ANCIENT DAYS.</p>
        <p>The much discu=sed question of email holdings is by no means confined to modern times. Far back in the classic ages there were troubles in regard to land and its tenure. In Cicero's time very few but the rich owned any land in Italy. But in Greece the majority of free men were the possessors of faims which they owned. Inborn was the love of husbandry with the Greeks, and the peculiar pride of possession in regard to land was theirs in a marked degree. From time immemorial the Greeks have been 3kilfal farmers, who exist very largely by the cultivation of the beautiful vine which yields the grape we receive in the form of Currants. No other country can compete with Greece in the production of the wonderful Currant, so full of nourishment, easily assimilated, and withal palatable and sppetising. England is a benefactor to Greece in so far as large quantities of Currants are consumed in these isles. But if the extraordinary food value of Currants were appreciated as it should be, even more of the delicious fruit would be eaten, and we should benefit in health and pocket.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>SALVATION ARMY EMIGRATION.</head>
        <p>SALVATION ARMY EMIGRATION.</p>
        <p>In connection with the development of the Salvation Army General Bramwell Booth has appointed Com- missioner D. C. Lamb to pay a visit to Australia and New Zealand. Commissioner Lamb while holding the ' position of International Secretary to the Colonies in the Army is also Director of the Salvation Army Emigration Department, and it is probable in view of his wide and varied experience of the subject that the Commonwealth authorities will take advantage of his visit to discuss with him the question of emigration as it affects the country. Commissioner Lamb will visit Canada on his way to Australia. During his absence the business of the Emigration Department will be supervised by Colonel Cuthbert. Lieut.- Colonel Turner, the officer responsible for the super vision of the Army's linmigratloa work in Canada, will be in London this week to consult with Com- missioner Lamb prior to the latter's jpurney to the Antipodes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>ROOKS KILL CAT.</head>
        <p>ROOKS KILL CAT.</p>
        <p>-- A large tabby cat which caught a rook (one of a flock of 12) in Lammas Park, Ealing, was attacked by the other eleven and was killed after a short and sharp fight. The event was witnessed by several people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 31, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>PILGRIMAGES.</head>
        <p>PILGRIMAGES.</p>
        <p>AN HOUR IN ELY AND A GLIMPSE OF THE OLD HOME</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>OF OLIVER CROMWELL.</head>
        <p>OF OLIVER CROMWELL.</p>
        <p>With summer weather to liberate the thoughts, our readers enjoy the mention of antiquities and interests which lie far afield, and which may perhaps be compassed in the course of holiday travel. Such, at least, is the verdict given; and it will furnish an excuse to mention in a brief article an inter- esting hour that fell quite accidentally in the course of a journey to Yarmouth. We had never known until the chance remark of a fellow-traveller revealed it, that the house was still standing in which Oliver Cromwell lived at Ely before the outbreak of the Civil War. With something less than fifty minutes before us we set out from the station in quest for Cromwell's home. CROMWELL A QUIET HOUSEHOLDER. It is difficult to think of Oliver Cromwell as a quiet householder. Our acquaintance with him has been made in the pages of history from whence he holds our interest in thrall, first as a military genius and next as a matchless ruler and statesman. It is equally difficult to think of him as the son of a noble house who lived the life of a popular country gentleman. Yet Cromwell until past middle life was such a one, and it is worth while to turn back to these early quiet days if only to realise</p>
        <p>still stands hard by the churchyard of St. Mary's. It is by no means a sumptuous mansion. It has two storeys, many windows, irregular chimneys and gables, and an air of quaintness and gentility still hangs over it. The storms of two !centuries have left their marks, but it remains almost as it was during the uneventful years when the future Lord Protector kept here the "noiseless tenour of his way" and watched his children at their tasks and games. Here his mother and sisters joined him and the house echoed to the music of voices, old and young, that sang Puritan hymns, talked Puritan talk, and spoke good cheer to tenants and to friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>POPULAR AS LANDLORD AND CITIZEN.</head>
        <p>POPULAR AS LANDLORD AND CITIZEN.</p>
        <p>There is abundant evidence of Cromwell's popularity as a landlord and a citizen, He took his share in local business as a country gentleman, and farmed his land with profit and discretion. His hold upon the affection of the people is reflected in the title "Lord of the Fens," which they con- ferred upon him. Long before he was known to the nation he was marked in the Fen country as the friend of the distressed and suffering. " Glete House" became the home of persecuted Puritans,', and, indeed, of</p>
        <p>SOME NEW STORIES OF ABRAHAM LINCOLN.</p>
        <p>Abraham Lincoln, the famous American President, was a man of shrewd sense and wit, and in the course of his varied career he gave materials for some good stories. Every now and again fresh stories crop up in America, and I always take a note of them, for they are invariably good. This week I will tell you some which have just got into print for the first time, and which are quite true.</p>
        <p>Lincoln was once in the midst of a big political campaign, and wanted to get thirty miles to speak at a big meeting in the next town.</p>
        <p>The livery man to whom he applied for a I horse was of the opposite political party, and I gave him the slowest horse he had, in the hope that Lincoln would not get to the meet- ing in time to speak. Mr. Lincoln got there, however, and when 1 he returned with the horse, he said, " You I keep this horse for funerals, don't you ?" "Oh, no," replied the livery-man. " Well, I I am glad of that," said Lincoln, with a drawl, "for he would never get a corpse there in time for the Resurrection." There have been few men who could show up a fallacy quicker than Lincoln. He could say more in a dozen words than some could put into a big speech. Here is a case in point. A deputation had come to see him when he was President to ask him to do. something with which he did not agree. They insisted upon taking things for granted which he regarded as false. This is bow he answered, " How many legs will a sheep have if you call the tail a leg ?" " Five." " No, " said Lincoln "you I are wrong. It would still have four legs and I a tail."</p>
        <p>General Sheridan, the great American cavalry soldier, was a little man, while, as we know, the traditional cavalryman of the world is a big swaggering fellow of six feet or more. When Lincoln first met ISheridan be patted him on the back and said, "I have changed my mind about a cavalryman; five feet four will do at a pinch." That was a delightful compliment to Phil Sheridan, but a lady who imperiously approached the President on a military matter was "turned down," as the Americans say, with cold effect. She wanted a colonel's commission for her son, saying that her grandfather fought at Lexington, that her uncle was the only man who did not run away at Bladensburg, that her father fought at New Orleans and that her husband was killed at Monterey. An ordinary man could never have resisted such an appeal. But Lincoln was not an ordinary man. "Madam," he said solemnly, "Your family has done enough for the country. It is time to give somebody else a chance."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>iROMWELL'S HOUSE AT ELY. lived as a country landowner until the outbreak of the Civil ,War.</head>
        <p>iROMWELL'S HOUSE AT ELY. lived as a country landowner until the outbreak of the Civil ,War.</p>
        <p>Here Cromwet</p>
        <p>the better how strange was the working that gave England her leader in a critical hour. To realise the early life and position of Oliver Cromwell nothing will help us more than a few quiet moments spent in the old city of Ely. Here, he spent twenty happy years, and we may try to realise him as he moved among his fellows, winning their goodwill, representing them for a short while in Parliament and earning their affection by a life of sympathy and usefulness. A PROUD DESCENT.</p>
        <p>-- - But before this effort of imagination let us take note of his family. In the matter of lineage Cromwell had as much room for pride as any of the old families of the land. His family was of good Welsh blood, having descended from Morgan Ap William, who in the 15th century had been a gentleman of the Privy Chamber to Henry VII. Oliver's grandfather had stood well in the estimation of Queen Elizabeth and had been knighted by her as Sir Hairy Cromwell in 1563. At his lordly home at Hinchinbrook, near Huntingdon, he enter- tained Elizabeth herself in 1564. This fine home was built upon his own estate and he incorporated in it a suppressed Benedictine Monastery. Generosity marked Sir Henry Cromwell. His hospitality was of the splndid kind, and his neighbours near and distant called him the "Golden Knight." Oliver Cromwell's father was Robert Cromwell, who settled in Huntingdon. His plain home and simple life stood in marked ' contrast from the splendour of Hinchinbrook. He lived in a stone house at the northern end of the town supposed to be part of the old hospital of St. John. Though a very plain building it had a fine garden and a stream that turned the Prior's Mill flowing through the courtyard. Here Oliver was born April 25th, 1599. The house is still known as Cromwell's house, and the footpath leading to the water is named " Cromwell's walk." After a time spent at the Huntingdon Grammar School he studied at Cambridge and carried away a fair amount o general knowledge. Two events contributed to place him, whilst yet a youth, in affluent circumstances. The first was the death of his father in 1617, which placed them in the possession of a moderate estate. Following this came the death of his mother's brother, Sir Thomas Steward, who left to Oliver his lands at Ely.</p>
        <p>every honest man who needed his protec- tion. A short break in his home life occurred in 1628 when he sat in Parliament. In 1640 he was again elected to Parliament, this time for Cambridge, but when the Sessions were over he returned to the country, settling to to his duties as overseer of the parish, helper of a good wife who was rearing a large family, and champion of the rights of the people as against grasping landlords who were drain- ing the Fenland and claiming it for them- selves. This country gentleman, far more at home in Fenland than on the benches at West- minster, is the one described by Sir Philip Warwick as standing up in the house "in a plain cloth suit, which seemed to have been made by an ill country tailor; his linen was plain and not very clean; and I remember a speck or two of blood upon his little band, which was not much larger than his collar. His hat was without a hat-band; his stature was of a good size ; his sword stuck close to his side ; his countenance swollen and reddish ; his voice sharp and untunable, and his eloquence full of fervour." Sir Philip adds : "It lessened much my reverence unto that Great Council for this gentleman was vlry much harkened unto." THE STAID MAN TAKES TO THE SWORD.</p>
        <p>At the age of forty-three we get another glimpse of this staid and earnest man. He has left his farming and taken the sword. The cause of Parliament appeals to him. He gives £5OO to help it, but, much more important, he gives himself. His experience at the battle of Edge Hill teaches him that Parliament must have better men. He asks for "men of a spirit," and strong Puritan hearts answer to his. He organises his own district and turns the scale in the great Civil War. What a subject for a painter lies in the scene which shows Oliver, now a Colonel of the Horse, standing hat in hand before his stout old Royalist uncle, Sir Oliver, while his men are searching the house for arms and munition! What a picture the stout Royalist's face must have made as he beheld his nephew—a real Cromwell—as the enemy of the aristocracy But Cromwell's heart was with the people, and his sympathies banished whatever love of family or boast of lineage he may have possessed. He passed from the quiet house in Ely to that career which is as well known as any that history records. Best hated on the one side, most admired upon the other, he holds a place that belongs to no other in our rough island story, and probably will do for over. A.M.</p>
        <p>disgraced children, as angels love the wing, less worms that they see far down below when they look out of God's great window— and the astounding, incredible thing is that, wanting all that, we sometimes get it." A TRUE STORY ABOUT JOHN BRIGHT.</p>
        <p>The publication of the biography of John Bright is sure to bring a crop of stories of that eminent statesman. Here is one told in the "British Weekly." Many stories were told about John Bright's caustic repartees in private life. This story is true : " I should like to come back to this world in fifty years to see what changes have happened in England," said a gentleman to Mr. Bright. "My good sir," replied Mr. Bright, "if you don't mend your ways you may be glad of any ecuse to come back."</p>
        <p>IN SLEEPLESS NIGHTS. Farewell! The story of our Love ig told, The hand,clasp severed, and the lass grown cold ; The noontide faded to a lonely gleam, The music hushed—and all the rest—a dream. Farewell! perchance that dream may haunt us yet. In sleepless nights, when we would fain forget. Then let us smile upon it and the years May hold its joy, and rob it of its tears.— "Cecil Mar" in "Woman at Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>HOW HE CA ME TO ELY.</head>
        <p>HOW HE CA ME TO ELY.</p>
        <p>It was the possession of this estate that brought Oliver to the city of Ely, and he made his home in the plain building which forms our illustration. "Glebe House"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>LITERARY GLEANINGS.</head>
        <p>LITERARY GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>THE SORT OF WOMAN A MAN LIKES.</p>
        <p>In a symposium in the June "Strand , Magazine" on "The Sort of Woman a Man Likes," W. B. Maxwcil says :—" The woman an ordinary man likes best is the one he wants in his dreams—the ineffably sweet shadow who takes colour, substance, life, while the uncontrolled circuits of his brain flash their memories, hopes and imagina- tions; who smiles, and comes nearer and nearer as he beckons and longs; who enters the haven of his arms with a sigh of rapture, and who at tbe same supreme moment fades or flames into nothingness because a servant is knocking at a bedroom door and saying that the dull, everyday round has begun again. Outside of dreams we ordinary men want a woman who is so pretty that we are always proud, and so good that we are never uneasy; a woman who wears well and look her best in two-year-old gowns; who con- siders the entrance fees of men's clubs reasonable and the price of ostrich feathers iniquitous; who employs the adjective important ' in relation to our work, our food, and our desire for unfettered holidays; who laughs at our small jokes and preserves a marble face when we are scored off by others; with whom we have the massively comfort - ing sensation that she will never now recognise the plain, staring fact that we are not brave, not wise, not kind, not clever; who goes on loving us grandly, without thought of the ugly past and without fear of the empty future, just as little girls love their broken dolls, as mothers love their</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>INDIGNANT FARMER.</head>
        <p>THE INDIGNANT FARMER.</p>
        <p>BY CAROLINE WELLS. " just as mad as I can be! " An angry farmer said; "Those early strawberries of mine Desire a folding bed! "And my potatoes have declined To ripen underground. Unless, to keep dust from their eyes, Smoked goggks I have found! "The cabbige-heads, among themselves, Indulge in secret chats, But I have overheard them, and They vow they'll have straw hats! "Snob foolishness I cannot stand; And now—just as I feared— Each single stalk of wheat demands A barber for its beard ! "The sunflowers, too, are getting proud, It almost makes me smile; They want the very finest neckwear, Of the very latest style! "But now the very limit's reached! I learn, with stifled groan Each ear of corn insists upon A private telephone! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>RIGHT!</head>
        <p>RIGHT!</p>
        <p>A British school-inspector was trying to extort from small boys the names of the islands adjacent to Great Britain. "Now think of an island," he said, " where you might ttink there are no women—what would be the name for an island without women ?" He wanted to suggest the Isle of Man, but one of the boys had got another idea. He shouted " The Silly (Scilly) Isles, sir."</p>
      </div>
