        <p>THE DEATH-BLOW TO TYPHOID FEVER. At the annual provincial meeting of the Society of Medical Officers of Health held in Liverpool on Saturday, a paper was read by Dr. C. 0, Stallybrar;s on the decline of enteric, He alluded to the remarkable decrease in the number of typhoid fever cases wi,hin the last twenty or thirty years. Amongst the reasons be placed improved sanitation and the fact that the number (1 cases treated at hospital at risen from 48 to 85 per cent. Describing what Liverpool had done with regard to rehousing, be said that for every court destroyed there was one case fewer per annum of typhoid. It was difficult to do otherwise than associate the diminution of courts and typhoid as cause and effect. Now the entelic fever had almost reached extinction he thought the time had come for placing the shell-fish industry under legal control. There could be little doubt that intestinal disorders of less sevetity than enteric were caused by contaminated shell-fish. Legal control would give the final death- blow to this once formidable disease.</p>
