        <p>sr • 14'..!;• or 44 tr 4•14,"_,.t. .4 V, . i 0 ;</p>
        <p>THE STORY OF THE SOUTH POLE.</p>
        <p>Heroes Who "Slogged on" and "Played the Game."</p>
        <p>Before the Royal Geographical Society, in the Albert Hall, London, last week, Commander Evans gave the first public lecture on the Scott Antarctic Expedition. Behind h;m as be told the story sat eleven other members of the expedition. Lord Curzon, who presided, said that Captain Scott's unselfish nature would have rebelled against the idea that the survivors should not receive their meed of honcur because he had fallen. Commander Evans added little to our knowledge of Scott's last days. There was a fresh anecdote about Oates, who with his badly frost-bitten bands and feet, appealed to Wilson for advice, " What should he do? ' "Slog on, just slog on," said Wilson. When Commander Evans came to the discovery that Amundsen had beaten them he betrayed how bitter a thing this must have been to Scott, but " he and his companions had done their best, and never from one of them came an uncharitable remark." He rounded off the story with this sailor-like conclusion—" Their watchword was ale aye "Play the game.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>TALE OF CAPTAIN SCOTT.</head>
        <p>THE TALE OF CAPTAIN SCOTT.</p>
        <p>For sixteen thousand miles They sailed till at last they came, From their green and gladsome northern isles, To the place where the mountains flame. Over a waste of snow, At the bottom of the world, Where never a flower is seen to blow Or a soft green leaf uncurled. And the good ship leaves them there, And the red sun fades and dies, As they dream of those northern islands fair And the light of their English skies. And, cheered by the lamplight's gleam, They talk of their distant goal, And, amid the eternal darkness dream Of the flag they shall plant at the Pole. And, at last the sun returns, . And over the waste of snow, As its cheering light in the chill sky burns, On their weary march they go. And their comrades leave them now, And the destined five march on, And their patient path through the drifts they plough Till, at last, the Pole is won. But, with sinking hearts, they see That another standard flies Where the great round globe spins silently Under the silent skies. The flags are flying still There, in the waste of snow ; And the thought of them makes our pulses thrill As we watch the heroes go, With their spoils on their heavy sleigh, And the winter in their veins, Along the white, unending way, And over the icy plains. But the strongest strangely failed, And the frcst bit like a knife, As over the weary wastes they trailed Towards home, and friends, and life. They were men of the British breed, And they stood by his side to the last, Till their comrade's suffering soul was freed, And into the darkness passed. And still the dauntless four Through the driving sleet crawled on With limbs that were heavy and feet that were sore, For their food was almost gone. They were nearing relief at last, . And one•day's fire remained, And two day's food, when the envious blast To the tent their tired feet chained. And one of them, feeble and frail, And unwilling to burden them more, Heard the call of Christ in the roaring gale, And remembered the Cross He bore; And out in the blinding snow He went tç his death, and he lies There, where the howling breezes blow, With the light of love in his eyes. He died for the sake of the rest, And, as long as the round world moves, She shall clasp his heart to her heaving breast With the shining souls she loves. They were not twelve miles from the camp, But their food and their strength were gone; The oil had oozed from the guttering lamp, And the endless night drew on; But with frozen fingers brave, For their hearts were undaunted still, Their dying word to the world they gave, "And "they laid them "down with a Let the blizzard howl its best, Let the blinding tempests blow, It shall not disturb their splendid rest Under the shining snow. But, although their bodies lie In the shadowy land afar, Their names shall brighten our northern sky, And their deeds are like a star. And the flag they planted there, As long as the world shall roll, The names of the dauntless band shall bear Who died at the Southern Pole. ARTHUR BENNETT.</p>
        <p>LONDON AND NORTH-WESTERN RAILWAY. NEW GENERAL MANAGER APPOINTED.</p>
        <p>In view of Sir Frank Roe's retirement during the course of next year from his position as general manager, the bpaid of the London and North-Western Railway, having previously offered the position to Sir Robert Turnbull, have entered into an arrangement with Mr. Guy Calthrop, formerly in their service and. at present general manager of the Buenos Ayres and Pacific Railway, whereby through the courtesy of the chairman and directors of that board, he will succeed at that time to the vacancy so created. It is also sated that the advantages of Sir Frank P,ee's great experience and abilities are not going to be lost to the company.</p>
