      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS,</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS,</p>
        <p>Sunshine Not Necessary for German Brides; Rain Brings Blessings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>TULLE, RIBBONS AND GLOVES.</head>
        <p>TULLE, RIBBONS AND GLOVES.</p>
        <p>The Royal weddirg in Berlin was not favoured with fine weather, but the Germans do not accept the popular view in this country which demands sunshine as the augury of a bride's happiness. "Rain brings blessings," says the old-time German marriage provab, and " Much rain much luck" was the frequent com- infnt of the populace in Berlin as the day passed off, marked by threatening skies and intermittent showers. The wedding presents are valiv,d at half a million sterling, and the bride's trousseau, which is dis- tinguished for its lack of over-elaboration, cost ten thousand pounds. By the Kaiserin's exprt as wish,the greater part of the lingerie was made by poor gentle- women. The Princess has a penchant for lace, conse- quently it was employed lather than embroidery, on the greater number of the garments. The Piincess's favourite colour is pale blue, and many of the evening and tea gowns are in this shade. The newly-married couple are to v;sit this Country before taking possession of their new home in Brunswick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>A. MASTER MOSAIC WORKER</head>
        <p>A. MASTER MOSAIC WORKER</p>
        <p>Everyone who has visited the roman Catholic Cathedral at Westminister must have admired the wonderful mosaic work with which the entire interior will in time be covered. It is interesting to note that some of the m, st important figures in the echeme of decoration have been entrusted to a lady artist, Mies Gertinde Martin, who claims to be the oily "master " mosaic worker in this country. Miss Martin studied in the art echools here and in Paris, and then decided to give up painting in favour of mosaic work. She has just finiahed thl panel of the Virgin and Child, in the Lady Chapel, which is a work of rare beauty, and is now enrged on the figures of the four greater prophet.. The work is exacting, and the tiniest pieces of glass have to be cut to a nicety, and the fact that the drcoration of the Cathedral has to be dt)ne by artificial light makes it E till more trying to the eye- sight. Miss Martin works on a very high platform, surrounded by canvas, and this is the first commission she has obtained as a master.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>L kDY CHURCHWARDENS.</head>
        <p>L kDY CHURCHWARDENS.</p>
        <p>Women are frequently reproached with neglecting spheres of labour open to them, and yet striving to enter the political arena, which is so jealously closed. Such a charge cannot be made against INliss C. D. Ellis, of Bedford Park, who fgr three years has presided with marked ability over the Guardians of the Brentford Union. For twenty years this lady has worked as G n3rdian, and is said to know more about the details of every department than any male member. There are several lady Guardians on the Brentford Board, of which Mis 3 Ellis is chairman. There are also many women churchwardens in vat ions parts of the country. Sussex has no fewer than seventeen, some of whom are ladies of title. It is gratifying to the sex to hear that in each instance the duties of the office are most satisf act rily carried out, as is also the case with the Hon. Elaihe Jenkins, who has recently been again elected to a seat on the Board of the Swansea and Mumbles RAilway Co. It is quite an unusual position for a woman to occupy, and she is the only member of her sex in the Blitish Isles to fill this important office. Like the women churchwardens, she is scrupulously exact in her performance of the duties appertaining to the position, and regularly attends the meetings of the Board, and takes her part in the business proceedings. THE VOGUE FOR TULLE.</p>
        <p>Never in the history of tulle or ribbon has either been so übiquitous in the fashionable world as this year. Nothing could be lighter or more summery than the transparent little hats that have just made their appearance. Some are wholly black, others all white, and still others a combination of both. Flowers or wheat ears are the usual trimmings. The smartest small hats show a tendency to severity in outline, and tulle on the brim has a softening and refining effmt. There are bats that consist ef a crown only, of the finest straw, the brim being replaced with three or four hand-made double tuffies, or rushes of black tulle, thit fall around the face, after the manner of an old-fas'lioned mob cap. A sweeping plume, a little to the back, is tho only trimming. Other hats have brims of the finest straw and tulle crowns that show the hair. Black is more frequently seen in tulle, than colours, although such bright shades are in the van of fashion. Even the sailor hat is black and trimmed as a rule with black tulle. The flowers that relieve it are, however, of the brightest possible tints. THE POPULARITY OF RIBBONS.</p>
        <p>Ribbons were never uv,re universally used than at esent. Black, or very dark blue ribbons cv-i-rth the brightest silk flowers embroidered on them in the loudest shades are a favourite trimming for the severe black straw sailor hat, to be worn with plain tailor-mades. There is a</p>
        <p>Continued in ?text column.</p>
        <p>_ - •• - - _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 31, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 31, 1913.</p>
        <p>COMPLETE ' EXAMINER ' STORY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>WHITE TOAD.</head>
        <p>THE WHITE TOAD.</p>
        <p>FROM THE FRENCH OF EUGENE MONTON. BY H. M. ASHTON.</p>
        <p>It happened on the 25th of June, 1836, and it is talked alymt still. It is the most unheard of event of which the records of Rode z have preserved the remembrance. Alphonse Bondet was an honest and skil- ful tailor of Touat Street. Nature, in bringing him into the world, had evidently made a mistake, for Alphonse Bondet, destined to all eternity to live in the body of a tailor, was born with the soul of a gymnast. It would be impossible to say why the head of the little tailor was filled with athletic visions. He never passed a gymnasium without becoming an unwearying spectator of the exercises, and he was filled with pi ide when one of those " strong men" as he respectfully called them deigned to drink a bottle of wine with him. Brindet lived in Touat Street, opposite the lane which leads to the Chapter House, a lodging of two rooms on the first fl or, each having a door on the staircase, and communicating between them by a glass door hung with a curtain. The first room, looking on the court, served as a kitchen and room for the housekeeper; in the second, -overlooking the Etreet, the master had his bed and his working bench. _</p>
        <p>All appeared to be well with the best of possible worlds in Toast-street at Rodez on June 25th, 1836, at thirty-seven minutes past eight in the evening, when a horrible cry rang out from the interior of Bondet's house, and a woman, dishevelled and wild with terror, tumbled from the top to the bottom of the staircase, rushed madly across ths street, and fell with all her weight on to M. Cu; a retired gendarme, who was sitting before his door, causing him to fall on his back. This woman was Mariette, Bondet's servant. As the gendarme struggled to his feet, the woman fell into a most violent fit of hysterics. Whilst charitable friends gathered round her, a small crowd was formed, which soon increased to two or three hundred people who, jostling and pushing and standing on tiptoe, craned their necks and asked one another what it was all about, Bondet's house was reached from three lides, from the Chapter Efous-., from the Prefecture and from the city, and in front from right and left. Now it happened that in the crowd on the left, those in the front rank, seeing that Bondet's servant had fallen on Cux and knocked him over, believed that the servant had assassinated the gendarme and said so to those behind them. The crowd on the right having seen the gendarme knocked over close to where the woman was stretched on t! e ground, thought he had just murdered her ; and the crowd in front thought that both had been murdered. _</p>
        <p>Each of these three reports spread in a different direction, the first towards the workmen's quarter, the second towards the city, the third towards th! cathedral. Five minutes later, the three reports had tun the whole town, causing great confusion and vastly exciting the popular mind. no Chief Constable, informed that the gendarme Cux, had just been murdered by a woman named Mariette, and the Marshal of the Gendarmerie informed that a woman named Marietta bad just been murdered by the gendarme Cux, huriied to the scene of the crime, while the Kirg's Marshal came from his quarters and directed that the bodies of the murdered persons should be rliscd. Meanwhile the gendarme Cux had risen, and they had succeeded in placing Mariette in a chair; and thanks to a large glass of water which had been thrown upon her face, she opened her eyes, but immediately uttered a fearful cry and fainted away again. Another glass of water was thrown over her, then a second, then a third. At the third, she stotd up and cried, " Oh my poor master," and fainted no more. Then amid a silence in which a hundred breasts palpitated with curiosity and terror, she spoke those fearful words: " Help, help! my poor master has just been dev•Jured by the White Toad! " A shudder of horror swept over the crowd like a storm of wind ; some endeavoured to fly, others pushed forward to se, so that after some minutes of wild struggling, the streets remained choked with the same crowd, striving in vain to escape and shouting with terror. However, a great space was cleared before the door of Bondt•t's house, and the gendarme Cu; who akne had remained calm and collected, took Marlette firmly by the arm and ajid, "Come, come what is all this about ?" " I tell you that the White Toad has devoured my poor master." "You've lost your head, my poor girl," said Cux, and he walked towards the door. But the girl clung to him crying, "Don't go, for Ood'esake don't go, you will be eaten tco." Cux was an old soldier who knew DO fear ; be threw off Mariette and in three strides was at the d tor ; he entered and vanished up the staircase four steps at a time.</p>
        <p>A death-like silence reigned amongst the crowd. Suddenly, a cry such as no human ears had ever heard, rang out from Bondet's room. It was not a shriek, or a groan or a bellow or a croak, Lut a dreadful com- bination of them all. At this cry Mariette rose like a spectre. "Did you hear that? It was the white toad." Two seconds later, a desperate clatter of feet was heard on the staircase, and the gendarme, literally green with terror, sank down b?fore the door, crying with a strangled voice, "To arms ! to arms ! " At this cry there was such a leap of terror among the crowd that those of the front rank recoiled fifty paces ; a fearful clamour rose from end to end of the street and ten thousand voices groaned at the same time, "The White Toad ! "</p>
        <p>The crowd dispersed as if by magic; all the houses were taken by assault, doors and shops were closcl, windows were opened, and the streets, so recently crowded with people were absolutely deserted. At this moment the drum of the Socisty of the Children of Mars was heard beating the call to arms, the drummer of the firemen sounded the fire alarm,and the Cathedral bellman rubbed like a madman to the clock tower and sounded the alarm, the other churches following buit. Hearing the bells, the inhabitants of the villages in the valley began to march towards Rodez, while the gendarmerie, the firemen and the garrison troops assembled in haste and hurried to the ecece of the tragt dy.</p>
        <p>In comp'ete ignorance as t 3 the species of beast which had just devoured the unfortunate tailor, a very sensible plan was adopted; the trc.ops advanced, from all sides, and formed around the door of the hcuso a wall of bayonets, and olden; were given that when the</p>
        <p>general tendency to decorate dresses with ribbons. Many of the newest Paris models are trimmed entirely with them. , A broad band on one model was stitched all round the skirt, rather more than a foot above the hem. Another band was arranged below the hips on which were arranged loops and bows. The belt was of swathed ribbon, and this was finished with a big bow in the centre of the back. Festoons of ribbon reach the edge of many tu&amp;cs, and blouses are largely trimmed with every kind of narrow ribLn. Shoes also have bows to finish and adorn them. FASHIONABLE GLOVES.</p>
        <p>Gloves have returned again, and now fewer rings are to be worn, these will be carefully chosen to match the toilette. The ungloved Land in theatres and other places cf amusement is not a cleanly idea, for dust gathers on the skin to the detriment of the same. Besides a well-cut glvo improves a dainty hand, and gives a fiiiish to an evening or morning toilette. A cheap kid gLve is a very dear purchas-., for it splits and is badly shaped. God lisle thread is generally to be preferred. This is to be had in elbow length, decorated with I:ce medallions, for afternoc n or evening wear, and is one of the novelties of this season. The most fashionable gloves are of undressed kid, that ruck to the elbow. Silk gloves are still in fashion, but are not altogether satisfactory. Cotton gloves have improved so much of late—although they still leave much to be desired, that they m3y be recommended where economy his to be studied, since they can so easily be washed at home and are quite inexpensive. FRUIT AS TABLE DECORATION.</p>
        <p>Some hostesses are this season using fruit as table decoration, instead of flowers, a fashion that comes from Paris. Not only does the fruit appar in beauti- ful dishes, hut it is laid up )n the cloth surrounded by pretiy greenery. By this means very beautiful com- binations of colour are achieved, and as the perfume fruit exhales is less overpowering than that of flowers this arrangement is considered by many women an advantage.</p>
        <p>-br</p>
        <p>beast, which it wa3 supposed had escaped from a menagerie, should come out, a single volley was to be fired. The gendarme Cux, now recovered from his fright, was able, from his window, to give some idea of the appearance of the beast. "I could never have imagined such a thing," be said. "I went into the kitchen, where I found nothing. The glass door was shut, and as the curtain is inside, I couldn't see anything it first, but the wind raised the curtain a little, and I had time to assure myself that there was nothing in the room; then I opened the door and had scarcely got my head in when I saw in the cornsr on the right an enormous white beast of terrifying appearance. It bad four paws and an enormous head split in the middle. When it saw me it uttered a horrible cry and tried to leap upon me. Fortunately I was able to throw myself back and shut the door. Of the unfortunate tailor there was no trace, but his cLthes which were on the floor at the side of an overturned chair. It is probable that he was devoured when he was undrez,sing himself for bed."</p>
        <p>M matte now appeared at the window, and was able to give some particulars, but not without bursting from time to time into tear:,</p>
        <p>"I was in my kitchen, "said she, "washing my plates,and I was getting ready to go to bed afterwards, when I heard weary sighs in my master's room, then oaths. I didn't pay much attention. A moment after I heard the sound of a body rising and falling and the voice of my master crying for help In my confusion I overturned my lamp, and \'! bile I looked in vain for the matches the cries continued 1, uder and fonder. At last havirg succeeded in relighting my lamp, I rushed into his room. My poor master ! Nothing remained of him but his clothes, which bad fallen on the floor from the chair where he had placed them before getting into bed, and then—and then in a corner, the beast, oh, the awful beast !'' And she fainted away. "But, "said the Captain of the firemen to Cux, if Bondet has been devoured bow is it that there is no blood on the floor? "</p>
        <p>" We have a red carpet on the floor," replied Mariette, who had just recovered from her swoon, "and that would prevent the blood from showing." " Perhapi, "said the Lieutenant of the firemen," the White Toad has licked up the blood." " Lieutenant, " said the Captain, "that was a wise remark of yours." _ _</p>
        <p>It was ..-ident from these two identical accounts that Bond, t had not left his room; nobody had seen him go out. There was in his room an enormous unknown animal that two persons had seen and whose cries had been heard in the street. Therefore it came to this: Bondet had not gone out, he was no longer there, and there was in his room a White Toad—the White Toad bad eaten Bondet.</p>
        <p>There was one obscure point. How had this Toad been able to cross the town and enter the house unperceived? On this point inquiries were made from window to window and statements %vele made, vague at first, but gathering confirmation little by little, promising some enlightenment, when the Gaptain of the gendarmerie, who had been found at last, arrived on the spot. He was told in a few words the state of affairs, listened attentively to the story of Cux and Mnriette, and btood awhile in thought.. Then he said, "It is impossible. Firbtly, there are no toads such as you describe ; secondly, a ferocious beast, whatever it might b-, could not enter the house unperceived ; thi.diy, we'll g) and have a look." And, drawing his sabre, he entered the house, forbidding anyone to follow him. Some minutes passed. A door was heard to slam violently, and he appeared on the staircale look- ing very serious.</p>
        <p>Maishal, Marshal," he cued. " Here, Captain." "There is something extraordinary _up here. It certainly looks like an animal. Take your musketeers and fix bayonets. You, Murin, will remain on the staircase, fit e paces from the stairhead, bayonet advanced and fivger on trigger. You, Schmidt, post yourself behind the door; I will enter the room. Now, whatever happens, do nothing till you hear the command .` Fire.' Up to that time don't move, and if the beast comes out at once cross bayonets." And nodding to Morin and Schmidt he re-entered the house with his sword drastn. By the sound of their foot- steps they were heard to ascend, to stop on the landing, and post themselves as they bad been ordered. An awful hush fell upon the crowd. How long this anguish lasted can never be told. Suddedly a fearful scull's was heard on the staircase, and Morin rushed into the street, followed quickly by Schmidt and the Captain. They looked at each other and holding their sides burst ieto a great roar of laughter. At this, the other gendarmes began to laugh, then the firemen and the troops; and from window to window and from house to house through the whole town there was heard that day something that will never be heard again ,ten thousand people laughing immoderately without knowing why. A fourth personage now appeared on the threshold at Bondet's house. It was Bondet himself, alive and in the fl,sh.</p>
        <p>A cry of astonishment burst from the crowd. He remained a moment abashed, and then he saluted and ran up his staircase again The troops broke their ranks, doors and shops were opened, people came down into the street, and the Captain on one side and Morin and Schmidt on the other, explained to the crowd, amid shouts of laughter, what had happened. On opening the doorof the room,the Captain had indeed seen a great white heist which writhed and struggled on the carpet, but it did not appear very nimble and the Captain had time to look at it closely. It uttered hoarse and plaintive cries. The Captain then went forward and the beast having turned a somersault he had seen a human head surrounded by four limbs. He had called the gendarmes and all three had recog- nised that the White Toad was none other than Lours Bon-et, in spite of the fact that he had his thighs hung over his shoulders in such a fashion that his legs were placel on each side of his neck. They had unhooked him, put him on his feet, and he had told them what follows:</p>
        <p>"I was at the Circus yesterday and saw a clown con- tort himself into a frog. I wanted to do the same thing and in order to be more supple, I took every- thing cff except my puts and vest. I succeeded in passing my legs over my shoulders, but when I wanted to draw them back aaain, I couldn't do it. Then fear took hold of me, I tried to call for help, but fright choked me, and I could only utter the cries wbich so frightened Marlette." That is how, in this vale of tears, Providence some- times gives a tragic turn to humour in order to make poor humanity laugh a little.</p>
      </div>
