      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>PARISH CHURCH MEN'S BOWLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH MEN'S BOWLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The opening main in connection with the above club took place on Saturday afternoon at the Rectory, and afforded great amusement, for all the veterans who were looked upon as certain prize-winners, were, defeated by novices. Whether it was the green or the sudden change of the weather, the fact remains. Some splendid games were witnessed, and it was generally hoped the Treasurer (Mr. J. Hill) would have been found amongst the prize-winners, but, after a ha rd fought game, he was beaten by J. R. Squire. Another surprise was the defeat of the Captain (Mr. R. W. Antwis) by Mr Done. The result was as follows: 1, J. R. Squire; 2, R. Done; 3, T. Watson; 4, E. Baston.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of the Secretary (Mr. Antis) the Rev. Canon Willis distributed the prizes. After some encouraging remarks be congratulated the winners and sympathised with the losers. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded him on the proposition of Mr. Walter Dennett, seconded by Mr. S. Tilling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>NOTES AND COMMENTS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND COMMENTS.</p>
        <p>Joe Gleave's Notable Victory.</p>
        <p>Glorious weather prevailed on Saturday, and local trundlers were highly satisfied with the result of the day's play. All will congratulate Joe Gleave, who was successful in winning the Whitsuntide handicap promoted by the British Crown Green Association. In the final he defeated C. W. Smith (Roebuck) by 21-8 which was voted by all to be a magnificent victory.</p>
        <p>In the Lancashire County kmateur Association's club championship competition the only War- rington team which failed to gain entry in the fourth round was Wbitecross who were defeated by 4 by C.W.S. Irlam at Kersall Moor. The Cemetery team won the most decisive victory, accounting for Wigan Ltd. men at Newton by 42 points.</p>
        <p>Warrington Cemetery beat the Griffin contingent at the Angel, Stretford, by 52 points, in the second team's championship and Rylands' Recs. were allowed a walk over agaicat Swinton Liberals, who were unable to raise a team.</p>
        <p>In the fourth round of the club championship Rylands Recs. should have little difficulty in beating the Co-op men from Irlam at Peel Green. At Newton- le-Willows everyone expects a keen game between Warrington Bull's Head and Roebuck. If the locals keep up their present form, we have every confidence that they will advance their position in the com- petition. The Cemetery men should just about pull it off against Newton-le-Willows whom they meet at Roebuck, although nothing will have to be left to charce.</p>
        <p>Warrington enthusiasts have only to look at the results of last Saturday to give them confidence in their representatives. In the game between the Cemetery and Wigan, G. B.ill (jun.) and W. Barton for the Warringtonians gained the best victories. defeating J. 'F. Berry and J. Haigham 21-4 and .21-3 respectively in 21 and 14 ends.</p>
        <p>There is no disputing the fact that. taken all through, the Cemetery men had the better of matters, not one main failing to record double figures. W. Bridge was quite the equal of It. Hough, although be lost by one point in 31 ends. It was a ding-dong game from the first, but, after peeling with 10, the Wigan player had a good run with the jack to lead 18-14 at the 24th end. Bridge then got on a good length and peeled with 20 after 30 lengths had been run. Hough managed a single on the next end, which won him the game,</p>
        <p>W. J. Woods (Cemetery) ought to have won in his game with J. Bullen. for he was always in front up to the 25th end, when the game was 16-15 in his favour, Bullin then peeled, but was 17-20 in arrears at the 30th end. Two singles and a double, beautifully judged, gave him the victory, to the amazement of the Cemetery players.</p>
        <p>R. Antrobus also played a good game, defeating R. Hilton 21-20 in 31 ends. From 15 each the Cemetery man could not get the woods running at all, and at the 25th end the score was 18-15 against him Then. be got off and running both woods well, peeled with 20 at the 30th end to win on the next length by a single point.</p>
        <p>Bull's Head, who gained a capital victory over Man and Scythe, are a consistent gat of players, and at the Railway Hotel, Cad ishead, they were easily favourites. " We are afraid of no one" is their motto, so com- petitors beware !</p>
        <p>J. W. Jolley bad an easy task in beating W. Green- balgh 21-12 in 23 ends, although up to the 11th end play was fairly even. A. Gleaye had bard lines to lose 19-21 to T. Schofield, who, however, was in rare form.</p>
        <p>We were sorry to see Whitecross go down. but still a team cannot always be expected to win. Then again the Co-op nn have "nothing to write home about" in a four point win. Whitecross were leading eight points up to the last men, and it was rather unfortunate that P. Gleave should have lost to T. Cell 9-21 in 21 eads.</p>
        <p>One of the best games was between J. Horton and J. Corner, the latter only just winning by the odd chalk. Corner got well away at the beginning leading 124 at the 12th end. Horton then ran his woods well gradually gaining ground until after peeling with 18 at the 27th end, he passed his man on the next, scoring a beautiful double in fine style. Corner got the jack and landed the winning double and a single.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>BEN</head>
        <p>BEN</p>
        <p>'t effect punched SEE Wll'</p>
