      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>PUBLI</head>
        <p>PUBLI</p>
        <p>LAWN TENNIS,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Warrington teem visited Newton on Saturday, and gained a victory by eight events to four, 17 sets to nine, 131 games to 105. Warrington play Sandbach at Liscard next Saturday. The following team will represent Warrington : Miss Rose. Mr. Bainbridge (capt.). Mrs. Johnson. Mr. Byerley. Mrs. Titian. Mr. Nicholson. Miss Walsh. tr. Harding. Mrs. Davenport. Mr. Johnson. Mrs. Jenks. Mr. Newton. Reserves : Miss Gittens and Mr. Haddock. "CAUSEWAY" (2ND TEAM) v. GRAPPENHALL. Played at Grappenhall on Saturday. Scores :</p>
        <p>IXF.D DOUBLES</p>
        <p>Mr. Gandy and Miss Clarke beat Mr. and Mrs. Banks, 6-2, 6-1. Mr. Sutton and Miss Bibby lost. to Mr. Hesketh and Mrs. Pharaoh. 0-6, 4-6. Mr. and Mrs. Bibby beat Mr. H. Shakeshaft and Miss Howson, 1-6, 6-0, 6-3. Mr. Ilawkesworth and Miss G. Wilson lost to Mr. W. Shakeshaft and Miss Read, 5-6, 5-6. Mr. Cox and Miss E. White lost to Mr. D. Holt and Miss M. Thompson, 5-6, 2-6. Mr. Minards and Miss Edwards lost to Mr. Swinton and Miss J. Thompson, 3-6, 5-6.</p>
        <p>LADIES' DOUBLES. Miss Clarke and Miss Bibby lost to Mrs. Pharoah and Miss Read, 6-4, 4-6, 1-6. Mrs. Bibby and Miss G. Wilson lost to Mrs. Banks and Miss J. Thompson, 4-6, 1-6. Miss E,White and Miss Edwards lost to Miss Howson and Miss M. Thompson, 2-6, 3-6.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>GENTS' DOUBLES.</head>
        <p>GENTS' DOUBLES.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Gandy and Sutton beat Messrs. Banks and Hesketb, 6-3, 6-3. Messrs. Bibby and Hawkesmith beat H. and W. Sbakeshaft, 6-1, 6-2.</p>
        <p>Messrs. 'Cox. and Minards beat Messrs. Holt and Swinton, 6-3, 6-1. The Causeway won 111 game, Grappenhall 113 games; Causeway 2nd team 11 sets, Grappenhall 15 sets : Causeway 5 events, Grappenhall 7 events. Grappenhall won by 2 games, 4 sets, and 2 events.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>ON THE WING.</head>
        <p>ON THE WING.</p>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES HOMING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Bournemouth old birds' race was flown on Saturday. Twenty-four members sent 197 birds; liberated at 9-27 ; wind South-west; result (subject to correction) : Members Velocity Summerfield Bros. A.P Churra P Barlow and Perkins P ll4B-3 Summertleld Bros. Barlow and Perkins Barlow and Perkins Boyle and Skelland Marsh and Joyner Bracegirdle Deakin Roughsedge Longmore llO9-6 Ashton Tindall Bros. Woolley Bros llO2-1 Harrison and Burgess Forster and Son 1697-7 Wheatley Welsby and Fibber lOBO-6 Keenan Bros. Wilcox and Donoghue Wilson Johnson 1042 Allen Prince Williams and Mather Randal Is</p>
        <p>P win the penny pool A wins special les. 6d. presented by Mr. R. Scott. WEST LANCASHIRE SATURDAY FEDERATION The third old bird race from Bournemouth was flown on Saturday. 1,456 birds were liberated by Sidney Taylor at 9.27 a.m., wind S.W., 1,456 birds competing. Official result :</p>
        <p>Humphries &amp; Son, Ditchford H.S. Taylor and Wright, Cadishead H.S Humphries and Son, Latchford H.S. Taylor and Wright, Cadishead H.S 1167 Holland, Cadishead Bate, Latchford U.S 1164 Davis, Cadishead H.S 1164 Newton, Lymm F. Club Summerfield Bros., Sankey Bridges H.S 1156 Clayton, Lowton H.S. Taylor and Wright, Cadishead U.S 1154 Shaw, Warrington, W. Men's F.C. ......</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>ANGLING NOTES.</head>
        <p>ANGLING NOTES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Angling Association (N.A. Centre).</p>
        <p>The distribution of prizes for the Rambling Match took place at the Gladstone Arms, Church-street, and members expressed great satisfaction with the awards, considering that there were only twenty-seven entries, a gold medal and eight other prizes were a fine collec- tion. A general meeting was held the same evening and a fair muster put in appearance. A collection was taken for the 1913 season, money specials and, according to the amount booked up, this season will surpass all records for specials. A smoking concert was held and a very pleasant evening was spent. It has been suggested that similar gatherings might, with advantage be held about once a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-31_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>GOWY.</head>
        <p>THE GOWY.</p>
        <p>There was another crowd at the Gowy on Sunday. Trout was not to be caught in numbers with the worm The best during the day was taken by Mr. Chadwick. weight 9oz. idrms. Eels were not plentiful, but during Monday and Tuesday gcod catches were to be had. W. Sanson was responsible for a fine pike. It gave him a tough struggle. but was finally gaffed and brought to land. Its length was also 3 feet and its weight 9lbs 6ozs. Experienced hands say • that if it had been taken a month later it would have turned the scales at 13lbs. The fish is of the female specie and has just passed through the spawning season 4 that being the cause of lightness, as a pike of 3 fee should be anything from If2lbs. to 151bs.</p>
        <p>LORD MORL EY ON LITERATURE. Viscount Morley,replying to the toast of "Literature" at the annual dinner of the Royal Literary Fund, held on Tuesday night at the Savoy Hotel, London. said that in his long experience poverty was no stimulus to literature. Nobody knew who had not gone through the experience the desire to write, the stif cies. desire in the breast to be a writer. it was very divine. Then there came the intoxicating joy of „the first proof-sheets and the encouragement of the first friendly—not too friendly—reviews. Insecurity was the root of all social mischiefs. The man of letters had to suffer insecurity in the most poignant degree. Journalism now absorbed an amount of literary energy and power. The publicist was a higher arid more responsibility writer than he was in the days he could remember. Literary criticism was infinitely higher. In literature the whole thing was to be independent. In politics it was necessary to be able to put one's mind into the joint-stock with other people. Literature exorcised an influence wider than that of their commerce and mightier than that of their arms, and on Empire-days and such occasions that should not be forgotten. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
