        <p>WARRINGITON CLUB. ,1 'HENRY PAIRCLOUG H. " Ai EMORLAI" 0 065 e The first qualifying round for tliteurdol,ool, Fairclough " memorial was played on 93;0 tile 00; firstfour lowest scorers qualifying to PIO :„, 11 These were C. J. Smith, 81 gross, 10 bandic'y Vroool W. S. Dutton, 81 gross, 8 handicap, 76 neY ItoD°lo Of bury, 81 gross, 7 handicap, 77 not; and E• Jm, of 87 gross, 8 handicap, 73 not. 0:119r soote rt, follows : 11. 80 0. 4 50 TCF a.. pBS fo Captain Handley In Br. ne E r .. v a n .9. : .......: . 8 6 ....*: eBB C. 11. Ford a 6: :I St th SL ' 7 6 81 J. E. Birtlos ... ... ••• 7 16 51 F.G. Fairhurst ... ... ••• ;;' 4 83 J. M. Murray ... ... ••• `,',- , 8 53 J. W. Bibby ... ... ••• ‘`'"` 15 53 Jno. Fairhurst ... ... • 20 51 0. Lofthouse ... ... .:: 103 7 86 Dr. W. J. Pe •• igili 21 85 A. Warburt0n............ ••• •••• 'Q.'S 13 85 H. Woods... ... ... ••• -„;.• 10 s 1 J. Hepherd ... ... . " o 6 55 J. Clarke ... - 113 ' ••• "' 110 •" 22 86 J. Bouchier ••• 10 00 "' 93 H. M. Roberts ... ... ••• 02 gil 112 ' F. A. Frost ... •" 100 1° 93 H. Dolan ... ... ... ••• 116 24 ___,-,/ F. Warburton ... ••• ••• _ pEIg---5-'gfeeii, In 3d. ado. 1/7 1°1421:01 ANYSZ YIB ChemUtElfreeLlfillTt:;;;# tD . ._ 82 Leadenhall St., EX.. &amp;</p>
        <p>and his form on Saturday was no exception. He met A. Barlow, who is one of the best in the Urmston club. After leading if with a single, Barlow sent up the jack, but Langley got a gr( d. wood in and secured the little 'un. How well he played his wood! When he lost the block at the ninth end he led by 9-1. Barlow hardly haying had a look in. At the 12th end, Langley led 13-4, but here Barlow played his very best, until at the 16th end he peeled with 16 at the 212f1d end. Langley led 19-16 in two more ends, but this was his final score, Barlow winning with five singles. W. Langley lost to J. C. Barlow, the trrmston skipper, by 10-21, be being beaten on long lengthsn,' G. Shaw, jun., had matters practically all his own. way, after toe first few ends, beating A. Whiteley 21-7 in 19 ends. After some very exciting final games, the wire- workers returned to town with a 16 point victory to crow about. The Junior Conservatives did well in the consola- tion competition to beat Stanley Arms, Liverpool, by 38 points. It was only on three individual games that they lost points, the Liverpool men being by far the inferior side. Bowlers flocked to the Bowling Green Inn like bees round honey, for a splendid day's programme was got through in the first round of the Peter Peacock Challenge Cup competition promoted by the Liberal Association. One of the best matches of the afternoon was between St. Austin's A and Town Hall C, the former, although handicapped 19 points behind their opponents, winning by four points. They only lost on two individual players, but had one walk-over, J. Lea not meeting A. Gleave, who was credited with 13 points. Whitecross A lost to Whitecross B by 20 points, three of their men failing to put in an appearance. Had the A team been fully represented the game would have had a different result. -- St. Austin's E are going strong and are well handi- capped at 26. On Saturday they gained a magnificent ' 14 point victory over Whitecross L, who were handi- capped at 21. Some capital bowling was witnessed and the St. Austin's men can be justly proud of their victory. The other games bustled with interesting incident, and in each match, taking into account the handicaps, the best team won. All the members of the above association are eligible to join the Warrington District Bowling Association's competition on June 14th, 21st and 28th. Entrance fee is. -- Four matches were played to complete the first round of the " Finlayson " 'Cup competition promoted by the Warrington Works Association, The second round will commence on Saturday next, june 7th, and the games will he :- 2.0. Town Hall A v Latchford A. 2.45. St. John's Cor Howley E v Sr. John's D. 3.30. Howley B v Whitecross Nor Fairfield G. 4.15_ St. Austin's E v St. Austin's A. 5.0. Bewsey Dor Orford D v Fairfield C. 5.45. Latchford CV. St. Austin's D. 6.30. St. Austin's B v Town Hall D. 7.15. Town Hall Bor St. Austin's F v Whitecross J. Will captains kindly note times of starting. Crosfield's No. 3 gained a fine victory over Fletcher's ' No. 2 defeating the moulders by 87 points. The two players on the Fletcher's side who recorded double figures were the only moulders to win, and they were P. Rafferty and J. Cartwright, who beat' R. Harrison and I'. Lloyd. Only five of the moulders put in an appearance. The representatives of Naylor's Ltd. won by 42 points over P. Stubs Ltd., the timber men losing on only three individual games. The games in the other two matches were fairly , interesting, and to-day (Saturday) a start will be made in the second round of the competition. The annual ladies' bowling competition in connec- tion with the Conservative Club took place on Satur- day afternoon before a large number of spectators. The ladies played good bowls and the final was exceptionally interesting. The entries numbered 76, and 40 prizes were distributed by Miss Wells to the following : 1, Mrs. Straw ; 2, Mrs. Reynolds ; 3, Miss Harrison; 4, Mrs. R. Warburton ; 5, Mrs. Kenworthy ; 6, Mrs. Lawton; 7, Miss Cooper; 8, Mrs. J. Warburton ; 9, Mrs. Griffiths • 10, Miss Carlisle ; 11, Mrs. Atkinson ; 12, Miss Lawrence ; 13, Miss E. A. Millington; 14, Mrs. Owen ; 15, Mrs. Fair Barber ; 16, Mrs. Clare ; 17 Mrs. E. Jones ; 18, Mrs. Baird ; 19, Mrs. Roberts; 20, Mrs. Ryder ; 21, Miss Gittens ; 22, Miss M Hayes; 23, Miss Clarke; 24, Mrs. Furnival ; 25, Mrs. Harrison ; 26, Miss Barlow ; 27, Miss P. Herbert ; 28, Miss Perdue •, 29, Miss Wells (prize presented by Councillor Wilson) ; 30, Miss G. Watkins; 31, Mrs. Holland ; 32, Mrs. Hind; 33, Mrs. D. Clayton; 34, Mrs. F. Cooke; 35, Mrs. Jolley ; 311, Mrs. Highley ; 37, Mrs. Bellian ; 38, Miss E. Weave; 39, Mrs. G. H. Smith; 40, Miss L. Herbert. In conclusion we must refer to Joe Gleave's success ' in the British Crown Green Associations's Whitsun- tide handicap. Coming of a well-known bowling tamily, his name is familiar to all local trundlers and ' hc has earned fame in the county competitions. A popular member of the Liberal Club, he has also been associated with the Bull's Head team, for which com- bination he rendered valuable services. The final rounds in the competition were played on. the Sandon Hotel Green, Liverpol, on Saturctay,before a large "gate." In the fourth round Gleave received a bye, but in the next contest he beat P. Betton (Sandon) by 21-19. G. Shaw (Walton) had very little chance in the 6th round when Gleave beat him by 21 points to S, always being a winning man. R. D. Jones (Richmonds was Gleave'a next victim, 21-15 being. the final sO.to of the game. In the semi-finals R. C. Cragg who had bodied con- sistently well up to this point could do nothing against the Warrington representative, wh, beat him 21-4. Q. W. Smith being the victor over H. Ashton by 21-19 Smith, the Roebuck man, only scored eight in the final game. Gleave was in great form and thoroughly deserved the first prize, which was a handsome canteen of cutlery. The other prize-winners were :- `). C. W. Smith, M'eh'ster. 5. J. Hughes, Sandon. 3. H. Ashton, St. Helens. 6. J. M`Dunald, L.Police. 4. R, Cragg, Southport. 7. R. D. Jones, R'hmond. 8. T. Peaccck, New Ferry. Gleave, although only a young player, has a gocd record with the woods. In 1907 he was in the last oight for the Lancashire Individual merit com- petition, and in 1903 he was in the last quartette. Last year he was the runner-up in the same competition, and he was also a runner-up in the Warrington section of the Lancashire competition. By virtue of his position in the Lancashire individual merit competi- tion last year he took part in the British Crown Green individual contest and was just out of the prizes in 1912. In addition to winning such big prizes he has been successful in landing numerous smaller trophies bearing testimony to his prowess on the green. • ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. WARRINGTON AND DI 3TRICT LEAGUE. Mr. R. G. Brandrith presided at an encouraging meeting in connection with the above League which was held on Monday evening. After outlining the past work of the League, the Chairman opened out a dis- cussion for the re-formation of the League for the season 1913-11, and it was agreed by the representatives of 16 clubs that the League be formed in two divisions. -Ist, open age ; 2nd limit age, under 20 ; and that two sets of medals be given to each division and the winners of the first division to be become holders of the Dodds Challenge Shield for the ensuing year. Suggestions from several of the delegates were fully discussed, and a committee of nine were appointed to revise the rules and report to the next meeting. Mr. It. G. Brandrith, Orford, was appointed secretary.</p>
