        <p>SUFFRAGE PILGRIMAGE. A July March through Warrington to London. "A REAL SIEGE." A correspondent writes :—The National Union of Woman Suffrage Societies held its provincial Council meeting at the Harnfield Hall, Exeter, last Friday. Vigorous determination to carry on active work in the constituencies and stimulate by all constitutional means the suffrage agitation throughout the country was the keynote of the meeting. Relative to the present situation the honorary Parliamentary Secre- tary said that the defeat of the Dickinson Bill had only confirmed the National Union in the line of policy which it had taken at its last Council meeting. There had been no set back in the movement, which was in a better position than it had ever been before. Liberals were realising that it was necessary for them to get the matter settled, and every question as it arose, whether it was connected with the rights of free speech, alteration of the Franchise Laws, Scottish Home Rule, &amp;c., was going to make the position of de Government more difficult in relation to Woman. Suffrage. .The Irish who had offered so much oppd- sition would have to realise still more fully the embarrassment that woman suffwge was causing, and the business of the Union was to organise its work more energetically-than ever in the constituencies. Miss Margaret Robertson summed up the situation by Fier convincing words, " kluffragists are under the walls of the city, and ale engaged in the real siege, so that they must look out for every means of attack. They know that they are near victory, and that every- thing said now against the suffrage is the dying struggle of the opposition." The scheme of the " Pilgrimage " in July has aroused the enthusiasm of the whole National Union. This great march from all parts of the country to London is being arranged by the National Union of Woman Suffrage Societies. Each of the federations of the Union is being asked to organise a procession to march to London, and it is suggested that the processions should join one another on the journey. The main routes taken will be along the Great North-road, Watling-street (and Warrington is on this road), the Bath-road and the' Portsmouth-toad. Propaganda work will btrarried out "en route, "and the demonstration will conclude with a great Hyde, Park meeting on Saturday, July 26th. Mrs. Fawcett, the President, expresses the hope that as many as possible will attend the afternoon seivice, which' will be held at St. Paul's Cathedral on the following day. PRIMITIVE METHODIST PRESIDENT.' REV. THOMAS JACKSON'S VISIT TO WARRINGTON. [By the Rev. W. Cooper.] The announcement that the Rev. Thomas Jackson is paying a visit to Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church on Thursday next, may not send the public into such hysterics as would an election or a repre- sentative fcotball match. It ought to be borne in mind, however, that one of England's best sons is , coming into our midst, '.and the following article is written in order that readers may have some idea of the worth and work of our distinguished visitor. A WORKER IN OBSCURITY. Mr. Jackson's appearance may not strike one very forcibly at first ; he never assumes the clerical garb, his worth is rather to be found in what work he can accomplish. His supreme faculty is work, work of a practical nature among the poorest of England. He is a man with a great soul, whose eye kindles into divine compassion where human suffering is involved, and who has given up everything for the welfare of his fellowmen. For over 35 years he has patiently laboured in obscurity, befriending the poorest of the poor, and ministering to the needy, cheering the depressed and strenuously fighting the battles of the weak and helpless This work could not be achieved without gaining some measure of publicity, and the Conference at Bradford honoured itself by placing him in the Presi- dential chair. This means that the highest honcur3 within the roach of Primitive Methodism were willingly and deservedly conferred upon him. It is interesting to add that the Rev. R. W. B. Whiteway, of Lymm, moved the nomination, which was subse- quently endorsed by the Conference with singular unanimity. EXPOSING THE SWEATING SYSTEM. As President of his church, Mr. Jackson has been compelled to ielinquish his regular work for twelve months and attend to business meetings and the fulfilment of numberless public functions. During his presidential tour he has utilised the opportunity of exposing the iniquitous sweating system of East London. It has been quite a surprise at some of our fashionable watering-places for people to assemble for a public meeting on the sands, and to their amaze- ment there was the President rigging up a clothes- line upon which he proceeded to suspend various articles of wearing apparel. Such a startling departure from conventional routine has only been surpassed by the telling address which followed, in which this blot upon our modern civilization has been ruthlessly exposed, and its horrors laid bare to the public gaze. Mr. Jackson is too practical a man to be satisfied with mere talk and agitation, he has also commenced to serve hundreds of cheap meals to the victims of this vicious system. WORK IN THE SLUMS. For about 35 years Mr. Jackson has been a pioneer in mission work in the slums of London, having been obliged to build his own machinery as he went along. He has by his own initiative founded various missions, whose membership will be over 1,000, and where over 2,000 scholars receive religious instruction. The Property alone is worth 5.50,000. On one occasion our fiero appeared before the Missionary Committee with the astounding news that he had purchased a block of property for several thousand pounds. He had received no sanction for such a project, and the problem of financing the scheme was beyond the reach of the Board. He received no encouragement in his undertaking—quite the opposite. At the next session, however, he announced that by a sale of a section of the property to a railway company, he had not only received enough to pay the original cost, but had sufficient ground left for the requirements of the mission. POTENTIAL CRIMINALS BECOME GOOD CITIZENS. In the thick of London's squalor, the subject of this sketch has been a burning and a shining light. He has taken pity upon` the lads in the street, and not only taught them morality and respectability, but has initiated them into useful trades and the truths of Christianity. In this way many potential criminals have become worthy citizens. For the destitute he has provided meals and night shelters. Criminals have been met as they finished their period of incarceration, and by timely and kindly treatment have been saved from lapsing into their former vices. For the sick and oppi essed, medical and legal assistance has been secured. A free doctor for the diseased and a free lawyer for those in diffi- culties has always been on Mr. Jackson's programme.. He has made the cause of the poor his own, and has devoted his best years to their needs. True, he may not have been able to introduce legislation into the House of Commons for the alleviation of so much suffering, but he has done much to ripen public opinion up.m the need for drastic social reform, and in the meantime he has done his best for the amelioration of the oppressed. A MODERN FRANCIS OF ASSISI. It may surprise the reader to know that Mr. Jackson will never have received more than the minimum salary paid to our ministers, viz., £lOO per annum. He will have collected stacks of clothing and thousands of pounds for those in need, but his thoughts have always been for the poor. One convert desired to hand over his possessions to this philanthropist, only to meet with an emphatic "No" The gift went to the Mission. Truly,this is Francis of Assisi again. In spite of all his depressing work, Mr. Jackson can be quite humorous in his utterances, and sometimes has seen hie audiences rock with laughter at some of his witticisms. He tells .for example how someone approached him in White- chapel with: the news, "Mr. Jackson, the Lord has assured me that if I come to you, you will give me half a sovereign." Mr. Jackson quietly measured his man and, perceiving that he was a regular sponger, replied, "Very well, you come again in an hour, and if the Lord directs me to do so, you shall have one." Needless to say he failed to appear. It is to be hoped that all wellwishers of social reform will avail themselves of this opportunity of hearing an expert upost the needs of the day. W. COOPER.</p>
